<div class="warning">
This document is <strong>not yet an official WinterTC draft</strong>. It is an individual proposal intended to be submitted to ECMA TC55 (WinterTC) for consideration. It has not been adopted, endorsed, or reviewed by the committee. The content may change substantially before or during that process.
</div>

<h2 class="no-num" id="intro">Introduction</h2>

The [Fetch Standard](https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/) defined {{Request}}, {{Response}}, {{Headers}}, and `fetch()` for browser HTTP clients. Server-side runtimes adopted these types but diverged on everything around them.

This specification defines a server-side API inspired by fetch that:

*   Defines {{ServerRequest}}, {{ServerResponse}}, and {{ServerHeaders}} for the HTTP messages a server receives and sends. They follow the shape of the Fetch Standard's {{Request}}, {{Response}}, and {{Headers}} where the behavior is the same, leave out client-only state and browser restrictions, and expose bodies as iterable streams [[ITER-STREAMS]].
*   Introduces a {{ServerContext}} for connection metadata, server capabilities, and lifecycle — the things that belong to the processing environment, not the HTTP message.
*   Unifies HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3 behind one programming model.
*   Handles both request/response exchanges and tunnel protocols (extended CONNECT).
*   Defines primitives for the Capsule Protocol and HTTP Datagrams.

<h3 class="no-num" id="goals">Goals</h3>

1.  **One server-side programming model for all HTTP versions**: A handler should not need to know whether a request arrived over HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, or HTTP/3. Protocol-version-specific behavior is the implementation's concern, not the application's.

2.  **Message types built for servers**: The request a handler receives is a {{ServerRequest}}, and the response it returns is a {{ServerResponse}}; both carry their header fields in {{ServerHeaders}}. They have no client-only state, such as `mode`, `credentials`, `cache`, or `redirect`, and none of the browser restrictions on header fields. Where the Fetch Standard's {{Request}}, {{Response}}, or {{Headers}} has a member with the same meaning, these types keep its name and behavior.

3.  **Clean separation of message and environment**: The HTTP message ({{ServerRequest}}) is separate from the server processing environment ({{ServerContext}}). Connection metadata, lifecycle management, and server capabilities are properties of the context, not the request.

4.  **Incremental adoption**: A handler that reads only {{ServerContext/request}} and returns a {{ServerResponse}} needs nothing else from the context. Server-specific capabilities are available when needed but never required.

5.  **Portability across runtimes**: The same handler code should run on any conforming runtime without per-runtime adapters.

6.  **Extensibility for future protocols**: Extended CONNECT is designed to carry new protocols. The handler model accommodates new `:protocol` values without API changes and allows for entirely new handlers to be defined.

<h3 class="no-num" id="non-goals">Non-Goals</h3>

1.  **Routing**: This specification does not define a router, URL pattern matching, or request dispatch. Routing is an application or framework concern.

2.  **Middleware**: This specification does not define a middleware pipeline, plugin system, or request/response transformation chain.

3.  **Response helpers**: Beyond the {{ServerResponse}} constructor and the static methods it shares with the Fetch Standard's {{Response}} ({{ServerResponse/json()}}, {{ServerResponse/redirect()}}, {{ServerResponse/error()}}), this specification does not define convenience methods for building responses (no `ctx.json()`, `ctx.html()`, etc.).

4.  **Replacing existing APIs**: This specification does not replace or deprecate existing runtime-specific server APIs. Those APIs continue to work alongside it.

5.  **Browser implementation**: This specification targets server-side runtimes.

<h3 class="no-num" id="related-specs">Relationship to Existing Specifications</h3>

*   [Fetch Standard](https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/): {{ServerRequest}}, {{ServerResponse}}, and {{ServerHeaders}} are modeled on the Fetch Standard's {{Request}}, {{Response}}, and {{Headers}}, and this specification uses the Fetch Standard's concepts and algorithms &mdash; such as header lists, status codes, and the encodings of non-streaming bodies &mdash; where the behavior is the same. It does not depend on the {{Request}} or {{Headers}} interfaces themselves, and depends on {{Response}} only to convert one into a {{ServerResponse}} ({{ServerResponse/from()}}). Two of its features have client-side counterparts that are proposed for the Fetch Standard but are not yet part of it: trailer fields, through the `trailers` members of {{Request}}, {{Response}}, `RequestInit`, and `ResponseInit` [[FETCH-TRAILERS]] [[FETCH-SEND-TRAILERS]], which {{ServerRequest/trailers|ServerRequest.trailers}}, {{ServerResponse/trailers|ServerResponse.trailers}}, and {{ServerResponseInit/trailers|ServerResponseInit.trailers}} follow; and informational responses, through an `onInformation` callback for `fetch()` [[FETCH-ONINFORMATION]], which receives what {{ServerContext/sendInformational()}} sends.
*   [Streams Standard](https://streams.spec.whatwg.org/): a {{ReadableStream}} is accepted as the body of a {{ServerResponse}}, the messages of a {{ServerWebSocketStream}} are read from a {{ReadableStream}} and written to a {{WritableStream}}, and the [[WEBTRANSPORT]] stream types used by {{WebTransportSession}} are built on this standard.
*   [Iterable Streams API](https://iter-streams.proposal.wintertc.org/) [[ITER-STREAMS]]: the bodies of a {{ServerRequest}} and a {{ServerResponse}} are async iterables of batched {{Uint8Array}} chunks, following that specification's stream model. A {{Tunnel}} has the shape of its {{DuplexChannel}}: tunnel data and datagrams are read as {{ByteReadableStream}}s and written through {{Writer}}s.
*   [WebSockets Standard](https://websockets.spec.whatwg.org/) [[WEBSOCKETS]]: {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}} returns the {{WebSocket}} interface it defines. {{ServerWebSocketStream}} is modeled on the `WebSocketStream` interface proposed for that standard [[WEBSOCKETSTREAM]], which is not yet part of it ([[#idl-serverwebsocketstream]]).
*   [WebTransport](https://w3c.github.io/webtransport/) [[WEBTRANSPORT]]: {{WebTransportSession}} strictly follows the W3C WebTransport API. Its member types &mdash; {{WebTransportDatagramDuplexStream}}, {{WebTransportBidirectionalStream}}, {{WebTransportSendStream}}, {{WebTransportReceiveStream}}, {{WebTransportCloseInfo}}, {{WebTransportReliabilityMode}} &mdash; are defined by that specification and are not duplicated here.
*   [RFC 9110](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110): HTTP semantics, including trailer fields and informational responses.
*   [RFC 9218](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9218): Extensible Prioritization Scheme for HTTP.
*   [RFC 9297](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9297): HTTP Datagrams and the Capsule Protocol.
*   [RFC 9221](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9221): Unreliable Datagram Extension to QUIC.
*   [RFC 6455](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455): The WebSocket Protocol, including its closing handshake and close codes.
*   [RFC 8441](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8441) / [RFC 9220](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9220): WebSocket bootstrapping via extended CONNECT over HTTP/2 and HTTP/3.
*   [RFC 9298](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9298): Proxying UDP in HTTP.
*   [RFC 9484](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9484): Proxying IP in HTTP.
*   [RFC 9651](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9651): Structured Field Values for HTTP.
*   [RFC 7838](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7838): HTTP Alternative Services, for advertisement of HTTP/3 bindings.

<h3 class="no-num" id="design-rationale">Design Rationale</h3>

<h4 class="no-num" id="why-context">Why a context object?</h4>

The properties needed on the server side fall into distinct categories:

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Category</th><th>Examples</th><th>Belongs to...</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>The HTTP message</td><td>method, url, headers, body</td><td>The {{ServerRequest}}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Connection metadata</td><td>remote address, ALPN protocol</td><td>The connection</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Server capabilities</td><td>informational responses</td><td>The response pipeline</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Execution lifecycle</td><td>waitUntil</td><td>The runtime environment</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

None of these are properties of the HTTP message itself. Putting them on the {{ServerRequest}} conflates the message with its processing environment. A context object keeps them separate.

Existing server-side runtimes and frameworks have independently arrived at similar patterns: some pass the {{Request}} along with additional positional arguments carrying lifecycle operations (such as `waitUntil()`) or connection metadata (such as the remote address); others pass a single context object that wraps the request.

A single context object avoids positional fragility and extends without signature changes.

The indirection also permits lazy materialization. Because the {{ServerRequest}} is reached through an accessor (`ctx.request`), an implementation can defer constructing it &mdash; parsing the URL, materializing the {{ServerHeaders}}, allocating the {{AbortSignal}} &mdash; until the handler first accesses it. A handler that denies a request under load never pays for a {{ServerRequest}} it never reads. A signature that passes the {{ServerRequest}} as an argument forecloses this optimization.

