Scope {#scope}
==============

This proposal defines the [=Fetch Server API=], a server-side HTTP API modeled on the Fetch Standard. It specifies:

*   A {{ServerContext}} interface providing connection metadata, server capabilities, and lifecycle management for incoming HTTP requests.
*   A {{ServerRequest}} interface representing the incoming HTTP request message, whose body is consumed as an iterable stream [[ITER-STREAMS]].
*   A {{ServerResponse}} interface representing the HTTP response message a handler returns, including its trailer fields.
*   A {{ServerHeaders}} interface for the header fields and trailer fields of those messages, without the browser restrictions of the Fetch Standard's {{Headers}}.
*   A {{ConnectContext}} interface extending {{ServerContext}} for CONNECT and extended CONNECT tunnel protocols.
*   An [=exchange=] state model governing response commitment, and an [=error code=] registry mapping protocol-version-independent codes to HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 error signals.
*   A {{Tunnel}} interface for bidirectional communication over established HTTP tunnels, read and written as iterable streams [[ITER-STREAMS]], including the Capsule Protocol and HTTP Datagrams.
*   A {{ServerWebSocketStream}} interface for WebSocket connections whose messages are read and written as streams, with backpressure.
*   A {{WebTransportSession}} interface for multiplexed streams and datagrams over HTTP connections.
*   A handler object model with `fetch()` and `connect()` methods for processing requests and tunnels.
*   An optional infrastructure layer providing `serve()`, {{Server}}, {{Listener}}, and lifecycle management.

Conformance {#conformance}
==========================

This specification has two layers:

**Core** (handler model): The {{ServerContext}}, {{ServerRequest}}, {{ServerResponse}}, {{ServerHeaders}}, {{ConnectContext}}, handler object pattern, and the handler callback signatures. A conforming implementation MUST support this layer.

**Infrastructure** (server lifecycle): The `serve()` function, {{Server}}, {{Listener}}, {{Closeable}}, {{ListenOptions}}, {{ServerOptions}}, and {{TLSOptions}}. An implementation MAY support this layer. An implementation that manages server lifecycle externally (e.g., an edge runtime where binding, TLS, and connection management are handled by the platform outside the application) is not required to expose `serve()`, {{Server}}, or {{Listener}}. Such an implementation is conforming as long as it implements the core layer.

<div class="note">
The two layers correspond to two deployment models. Where the platform owns the server &mdash; the application supplies only a handler object, and the platform handles everything else &mdash; an implementation would provide the core layer alone. Where applications create and manage their own servers, an implementation would typically provide both layers.
</div>

A conforming implementation MUST also conform to [[!ECMASCRIPT]] and [[!WEBIDL]].

The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [[!RFC2119]] [[!RFC8174]] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.

Support for the following features is OPTIONAL at both layers:

*   Priority
*   Completion signaling
*   Informational responses
*   Request trailers
*   Response trailers
*   CONNECT, extended CONNECT, and tunnel protocols
*   HTTP/1.1 upgrade normalization ([[#connectcontext-upgrade]])
*   WebSocket ({{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}} and {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocketStream()}})
*   WebTransport
*   HTTP Datagrams
*   HTTP/3 and QUIC
*   In the infrastructure layer: SNI-based certificate selection, and the binding capabilities {{ListenOptions/reusePort}} and {{ListenOptions/ipv6Only}}

An implementation that does not support an optional feature MUST still expose the relevant interface members. A method whose effect the implementation cannot provide throws a `"NotSupportedError"` {{DOMException}}; promise-returning methods reject with the same error. Priority, completion signaling, and trailers are the exceptions: they degrade silently. (Priority and completion signaling are advisory by design.) For priority, {{ServerContext/clientPriority}} returns the defaults, assignments to {{ServerContext/serverPriority}} are accepted and ignored, and an {{ServerContext/onpriority}} callback is never invoked. For completion signaling, {{ServerContext/completed}} returns `null` ([[#servercontext-lifecycle]]). For request trailers, any received trailer fields are discarded, and {{ServerRequest/trailers}} is fulfilled with an empty {{ServerHeaders}} object once the body has been read to its end. For response trailers, no trailer fields are sent: the stream ends once the body has ended. An implementation that does not support HTTP/1.1 upgrade normalization routes every request that carries `Upgrade` to `fetch()` as an ordinary request, as [RFC 9110 Section 7.8](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-7.8) permits. The {{ServerResponse}} itself, including its {{ServerResponse/trailers}} attribute, behaves the same either way ([[#serverresponse-sending]]).

{{ServerContext/deny()}} and {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} are not optional. Every implementation can decline a request; the wire-level fidelity of [=error codes=] varies by protocol, and where a signal is unavailable the implementation uses the closest supported behavior (e.g., the HTTP/1.1 fallback in [[#servercontext-denial]]). Applications rely on {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} for cleanup and logging actions that must complete, so every implementation MUST honor it (subject to the time limit in [[#servercontext-lifecycle]]).

A conforming implementation MAY provide non-standard extensions: interfaces, attributes, methods, and dictionary members beyond those this specification defines. These include additional members on the objects it defines (such as {{ServerContext}}, {{ServerRequest}}, {{ServerResponse}}, and {{Server}}), additional options in its dictionaries (such as {{ServerOptions}} and {{ServerResponseInit}}), and additional handler methods on the handler object, for example for events other than HTTP exchanges. An extension MUST NOT contradict, or cause the non-conformance of, any behavior this specification defines. Within that limit, the presence of an extension does not make an implementation non-conforming, and this specification's definitions are not to be read as forbidding members or options that they do not mention.

Normative references {#normative-references}
=============================================

The following documents are referred to in the text in such a way that some or all of their content constitutes requirements of this document.

In addition to the documents cited inline, this specification depends on the following IETF documents: HTTP Semantics [[!RFC9110]], HTTP/1.1 [[!RFC9112]], HTTP/2 [[!RFC9113]], HTTP/3 [[!RFC9114]], URI Generic Syntax [[!RFC3986]], the Extensible Prioritization Scheme for HTTP [[!RFC9218]], HTTP Datagrams and the Capsule Protocol [[!RFC9297]], the Unreliable Datagram Extension to QUIC [[!RFC9221]], Proxying UDP in HTTP [[!RFC9298]], Proxying IP in HTTP [[!RFC9484]], the WebSocket Protocol [[!RFC6455]], Bootstrapping WebSockets with HTTP/2 [[!RFC8441]] and HTTP/3 [[!RFC9220]], HTTP Alternative Services [[!RFC7838]], and Service Identity in TLS [[!RFC9525]]. It refers informatively to Structured Field Values for HTTP [[RFC9651]] and the 103 (Early Hints) status code [[RFC8297]].

<div data-fill-with="references"></div>

Terms and definitions {#terminology}
====================================

For the purposes of this document, the terms and definitions given in [[ECMASCRIPT]], the Fetch Standard [[FETCH]], the DOM Standard [[DOM]], and the following apply.

<h3 class="no-toc" id="term-fetch-server-api"><dfn>Fetch Server API</dfn></h3>

the server-side HTTP API defined by this specification

<h3 class="no-toc" id="term-web-interoperable-runtime"><dfn export>web-interoperable runtime</dfn></h3>

ECMAScript-based runtime environment as defined by WinterTC

<h3 class="no-toc" id="term-server"><dfn dfn-type="dfn" id="dfn-server">server</dfn></h3>

an entity that accepts HTTP connections, receives requests, and sends responses

<h3 class="no-toc" id="term-handler"><dfn>handler</dfn></h3>

a JavaScript function provided by the application that processes incoming requests or tunnel establishment attempts

<h3 class="no-toc" id="term-exchange"><dfn export>exchange</dfn></h3>

the server-side processing of a single incoming request or tunnel establishment attempt, from receipt until the response (or the established tunnel) has concluded and all lifecycle-extension promises settle

<h3 class="no-toc" id="term-tunnel"><dfn dfn-type="dfn" id="dfn-tunnel">tunnel</dfn></h3>

a long-lived bidirectional communication channel established through an HTTP connection via the CONNECT method or extended CONNECT

<h3 class="no-toc" id="term-connect-protocol"><dfn export>connect protocol</dfn></h3>

the value of the `:protocol` pseudo-header in an extended CONNECT request, identifying the protocol being tunneled (e.g., `"websocket"`, `"webtransport"`, `"connect-udp"`, `"connect-ip"`)

<h3 class="no-toc" id="term-protocol-marker"><dfn>protocol marker</dfn></h3>

a `Symbol.for('wintertc.server.protocol')` property on a [=handler=] object with an integer version value, used by the runtime to distinguish this API from legacy invocation conventions. The key follows the [[SYMBOL-NAMING]] convention.


Web IDL definitions {#idl}
==========================

SocketAddress {#idl-socketaddress}
----------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
enum SocketAddressFamily { "IPv4", "IPv6", "unix" };

dictionary SocketAddress {
  DOMString address = "";
  unsigned short port = 0;
  SocketAddressFamily family;
  USVString path;
};
</pre>

A {{SocketAddress}} represents a network endpoint. The {{SocketAddress/family}} member indicates the address family:

*   For `"IPv4"` and `"IPv6"`, the {{SocketAddress/address}} member is the IP address as a string, in the textual form of [[!RFC5952]] for IPv6 (without brackets or a zone identifier), and the {{SocketAddress/port}} member is the port. The {{SocketAddress/path}} member is absent.
*   For `"unix"`, a Unix domain socket, the {{SocketAddress/path}} member is the socket's path (absent for an unnamed socket), {{SocketAddress/address}} is the empty string, and {{SocketAddress/port}} is `0`.

When the address is not available ([[#servercontext-connection]]), {{SocketAddress/family}} and {{SocketAddress/path}} are absent, {{SocketAddress/address}} is the empty string, and {{SocketAddress/port}} is `0`.

RequestPriority {#idl-requestpriority}
--------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary RequestPriority {
  [EnforceRange] unsigned short urgency = 3;
  boolean incremental = false;
};
</pre>

A {{RequestPriority}} represents a priority signal per [RFC 9218](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9218). The {{RequestPriority/urgency}} member is an integer in the range 0&ndash;7, where 0 is the highest priority and 7 is the lowest. The default is 3. The {{RequestPriority/incremental}} member indicates whether incremental delivery is preferred.

Callback definitions {#idl-callbacks}
-------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
callback PriorityCallback = undefined (optional RequestPriority priority = {});

callback FetchHandler = any (ServerContext ctx);

callback ConnectHandler = any (ConnectContext ctx);

callback ErrorHandler = any (ServerContext ctx, any error);
</pre>

The {{FetchHandler}} callback receives a {{ServerContext}} and returns a {{ServerResponse}}, `undefined`, or a {{Promise}} resolving to one of those. The return type is specified as `any` because Web IDL cannot express `(ServerResponse or undefined or Promise<ServerResponse or undefined>)` as a callback return type.

The {{ConnectHandler}} callback receives a {{ConnectContext}} and returns a {{ServerResponse}}, `undefined`, or a {{Promise}} resolving to one of those.

The {{ErrorHandler}} callback receives the context of a failed [=exchange=] (a {{ServerContext}} or {{ConnectContext}}) and the thrown value, and returns a {{ServerResponse}}, `undefined`, or a {{Promise}} resolving to one of those. See [[#handler-error]].

Options dictionaries {#idl-options}
-----------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary ConnectAcceptInit {
  ServerHeadersInit headers;
  ByteString protocol;
};

dictionary WebSocketUpgradeInit {
  sequence&lt;DOMString> protocol;
  boolean compression;
  [EnforceRange] unsigned long long pingInterval;
};

dictionary TLSCertificate {
  (DOMString or BufferSource) cert;
  (DOMString or BufferSource) key;
};

callback SNICallback = any (DOMString hostname);

dictionary TLSOptions : TLSCertificate {
  sequence&lt;DOMString> alpn;
  (SNICallback or record&lt;DOMString, TLSCertificate>) sni;
};

dictionary QUICOptions {
};

dictionary TimeoutOptions {
  [EnforceRange] unsigned long long handshake;
  [EnforceRange] unsigned long long idle;
  [EnforceRange] unsigned long long headers;
  [EnforceRange] unsigned long long receive;
  [EnforceRange] unsigned long long send;
  [EnforceRange] unsigned long long tunnelIdle;
  [EnforceRange] unsigned long long closeHandshake;
  [EnforceRange] unsigned long long waitUntil;
};

dictionary LimitOptions {
  [EnforceRange] unsigned long long maxHeaderSize;
  [EnforceRange] unsigned long long maxRequestBodySize;
  [EnforceRange] unsigned long long maxRequestsPerConnection;
  [EnforceRange] unsigned long maxConcurrentStreams;
  [EnforceRange] unsigned long maxConnections;
};

dictionary ServerOptions {
  [EnforceRange] unsigned short port;
  DOMString hostname;
  boolean reusePort = false;
  boolean ipv6Only = false;
  [EnforceRange] unsigned long backlog;
  TLSOptions tls;
  (boolean or QUICOptions) quic = false;
  (boolean or DOMString) altSvc;
  boolean autoContinue = true;
  boolean extendedConnect;
  boolean webTransport;
  boolean datagrams;
  TimeoutOptions timeouts;
  LimitOptions limits;
  AbortSignal signal;
};

dictionary ListenOptions {
  [EnforceRange] unsigned short port;
  DOMString hostname;
  boolean reusePort;
  boolean ipv6Only;
  [EnforceRange] unsigned long backlog;
  TLSOptions tls;
  (boolean or QUICOptions) quic;
  (boolean or DOMString) altSvc;
  boolean extendedConnect;
  boolean webTransport;
  boolean datagrams;
  TimeoutOptions timeouts;
  LimitOptions limits;
};

dictionary HandlerObject {
  FetchHandler fetch;
  ConnectHandler connect;
  ErrorHandler error;
};
</pre>

The ServerRequest protocol {#idl-serverrequest}
-----------------------------------------------

A <dfn interface>ServerRequest</dfn> is an object, supplied by the implementation, that represents the HTTP request message of an [=exchange=]: its method, URL, header fields, body, and trailer fields. The body is consumed by iterating the {{ServerRequest}} itself ([[#serverrequest-body]]).

{{ServerRequest}} is not a Web IDL interface but an object protocol, in the manner of the protocols of [[ITER-STREAMS]]: it is defined by the properties that a conforming object has and how they behave, not by a class. There is no `ServerRequest` global and no constructor, and `instanceof` checks against it are not possible. This leaves an implementation free to construct the object lazily, or to back it with whatever representation suits it ([[#why-context]]). An object is a {{ServerRequest}} if it has the following properties, as own or inherited properties, and as data properties or accessors:

<table class="data">
  <thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Value</th><th>Defined in</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>`method`</td><td>A String: the request method, a byte string as for a Web IDL `ByteString`</td><td>{{ServerRequest/method}}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`url`</td><td>A String: the request URL, a scalar value string as for a Web IDL `USVString`</td><td>{{ServerRequest/url}}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`headers`</td><td>A {{ServerHeaders}} object; the same object on every get</td><td>{{ServerRequest/headers}}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`signal`</td><td>An {{AbortSignal}}; the same object on every get</td><td>{{ServerRequest/signal}}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`trailers`</td><td>A promise for a {{ServerHeaders}} object; the same promise on every get</td><td>{{ServerRequest/trailers}}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`Symbol.asyncIterator`</td><td>A method that returns an async iterator over the body, yielding arrays of {{Uint8Array}}</td><td>[[#serverrequest-body]]</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Note: Because the body is read through `Symbol.asyncIterator`, a {{ServerRequest}} conforms to the `AsyncIterable<Uint8Array[]>` protocol of [[ITER-STREAMS]], and can be passed to that specification's consumers. No other methods are required; in particular, there is no `values()` method.

The ServerResponse interface {#idl-serverresponse}
--------------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
typedef (Blob or BufferSource or FormData or URLSearchParams or USVString) NonStreamingBodyInit;
typedef (async_sequence&lt;sequence&lt;Uint8Array>> or ReadableStream) StreamingBodyInit;
typedef (StreamingBodyInit or NonStreamingBodyInit) ServerBodyInit;

[Exposed=*]
interface ServerResponse {
  constructor(optional ServerBodyInit? body = null, optional ServerResponseInit init = {});

  [NewObject] static ServerResponse error();
  [NewObject] static ServerResponse redirect(USVString url, optional [EnforceRange] unsigned short status = 302);
  [NewObject] static ServerResponse json(any data, optional ServerResponseInit init = {});
  [NewObject] static ServerResponse from(Response response);

  readonly attribute ResponseType type;

  readonly attribute USVString url;
  readonly attribute boolean redirected;
  readonly attribute unsigned short status;
  readonly attribute boolean ok;
  readonly attribute ByteString statusText;
  [SameObject] readonly attribute object headers;
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;object> trailers;

  [NewObject] ServerResponse clone();

  async_iterable&lt;sequence&lt;Uint8Array>>;
};

dictionary ServerResponseInit {
  [EnforceRange] unsigned short status = 200;
  ByteString statusText = "";
  ServerHeadersInit headers;
  Promise&lt;ServerHeadersInit> trailers;
};
</pre>

The {{ServerResponse}} interface represents the HTTP response message a handler returns for an [=exchange=]: its status, header fields, body, and trailer fields. Its body is read by iterating the {{ServerResponse}} itself, as with a {{ServerRequest}} ([[#serverresponse-body]]).

The ServerHeaders protocol {#idl-serverheaders}
-----------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
typedef (sequence&lt;sequence&lt;ByteString>> or record&lt;ByteString, ByteString>) ServerHeadersInit;
</pre>

A <dfn interface>ServerHeaders</dfn> object, supplied by the implementation, represents the header fields or trailer fields of a {{ServerRequest}} or {{ServerResponse}} ([[#sec-serverheaders]]). It has the methods of the Fetch Standard's {{Headers}} interface, without that interface's guards.

Like {{ServerRequest}}, {{ServerHeaders}} is an object protocol rather than a Web IDL interface: there is no `ServerHeaders` global and no constructor. An object is a {{ServerHeaders}} object if it has the following methods, as own or inherited properties:

<table class="data">
  <thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>`append(name, value)`</td><td>{{ServerHeaders/append()}}; returns `undefined`</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`delete(name)`</td><td>{{ServerHeaders/delete()}}; returns `undefined`</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`get(name)`</td><td>{{ServerHeaders/get()}}; returns a String or `null`</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`getSetCookie()`</td><td>{{ServerHeaders/getSetCookie()}}; returns a new Array of Strings</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`has(name)`</td><td>{{ServerHeaders/has()}}; returns a Boolean</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`set(name, value)`</td><td>{{ServerHeaders/set()}}; returns `undefined`</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`entries()`, `keys()`, `values()`, `forEach(callback, thisArg)`, and `Symbol.iterator`</td><td>As for a Web IDL interface with a pair iterator declaration `iterable<ByteString, ByteString>` [[!WEBIDL]], over the [=value pairs to iterate over=] ([[#serverheaders-methods]]); `Symbol.iterator` is the same function as `entries`</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Each method converts its `name` and `value` arguments to byte strings as Web IDL converts a JavaScript value to a `ByteString` [[!WEBIDL]], rethrowing any exception. The methods operate on the [=ServerHeaders/header list=] and [=ServerHeaders/mutability=] that the implementation associates with the object; a method called with a `this` value that has none throws a {{TypeError}}.

A {{ServerHeadersInit}} has the same form as the Fetch Standard's `HeadersInit`: a sequence of name/value pairs, or a record of names to values. Any iterable of name/value pairs is accepted as a sequence, so a {{ServerHeaders}} object or a {{Headers}} object can be used as one.

The ServerContext interface {#idl-servercontext}
------------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=*]
interface ServerContext {
  [SameObject] readonly attribute object request;

  readonly attribute object remoteAddress;
  readonly attribute object localAddress;
  readonly attribute DOMString alpnProtocol;
  readonly attribute boolean encrypted;
  readonly attribute DOMString? serverName;

  readonly attribute object clientPriority;
  attribute object? serverPriority;
  attribute PriorityCallback? onpriority;

  readonly attribute boolean autoContinue;
  undefined sendInformational([EnforceRange] unsigned short status,
                              optional ServerHeadersInit headers);

  undefined deny(optional any error);

  readonly attribute object? completed;
  undefined waitUntil(Promise&lt;any> promise);
};
</pre>

The {{ServerContext}} interface is the server-side processing environment for an incoming HTTP request. It provides the {{ServerRequest}}, connection metadata, server capabilities, and lifecycle management.

Web IDL does not allow dictionary-typed attributes, so the attributes that carry a {{SocketAddress}} ({{ServerContext/remoteAddress}}, {{ServerContext/localAddress}}, and {{Listener/address}}) or a {{RequestPriority}} ({{ServerContext/clientPriority}} and {{ServerContext/serverPriority}}) are typed `object`. Each of these getters returns the dictionary converted to a JavaScript value [[!WEBIDL]], which is a new object on every get. The {{ServerContext/serverPriority}} setter converts a non-null value to a {{RequestPriority}}, rethrowing any exception, so `ctx.serverPriority = { urgency: 1 }` works as for a dictionary, and then throws a {{RangeError}} if the resulting {{RequestPriority/urgency}} is greater than 7 ([[#priority-server]]). Because {{ServerRequest}} and {{ServerHeaders}} are object protocols rather than Web IDL interfaces ([[#idl-serverrequest]], [[#idl-serverheaders]]), the attributes that return them, {{ServerContext/request}}, {{ServerResponse/headers}}, and {{ServerResponse/trailers}}, are typed `object` (or a promise for one); their values are a {{ServerRequest}}, a {{ServerHeaders}} object, and a promise for one. Likewise, {{ServerContext/completed}} is typed `object?` because Web IDL does not allow nullable promise types: its value is a promise or `null` ([[#servercontext-lifecycle]]).

The ConnectContext interface {#idl-connectcontext}
--------------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=*]
interface ConnectContext : ServerContext {
  readonly attribute DOMString? connectProtocol;

  Promise&lt;Tunnel> accept(optional ConnectAcceptInit init = {});

  WebSocket upgradeWebSocket(optional WebSocketUpgradeInit options = {});
  ServerWebSocketStream upgradeWebSocketStream(optional WebSocketUpgradeInit options = {});
  Promise&lt;WebTransportSession> upgradeWebTransport();
};
</pre>

The {{ConnectContext}} interface is the server-side processing environment for an incoming CONNECT or extended CONNECT request. It extends {{ServerContext}} with protocol identification and tunnel establishment capabilities.

The Tunnel interface {#idl-tunnel}
----------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=*]
interface Tunnel {
  readonly attribute ByteReadableStream readable;
  readonly attribute Writer writer;

  CapsuleStream capsules();

  DatagramStream datagrams();

  Promise&lt;undefined> close();
  undefined destroy(optional any error);
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;undefined> closed;
};
</pre>

The {{Tunnel}} interface represents an established [=tunnel=] through an HTTP connection. Its data is read and written as iterable streams [[!ITER-STREAMS]]: {{Tunnel/readable}} is a {{ByteReadableStream}}, and {{Tunnel/writer}} is a {{Writer}}. It supports `Symbol.asyncDispose`, enabling `await using` declarations; see [[#tunnel-closure]].

Note: {{Tunnel}} has the shape of the {{DuplexChannel}} interface of [[ITER-STREAMS]] &mdash; {{Tunnel/readable}}, {{Tunnel/writer}}, a promise-returning {{Tunnel/close()}}, and `Symbol.asyncDispose` &mdash; so it can be used where a {{DuplexChannel}} is expected. Unlike the channels that `Stream.duplex()` creates, closing a tunnel does not stop iteration of its {{Tunnel/readable}} ([[#tunnel-closure]]). Once {{Tunnel/capsules()}} has been called, getting {{Tunnel/readable}} or {{Tunnel/writer}} throws an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.

The CapsuleStream interface {#idl-capsulestream}
------------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=*]
interface CapsuleStream {
  [SameObject] readonly attribute object readable;
  Promise&lt;undefined> send(Capsule capsule);
};
</pre>

A {{CapsuleStream}} provides typed access to the Capsule Protocol on the CONNECT data stream. Its {{CapsuleStream/readable}} attribute is an object conforming to the `AsyncIterable<Capsule>` protocol, which yields each received {{Capsule}}; its {{CapsuleStream/send()}} method sends one {{Capsule}}. See [[#tunnel-capsules]].

Note: Capsules are typed messages rather than bytes, so {{CapsuleStream/readable}} yields {{Capsule}} objects one at a time. It is not a {{ByteReadableStream}}.

The Capsule dictionary {#idl-capsule}
-------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary Capsule {
  required [EnforceRange] unsigned long long type;
  required Uint8Array data;
};
</pre>

A {{Capsule}} represents a single typed capsule as defined in [RFC 9297](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9297).

The DatagramStream dictionary {#idl-datagramstream}
---------------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary DatagramStream {
  required ByteReadableStream readable;
  required Writer writer;
  required boolean unreliable;
  required unsigned long maxDatagramSize;
};
</pre>

A {{DatagramStream}} provides access to the HTTP Datagrams associated with a [=tunnel=]. Its {{DatagramStream/readable}} member is a {{ByteReadableStream}} in which each chunk is one received datagram payload; its {{DatagramStream/writer}} member is a {{Writer}} that sends each chunk written to it as one datagram [[!ITER-STREAMS]]. The {{DatagramStream/unreliable}} member indicates whether datagrams are sent as unreliable QUIC DATAGRAM frames (`true`) or as reliable DATAGRAM capsules (`false`). The {{DatagramStream/maxDatagramSize}} member is the largest datagram payload, in bytes, that could be sent when {{Tunnel/datagrams()}} was called.

Note: {{DatagramStream}} is a dictionary, not an interface: a plain data carrier with no behavior of its own, returned by {{Tunnel/datagrams()}} and destructured by the caller.

Note: WebTransport session datagrams do not use {{DatagramStream}}; they use the {{WebTransportDatagramDuplexStream}} interface defined by [[WEBTRANSPORT]]. See [[#webtransport-datagrams]].

The ServerWebSocketStream interface {#idl-serverwebsocketstream}
----------------------------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary WebSocketOpenInfo {
  ReadableStream readable;
  WritableStream writable;
  DOMString extensions;
  DOMString protocol;
};

dictionary WebSocketCloseInfo {
  [EnforceRange] unsigned short closeCode;
  USVString reason = "";
};

[Exposed=*]
interface ServerWebSocketStream {
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;WebSocketOpenInfo> opened;
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;WebSocketCloseInfo> closed;
  undefined close(optional WebSocketCloseInfo closeInfo = {});

  /* server-side extension; not part of WebSocketStream */
  undefined destroy(optional any error);
};
</pre>

The {{ServerWebSocketStream}} interface represents an established WebSocket connection whose messages are read and written as streams [[!STREAMS]]: each chunk of its {{WebSocketOpenInfo/readable}} stream is one message received from the peer, and each chunk written to its {{WebSocketOpenInfo/writable}} stream is sent as one message. Unlike the {{WebSocket}} returned by {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}}, it applies backpressure in both directions ([[#websocketstream-messages]]). It supports `Symbol.asyncDispose`, enabling `await using` declarations; see [[#websocketstream-closure]].

{{ServerWebSocketStream}} is the server-side counterpart of the `WebSocketStream` interface [[WEBSOCKETSTREAM]], which has been proposed for the WebSockets Standard [[WEBSOCKETS]] but is not yet part of it. Each member of {{ServerWebSocketStream}} has the semantics of the like-named member of `WebSocketStream`, adapted to the server side of the connection, and {{WebSocketOpenInfo}} and {{WebSocketCloseInfo}} have the members of the like-named dictionaries. Because `WebSocketStream` is not yet standardized, this specification defines these semantics normatively ([[#sec-serverwebsocketstream]]). The differences are the [=server-side extensions=] {{ServerWebSocketStream/destroy()}} and `Symbol.asyncDispose`; the close codes that {{ServerWebSocketStream/close()}} accepts, which are those a server can send ([[#websocketstream-close-codes]]); and the errors with which abnormal closure rejects, which follow this specification's error model ([[#errors]]). Should [[WEBSOCKETS]] adopt `WebSocketStream`, this specification defers to its definitions of {{WebSocketOpenInfo}}, {{WebSocketCloseInfo}}, and the message semantics ([[#websocketstream-messages]]).

Note: {{ServerWebSocketStream}} omits the client-only surface of `WebSocketStream`: the constructor, the `url` attribute (the URL is available as the {{ServerRequest/url}} of {{ServerContext/request}}), and the `signal` option for aborting the opening handshake.

Note: Messages are carried by a {{ReadableStream}} and a {{WritableStream}} rather than by the iterable streams of [[ITER-STREAMS]], which carry only bytes: a WebSocket message is either text or binary, and a byte stream cannot preserve that distinction. A {{ReadableStream}} is async iterable, so messages can still be read with `for await`.

The WebTransportSession interface {#idl-webtransportsession}
------------------------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=*]
interface WebTransportSession {
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;undefined> ready;
  readonly attribute WebTransportReliabilityMode reliability;

  readonly attribute WebTransportDatagramDuplexStream datagrams;

  Promise&lt;WebTransportBidirectionalStream> createBidirectionalStream(
      optional WebTransportSendStreamOptions options = {});
  /* a ReadableStream of WebTransportBidirectionalStream objects */
  readonly attribute ReadableStream incomingBidirectionalStreams;

  Promise&lt;WebTransportSendStream> createUnidirectionalStream(
      optional WebTransportSendStreamOptions options = {});
  /* a ReadableStream of WebTransportReceiveStream objects */
  readonly attribute ReadableStream incomingUnidirectionalStreams;

  undefined close(optional WebTransportCloseInfo closeInfo = {});
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;WebTransportCloseInfo> closed;
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;undefined> draining;

  /* server-side extension; not part of the WebTransport API */
  undefined destroy(optional any error);
};
</pre>

The {{WebTransportSession}} interface represents an established WebTransport session, providing multiplexed streams and datagrams over an HTTP connection. It is the server-side counterpart of the [[WEBTRANSPORT]] `WebTransport` interface, and strictly follows that specification's types: {{WebTransportDatagramDuplexStream}}, {{WebTransportBidirectionalStream}}, {{WebTransportSendStream}}, {{WebTransportReceiveStream}}, {{WebTransportSendStreamOptions}}, {{WebTransportCloseInfo}}, and {{WebTransportReliabilityMode}} are the interfaces, dictionaries, and enumerations defined by [[WEBTRANSPORT]]; this specification does not duplicate them. Each member of {{WebTransportSession}} has the semantics of the like-named member of the `WebTransport` interface, adapted to the server side of the session. The exceptions are explicitly labeled <dfn export>server-side extensions</dfn> &mdash; {{WebTransportSession/destroy()}} and `Symbol.asyncDispose` &mdash; which have no `WebTransport` counterpart and are defined by this specification ([[#webtransport-lifecycle]]). Should [[WEBTRANSPORT]] later define equivalent members, this specification defers to those definitions.

Note: {{WebTransportSession}} deliberately omits the client-only surface of the `WebTransport` interface (the constructor, connection options, certificate hashes, `getStats()`, and pooling-related members). Additional members may be adopted as the server-side profile matures.

The Closeable interface mixin {#idl-closeable}
----------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
interface mixin Closeable {
  attribute boolean busy;
  Promise&lt;undefined> close();
  undefined destroy(optional any error);
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;undefined> closed;
};
</pre>

The {{Closeable}} mixin provides a uniform lifecycle interface shared by {{Server}} and {{Listener}}. Objects that include {{Closeable}} also have a `Symbol.asyncDispose` method, which calls {{Closeable/close()}} and waits for the {{Closeable/closed}} promise ([[#closeable-dispose]]). Web IDL has no syntax for declaring `Symbol.asyncDispose`, so it is specified in prose here and for {{Tunnel}}, {{ServerWebSocketStream}}, and {{WebTransportSession}}.

The Listener interface {#idl-listener}
--------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=*]
interface Listener {
  readonly attribute object address;
};
Listener includes Closeable;
</pre>

The {{Listener}} interface represents a single network binding. The {{Listener/address}} attribute returns the {{SocketAddress}} that this listener is bound to; like the address attributes of {{ServerContext}}, it is typed `object` and returns the dictionary converted to a JavaScript value ([[#idl-servercontext]]).

The Server interface {#idl-server}
----------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=*]
interface Server {
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;undefined> ready;
  Promise&lt;Listener> listen(optional ListenOptions options = {});
  iterable&lt;Listener>;
};
Server includes Closeable;
</pre>

The {{Server}} interface manages one or more listeners and dispatches incoming requests to handlers.

The serve() function {#idl-serve}
---------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
partial interface mixin WindowOrWorkerGlobalScope {
  [NewObject] Server serve(object handler, optional ServerOptions options = {});
};
</pre>

The {{WindowOrWorkerGlobalScope/serve()}} function is exposed on the global object, as `fetch()` is. It is part of the infrastructure layer ([[#conformance]]).


Exchange states {#exchange-states}
==================================

Each [=exchange=] is in exactly one of six states. The state determines which context operations are valid, and is the foundation for the error model in [[#errors]].

: <dfn export for="exchange">open</dfn>
:: Nothing has been sent to the client. The initial state.
: <dfn export for="exchange">informational</dfn>
:: At least one informational (1xx) response has been sent; no final response has begun.
: <dfn export for="exchange">committed</dfn>
:: A final response has begun: the handler returned a {{ServerResponse}} (for one created by {{ServerResponse/error()}}, the stream is reset instead; see [[#serverresponse-sending]]), or a tunnel was accepted via {{ConnectContext/accept()}}, {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}}, {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocketStream()}}, or {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebTransport()}}.
: <dfn export for="exchange">complete</dfn>
:: The response or tunnel has concluded as the server directed: the response, including any trailer fields, was fully handed to the transport; the stream was reset for a returned {{ServerResponse/error()}} response or by an outbound error after commitment ([[#errors-outbound]]); or the [=tunnel=], WebSocket connection, or WebTransport session that was established has closed, or was terminated by the server (for example, by {{Tunnel/destroy()}}). Terminal.
: <dfn export for="exchange">denied</dfn>
:: {{ServerContext/deny()}} was called. Terminal.
: <dfn export for="exchange">aborted</dfn>
:: Before the exchange became [=exchange/complete=], the client reset the stream, the connection was lost, or a timeout expired ([[#serve-timeouts]]). For an established tunnel, WebSocket connection, or WebTransport session, this is its abnormal termination by the peer or by a timeout. Terminal. The exchange's <dfn export>abort reason</dfn> is a [=protocol error=] describing the cause ([[#errors-inbound]]).

Once an exchange is [=exchange/complete=], [=exchange/denied=], or [=exchange/aborted=], its state no longer changes. In particular, the loss of the connection after an exchange is [=exchange/complete=] does not make it [=exchange/aborted=], and does not abort {{ServerRequest/signal}}. The [=exchange=] itself (its lifetime, per [[#term-exchange]]) lasts until it has reached a terminal state and every promise passed to {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} has settled.

An exchange is <dfn export>response-capable</dfn> while it is [=exchange/open=] or [=exchange/informational=].

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Operation</th><th>[=exchange/open=]</th><th>[=exchange/informational=]</th><th>[=exchange/committed=] or [=exchange/complete=]</th><th>[=exchange/denied=]</th><th>[=exchange/aborted=]</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>{{ServerContext/sendInformational()}}</td>
      <td>Allowed &rarr; [=exchange/informational=]</td>
      <td>Allowed</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Discarded</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{ServerContext/deny()}}</td>
      <td>Allowed &rarr; [=exchange/denied=]</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Discarded</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{ServerContext/deny()}} with the [=fall-through sentinel=]</td>
      <td>Allowed (remains [=exchange/open=]; rerouted to `fetch()`)</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Discarded</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{ConnectContext/accept()}} and the upgrade methods</td>
      <td>Allowed &rarr; [=exchange/committed=]</td>
      <td>Allowed &rarr; [=exchange/committed=]</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Rejects with the [=abort reason=]; {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}} returns a closed {{WebSocket}}, and {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocketStream()}} a {{ServerWebSocketStream}} whose {{ServerWebSocketStream/opened}} and {{ServerWebSocketStream/closed}} promises are rejected with the [=abort reason=]</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

The two failure behaviors are deliberate:

*   **Deterministic misuse throws.** The [=exchange/informational=], [=exchange/committed=], and [=exchange/denied=] states are entered by the handler's own actions, and [=exchange/complete=] follows from them, so a violation is a programming error: the operation throws an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}. For the promise-returning methods, {{ConnectContext/accept()}} and {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebTransport()}}, the returned promise is rejected with that exception instead, as [[!WEBIDL]] requires of operations that return promises.
*   **Races are inert.** The [=exchange/aborted=] state can be entered by the peer at any moment; a handler cannot reliably check-then-act against it. Synchronous operations are silently discarded rather than throwing. Promise-returning establishment methods reject with the [=abort reason=]. {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}} returns a {{WebSocket}} that is already closed ([[#connectcontext-websocket]]), and {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocketStream()}} returns a {{ServerWebSocketStream}} whose {{ServerWebSocketStream/opened}} and {{ServerWebSocketStream/closed}} promises are rejected with the [=abort reason=] ([[#connectcontext-websocketstream]]).

Note: {{ServerContext/deny()}} is restricted to [=exchange/open=] because its default signal (`ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_REJECTED`) is a guarantee to the client that no processing occurred and the request may be safely retried &mdash; even for non-idempotent methods. Once anything has been sent, that guarantee would be false, and the state model makes it impossible to send the signal after that point. The state model tracks only what has been sent, however, not what the handler has done: a handler that has already performed non-idempotent side effects (for example, a database write) can still call {{ServerContext/deny()}}, and the client may then replay the request. A handler SHOULD NOT call {{ServerContext/deny()}} with the default code, or with `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY`, after it has performed side effects that a retry would repeat; `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED`, an error response such as 503 (Service Unavailable), or an {{ServerResponse/error()}} response makes no claim that the request went unprocessed.

ServerContext {#sec-servercontext}
==================================

The request {#servercontext-request}
------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">request</dfn> attribute returns the {{ServerRequest}} for the [=exchange=], constructed by the implementation from the incoming HTTP message ([[#sec-serverrequest]]).
</div>

Connection metadata {#servercontext-connection}
------------------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">remoteAddress</dfn> attribute returns the {{SocketAddress}} of the remote peer. If the remote address is not available (e.g., the runtime abstracts it away), the implementation MAY return a {{SocketAddress}} without a {{SocketAddress/family}}, with empty {{SocketAddress/address}} and `0` {{SocketAddress/port}} ([[#idl-socketaddress]]).
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">localAddress</dfn> attribute returns the {{SocketAddress}} of the local endpoint the connection arrived on, identifying the binding when a server listens on multiple addresses or ports. The same availability escape applies as for {{ServerContext/remoteAddress}}.
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">alpnProtocol</dfn> attribute returns the ALPN protocol identifier negotiated for this connection. Common values are `"http/1.1"`, `"h2"`, and `"h3"`. If ALPN was not negotiated, the value is the ALPN protocol identifier of the HTTP version in use: `"http/1.1"` for HTTP/1.1 (or HTTP/1.0), and `"h2c"` for HTTP/2 over cleartext TCP (prior knowledge).
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">encrypted</dfn> attribute returns `true` if the connection is protected by TLS (including QUIC, where TLS is intrinsic), and `false` otherwise. This distinguishes plaintext HTTP/1.1 from HTTP/1.1 over TLS without ALPN, which {{ServerContext/alpnProtocol}} alone cannot.
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">serverName</dfn> attribute returns the SNI server name from the TLS ClientHello, or `null` if the connection is not encrypted or the client did not send SNI. Unlike the `Host` header, this value is what TLS certificate selection was based on; the two can legitimately differ, which matters for multi-tenant routing.
</div>

Note: The {{ServerContext/alpnProtocol}} value identifies the HTTP version of the connection. Handlers generally should not branch on this value. It is provided for logging, debugging, and the rare case where application behavior legitimately depends on the transport.

Priority {#servercontext-priority}
-----------------------------------

[RFC 9218](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9218) defines the Extensible Prioritization Scheme with {{RequestPriority/urgency}} (0&ndash;7, default 3) and {{RequestPriority/incremental}} (boolean, default false). Priority signals flow in two directions &mdash; client to server and server to implementation &mdash; represented by two properties:

*   **{{ServerContext/clientPriority}}** (read-only) &mdash; what the client asked for.
*   **{{ServerContext/serverPriority}}** (read/write) &mdash; what the server decided.

### Client priority ### {#priority-client}

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">clientPriority</dfn> attribute returns the client's current priority signal, parsed from the `Priority` request header. The header is parsed as a Dictionary structured field ([[RFC9651]]), as [RFC 9218 Section 4](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9218#section-4) specifies: if parsing fails, the header is ignored; unknown parameters are ignored; and a parameter whose value is out of range or of the wrong type (an urgency outside 0&ndash;7, or a non-Boolean incremental) is ignored, so that member keeps its default. The same rules apply to the priority field value of a `PRIORITY_UPDATE` frame.

The {{ServerContext/clientPriority}} getter is live: it always reflects the most recent client signal. When the client sends a PRIORITY_UPDATE frame (HTTP/2 or HTTP/3), the implementation updates the value returned by {{ServerContext/clientPriority}} before invoking any registered callback.

If the request has no `Priority` header and no PRIORITY_UPDATE frame has been received, {{ServerContext/clientPriority}} returns the default values (`{ urgency: 3, incremental: false }`).
</div>

### Reprioritization ### {#priority-reprioritization}

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">onpriority</dfn> attribute holds the callback invoked when a PRIORITY_UPDATE frame is received for this request's stream, following the web platform's event-handler idiom: `ctx.onpriority = fn`. The callback receives a {{RequestPriority}} dictionary with the new values. The attribute defaults to `null`; because it is a plain attribute, at most one callback is registered at a time, assignment replaces the previous value, and assigning `null` removes it. When a `PRIORITY_UPDATE` frame is received, the implementation updates {{ServerContext/clientPriority}} and then queues a task to invoke the callback, if one is registered when the task runs, with the new priority and with the {{ServerContext}} as its callback this value. The callback is invoked for every frame received, even if the values have not changed. If it throws, the exception is reported, as for an exception thrown by an event listener, and has no other effect. Once the [=exchange=] has reached a terminal state, the callback is no longer invoked.

PRIORITY_UPDATE frames are hop-by-hop (HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 only) and may arrive at any time during the request lifecycle. If no callback is registered, PRIORITY_UPDATE frames still update {{ServerContext/clientPriority}} &mdash; the handler is simply not notified.

For HTTP/1.1, where PRIORITY_UPDATE frames do not exist, the callback is never invoked. The initial priority from the `Priority` header (if present) is still available via {{ServerContext/clientPriority}}.
</div>

### Server priority ### {#priority-server}

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">serverPriority</dfn> attribute is a read/write property that overrides the server-internal scheduling priority for this response's delivery. It defaults to `null`, meaning "use the client's priority signal." The setter converts a non-null value to a {{RequestPriority}}, and throws a {{RangeError}}, leaving the attribute unchanged, if its {{RequestPriority/urgency}} is greater than 7.

When {{ServerContext/serverPriority}} is `null`, the implementation uses the client's priority signal ({{ServerContext/clientPriority}}) for scheduling. When {{ServerContext/serverPriority}} is set, the implementation uses the server's value instead, regardless of subsequent PRIORITY_UPDATE frames from the client. (The client's updates still appear in {{ServerContext/clientPriority}} and still trigger the {{ServerContext/onpriority}} callback &mdash; the server simply overrides the scheduling decision.)
</div>

{{ServerContext/serverPriority}} is purely about server-internal scheduling. It does NOT set the `Priority` response header &mdash; that is for signaling priority preferences to intermediaries and is set directly on the {{ServerResponse}}:

```js
ctx.serverPriority = { urgency: 1 };   // internal scheduling
return new ServerResponse(body, {
  headers: { 'Priority': 'u=1' },      // signal to intermediaries
});
```

These are intentionally separate concerns. A server may override internal scheduling without signaling intermediaries, or vice versa.

Informational responses {#servercontext-informational}
------------------------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ServerContext">sendInformational(status, headers)</dfn> method sends an informational (1xx) response to the client.

<ol>
<li>If the implementation does not support informational responses, throw a `"NotSupportedError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>If |status| is `101`, or is not in the range 100&ndash;199 inclusive, throw a {{RangeError}}.
<li>If this context is [=ServerContext/inert=] ([[#connectcontext-fallthrough]]), or the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/committed=], [=exchange/complete=], or [=exchange/denied=], throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>If the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/aborted=], return.
<li>If the request was received over HTTP/1.0, return without sending anything; the exchange's state does not change. A server must not send a 1xx response to an HTTP/1.0 client ([RFC 9110 Section 15.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-15.2)). This is not an error, because the handler cannot change the client's HTTP version: like a call on an [=exchange/aborted=] exchange, the call is inert. HTTP/1.0 defines no `Expect: 100-continue` handling, so no [=pending 100 obligation=] exists for such a request.
<li>If |status| is `100`:
    <ol>
    <li>If no [=pending 100 obligation=] exists, throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
    <li>Let |fields| be the result of [=convert a headers init|converting=] |headers| if it is given, and « » otherwise.
    <li>Send a `100 Continue` response with the header fields in |fields|, and clear the obligation. The exchange is now [=exchange/informational=].
    <li>Return.
    </ol>
<li>Let |fields| be the result of [=convert a headers init|converting=] |headers| if it is given, and « » otherwise.
<li>If {{ServerContext/autoContinue}} is `true` and a [=pending 100 obligation=] exists, send a `100 Continue` response and clear the obligation.
<li>Send the informational response with status |status| and the header fields in |fields|. The exchange is now [=exchange/informational=].
</ol>

Header fields in |headers| whose names or values are invalid cause a {{TypeError}} to be thrown ([[#serverheaders-model]]). Informational responses are sent subject to [[#serverheaders-outgoing]].
</div>

Status `101 (Switching Protocols)` is categorically rejected: protocol upgrades are handled through the `connect()` handler, where {{ConnectContext/accept()}} performs the handshake on the application's behalf ([[#connectcontext-upgrade]]). A `101` is also unrepresentable in HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 ([RFC 9113 Section 8.6](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9113#section-8.6)), so permitting it would leak an HTTP/1.1 wire artifact through the version-transparent handler model.

Notable informational status codes:

*   **100 Continue**: Indicates the server is willing to accept the request body. Sent by the implementation according to the rules below.
*   **103 Early Hints**: Provides header fields (typically `Link` headers) that the client can use to start preloading resources before the final response ([RFC 8297](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8297)).

An implementation that does not support informational responses throws a `"NotSupportedError"` {{DOMException}} rather than silently discarding the call: a swallowed 1xx would misrepresent observable protocol behavior that applications depend on, such as `Expect: 100-continue` flows and Early Hints preloading.

Note: The `onInformation` callback that [[FETCH-ONINFORMATION]] proposes for `fetch()` is the client-side counterpart of {{ServerContext/sendInformational()}}. It is invoked with the status code and header fields of each informational response that the client receives, the two values that {{ServerContext/sendInformational()}} takes.

A {{Headers}} object is accepted as a {{ServerHeadersInit}}, so a handler that forwards a request with `fetch()` can relay the Early Hints it receives:

```js
const upstream = await fetch(originURL, {
  onInformation(status, headers) {
    if (status === 103) ctx.sendInformational(status, headers);
  },
});
```

### 100 Continue ### {#servercontext-100-continue}

When a request arrives with `Expect: 100-continue`, the implementation records a <dfn export>pending 100 obligation</dfn> rather than responding immediately. Sending `100 Continue` on arrival would invite the request body before the handler has had the opportunity to {{ServerContext/deny()}} the exchange, defeating the purpose of the `Expect` mechanism.

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">autoContinue</dfn> attribute returns `true` if the implementation resolves the [=pending 100 obligation=] automatically, and `false` if sending the `100 Continue` is the application's responsibility. In the infrastructure layer the value is configured via {{ServerOptions/autoContinue}} (default `true`). In runtimes without the infrastructure layer, the value is determined by the runtime &mdash; platform policy, a compatibility flag, or similar external mechanism &mdash; and this attribute is how the application discovers it.
</div>

An explicit `sendInformational(100)` call is valid if and only if a [=pending 100 obligation=] exists, in both modes; it sends the `100 Continue` (with the caller's headers) and clears the obligation. Calling it when no obligation exists &mdash; the request carried no `Expect: 100-continue`, or a `100 Continue` was already sent &mdash; throws an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}: an unsolicited or duplicate `100 Continue` is always a programming error. ([RFC 9110 Section 15.2.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-15.2.1) merely has clients discard such interim responses; this API rejects them loudly rather than emitting meaningless ones.)

The obligation otherwise resolves at the first of the following events:

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Event</th><th>{{ServerContext/autoContinue}} is `true`</th><th>{{ServerContext/autoContinue}} is `false`</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>The handler calls `sendInformational(100)`</td><td colspan=2>Send `100 Continue` with the caller's headers; clear the obligation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The handler sends another 1xx via {{ServerContext/sendInformational()}}</td><td>Send `100 Continue` first, then the requested response</td><td>Send only the requested response</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The handler begins iterating the request body ([[#serverrequest-body]])</td><td>Send `100 Continue` &mdash; the server is ready to receive</td><td>Nothing is sent</td></tr>
    <tr><td>On an [=upgrade-originated=] exchange, the handler calls {{ConnectContext/accept()}}, {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}}, or {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocketStream()}}</td><td colspan=2>Send `100 Continue` before the `101 (Switching Protocols)` ([[#connectcontext-upgrade]])</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The handler returns a final {{ServerResponse}} without reading the body</td><td colspan=2>Omit, per [RFC 9110 Section 10.1.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-10.1.1)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>{{ServerContext/deny()}} is called, or the exchange is [=exchange/aborted=]</td><td colspan=2>Discard</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

An automatically sent `100 Continue` transitions the exchange to [=exchange/informational=], exactly as an explicit informational response does: bytes have been sent, so {{ServerContext/deny()}} is no longer available.

Flushing the pending `100 Continue` ahead of any other explicit informational response keeps the client's view deterministic: a client waiting on its `Expect` never has to interpret some other 1xx as an implicit answer. Any 1xx from the handler means the exchange is proceeding, so the implementation confirms the body is welcome first.

Note: When {{ServerContext/autoContinue}} is `false`, iterating the request body without having sent the `100 Continue` can stall: clients typically wait for the `100` before transmitting content, though [RFC 9110 Section 10.1.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-10.1.1) advises them not to wait indefinitely. Liveness is the application's responsibility. What manual mode buys is finer control &mdash; for example, sending `103 Early Hints` while authorization is still deciding, without committing to receive the body.

Denial {#servercontext-denial}
-------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ServerContext">deny(error)</dfn> method signals that the handler is declining to process the request. No {{ServerResponse}} is sent; the handler returns `undefined` (or nothing) after calling {{ServerContext/deny()}}.

<ol>
<li>If the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/aborted=], return.
<li>If this context is [=ServerContext/inert=] ([[#connectcontext-fallthrough]]), or the [=exchange=] is not [=exchange/open=], throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}} (see [[#exchange-states]]).
<li>If |error| is the [=fall-through sentinel=]:
    <ol>
    <li>If the context is not a {{ConnectContext}}, or the exchange is not [=upgrade-originated=], throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}. (The `fetch()` invoked after a fall-through receives a {{ServerContext}}, so it cannot fall through again.)
    <li>Mark the exchange as falling through to `fetch()`, and set this context's [=ServerContext/inert=] flag. The exchange remains [=exchange/open=] and nothing is sent. See [[#connectcontext-fallthrough]].
    <li>Return.
    </ol>
<li>Set the exchange state to [=exchange/denied=].
<li>Reset the stream, carrying the protocol error code selected by |error|:
    *   HTTP/2: `RST_STREAM`
    *   HTTP/3: `RESET_STREAM`
    *   HTTP/1.1: Implementation-defined (close the connection, or send a 503 response and close as a pragmatic fallback)
</ol>
</div>

{{ServerContext/deny()}} is terminal for the handler: the only valid completion after calling it is `undefined` (or a promise resolving to `undefined`). The handler may perform additional work before returning &mdash; the exchange is not complete until the handler's promise and all {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} promises settle &mdash; but no response is expected, and resolving with any other value is an invalid return ([[#handler-errors]]).

If the [=error code carried by=] |error|, given the codes sendable via {{ServerContext/deny()}} ([[#errors-codes]]), is not null, the implementation maps it to the corresponding wire-level code. When |error| is omitted or carries no such code, the default is `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_REJECTED` (REFUSED_STREAM / H3_REQUEST_REJECTED). This is the correct default because {{ServerContext/deny()}} means the request was not processed, which is exactly the semantic REFUSED_STREAM was designed to express. A client receiving this signal knows the request can be safely retried &mdash; including non-idempotent methods like POST.

The `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY` code is a connection-level signal. Unlike the other codes which reset a single stream, `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY` instructs the implementation to:

1.  Refuse the current request with the default code, `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_REJECTED` (REFUSED_STREAM / H3_REQUEST_REJECTED).
2.  Send a GOAWAY frame on the underlying connection, indicating that no new streams will be accepted. The frame carries the NO_ERROR (HTTP/2) or H3_NO_ERROR (HTTP/3) error code. Its last stream identifier (HTTP/2) or stream ID (HTTP/3) is chosen so that the current request is not included, and so that streams that were not processed may be retried on another connection ([RFC 9113 Section 6.8](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9113#section-6.8), [RFC 9114 Section 5.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114#section-5.2)).
3.  Allow in-flight streams (requests already being processed by other handler invocations on the same connection) to complete normally.

This provides a per-request escape hatch for connection-level concerns &mdash; rate limiting, credential revocation, or misbehavior detection &mdash; without exposing a connection object to the handler.

For HTTP/1.1, the fallback of {{ServerContext/deny()}} already closes the connection, so `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY` behaves as the default code does.

Note: Proactive GOAWAY (shutting down connections without a triggering request) is handled by {{Closeable/close()}} and {{Closeable/destroy()}} in the infrastructure layer, not by {{ServerContext/deny()}}. The {{ServerContext/deny()}} mechanism covers the reactive case where a handler decides during request processing that the connection should be closed.

Request lifecycle {#servercontext-lifecycle}
--------------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ServerContext">waitUntil(promise)</dfn> method extends the lifetime of the request processing beyond the return of the handler function. The [=server=] does not consider the request fully complete until all promises passed to {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} have settled.

A call made after the [=exchange=] has ended (it has reached a terminal state and every promise previously passed to {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} has settled, or the {{TimeoutOptions/waitUntil}} limit has been reached) has no effect: the promise is not tracked, and its rejection is not reported. A call made while earlier promises are still outstanding, for example from code that runs as one of them settles, extends the lifetime as any other call does.
</div>

This is analogous to `ExtendableEvent.waitUntil()` in [Service Workers](https://w3c.github.io/ServiceWorker/), and to similar lifecycle-extension mechanisms in existing server-side runtimes.

Unlike the optional features enumerated in [[#conformance]], {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} MUST be supported by every implementation: applications depend on it for cleanup and logging actions that must complete.

An implementation MAY impose an implementation-defined time limit on how long it will wait for outstanding {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} promises to settle. In the infrastructure layer, this limit is configurable via the {{TimeoutOptions/waitUntil}} member of {{TimeoutOptions}} ([[#serve-timeouts]]). Reaching the limit does not cancel the promises &mdash; the exchange simply stops waiting for them; see [[#serve-timeouts]].

If a promise passed to {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} rejects, the implementation reports the rejection in an implementation-defined manner (e.g., logging). The rejection does not affect the response and does not invoke the handler's `error()` method ([[#handler-error]]).

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">completed</dfn> attribute returns a promise that settles when the [=exchange=] reaches a terminal state ([[#exchange-states]]), or `null` if the implementation is not tracking completion for this exchange.

<ul>
<li>The promise is fulfilled when the exchange becomes [=exchange/complete=], or when the denial signal has been sent following {{ServerContext/deny()}} ([=exchange/denied=]). For a response, this is when the response was fully handed to the transport (including any trailers), or the stream was reset for a returned {{ServerResponse/error()}} response ([[#serverresponse-sending]]). For a [=tunnel=], WebSocket connection, or WebTransport session, it is when that has closed, or has been terminated by the server.
<li>The promise is rejected with the [=abort reason=] when the exchange becomes [=exchange/aborted=]: client abort, stream reset, connection loss, or a timeout ([[#serve-timeouts]]), including the abnormal termination of an established tunnel by the peer.
</ul>
</div>

"Handed to the transport" does not imply delivery acknowledgment from the peer. {{ServerContext/completed}} reports the conclusion of the response or tunnel only; it does not wait for {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} promises to settle.

An established tunnel, WebSocket connection, or WebTransport session is part of its [=exchange=]: the exchange is in flight until it closes, even after the `connect()` handler has returned. A handler does not need {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} to keep an established tunnel alive; the implementation keeps the execution environment alive for it as for any in-flight exchange.

The value is stable for the lifetime of the exchange: repeated gets return the same promise, or `null` consistently. The promise is always [=mark as handled|marked as handled=], so a rejection the application never observes is not reported as unhandled.

An implementation MAY return `null` globally, per deployment, or per exchange &mdash; for example, for a response handed off to a transport-level optimization the implementation no longer observes. A non-null {{ServerContext/completed}} does not obligate the implementation to extend the lifetime of the execution environment beyond its normal policy; where tracking completion would require doing so, returning `null` is the conforming behavior.

Note: `await ctx.completed` degrades gracefully where completion is untracked, since awaiting `null` yields `null` immediately. Code written against this attribute cannot hang on runtimes that do not provide it:

```js
const start = performance.now();
const response = await inner(ctx);
ctx.completed?.then(
  () => log('sent', performance.now() - start),
  (err) => log('aborted', err.code),
);
return response;
```


ServerRequest {#sec-serverrequest}
==================================

A {{ServerRequest}} represents the HTTP request message of an [=exchange=]. The implementation constructs it from the incoming message, and handlers reach it through {{ServerContext/request}}.

Attributes {#serverrequest-attributes}
--------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerRequest">method</dfn> attribute returns the request method.
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerRequest">url</dfn> attribute returns the full request URL, reconstructed from the request target, `Host` header, and connection properties (scheme, port).

A plain CONNECT request has a target in authority-form ([RFC 9110 Section 9.3.6](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-9.3.6)), with no scheme or path. For such a request, the URL is the connection's scheme (`https` if the connection is encrypted, `http` otherwise), followed by `://`, the target authority, and `/`: for example, `https://example.com:8443/`. As with any URL, a port equal to the scheme's default port is omitted when the URL is serialized.

The authority of the URL is determined as follows:

*   If the request target is in absolute-form, the URL is the request target, and the `Host` header field is ignored ([RFC 9112 Section 3.2.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9112#section-3.2.2)).
*   Otherwise, on HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, the authority is the value of `:authority` if present, and of `Host` otherwise. A request in which both are present with different values is malformed ([RFC 9113 Section 8.3.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9113#section-8.3.1), [RFC 9114 Section 4.3.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114#section-4.3.1)).
*   Otherwise, on HTTP/1.1, the authority is the value of `Host`. An HTTP/1.1 request without exactly one `Host` header field is malformed ([RFC 9112 Section 3.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9112#section-3.2)).

Malformed requests are rejected by the implementation before dispatch ([[#http-versions-transparent]]); no [=exchange=] is created for them.
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerRequest">headers</dfn> attribute returns a {{ServerHeaders}} object whose [=ServerHeaders/header list=] holds the request header fields and whose [=ServerHeaders/mutability=] is "`immutable`", so that its {{ServerHeaders/append()}}, {{ServerHeaders/delete()}}, and {{ServerHeaders/set()}} methods throw a {{TypeError}}.

The implementation builds the header list from the received header section so that a handler sees the same fields on every HTTP version:

*   Pseudo-header fields, such as `:method` and `:authority`, do not appear.
*   On HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, if the request has no `Host` header field, a `host` field with the value of `:authority` is added.
*   On HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, multiple `cookie` field lines are joined into one, separated by `"; "` ([RFC 9113 Section 8.2.3](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9113#section-8.2.3), [RFC 9114 Section 4.2.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114#section-4.2.1)).
*   `Connection`, `Keep-Alive`, `Proxy-Connection`, `Transfer-Encoding`, and any other field named in `Connection` are removed ([RFC 9110 Section 7.6.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-7.6.1)), except `Upgrade` and `TE`. `Upgrade` is kept because [=upgrade-originated=] exchanges are routed on it ([[#connectcontext-upgrade]]), and `TE` because it tells the handler whether the client accepts trailer fields.
*   Each obs-fold in a field value is replaced with SP ([RFC 9112 Section 5.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9112#section-5.2)).
*   On HTTP/1.1, a request that carries both `Transfer-Encoding` and `Content-Length` might be an attempt at request smuggling ([RFC 9112 Section 6.3](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9112#section-6.3)). The implementation either rejects it as malformed before dispatch, or processes it according to `Transfer-Encoding` alone, removes `Content-Length` from the header list so that no field inconsistent with the message framing reaches the handler, and closes the connection after the response ([RFC 9112 Section 6.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9112#section-6.1)).

</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerRequest">signal</dfn> attribute returns an {{AbortSignal}} that is aborted when the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/aborted=] &mdash; for example, because the client disconnects or the connection is lost &mdash; with the [=abort reason=] as its abort reason ([[#errors-inbound]]).
</div>

Trailers {#serverrequest-trailers}
----------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerRequest">trailers</dfn> attribute returns a promise for the request's trailer fields. It returns the same promise every time.

<ul>
<li>For the request of a CONNECT or extended CONNECT exchange, which has no content ([[#serverrequest-body]]), the promise is rejected with an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>Otherwise, the promise is fulfilled with a {{ServerHeaders}} object containing the trailer fields once the body has been read to its end ([[#serverrequest-body]]), or, for a request without a body, once the request has been received. The object's [=ServerHeaders/mutability=] is "`immutable`", and fields with a [=prohibited trailer name=] are removed from its [=ServerHeaders/header list=] ([[#serverheaders-trailers]]). If no trailer fields remain, the header list is empty.
<li>If the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/aborted=] first, the promise is rejected with the [=abort reason=]. If the body exceeds a configured content limit ([[#serve-limits]]), the promise is rejected with the same [=protocol error=] that iteration rejects with.
<li>If the body will not be read to its end &mdash; iteration ends early, or the exchange becomes [=exchange/complete=] or [=exchange/denied=] first &mdash; the promise is rejected with a [=protocol error=] whose code is `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_BODY_REJECTED`.
</ul>

The promise is always [=mark as handled|marked as handled=], so a rejection the application never observes is not reported as unhandled.
</div>

Note: This is how the `trailers` attribute that [[FETCH-TRAILERS]] proposes behaves for a {{Response}} obtained from `fetch()`: the promise is fulfilled only once the body has been read to its end. Because an [=exchange=] always ends, the promise here does not stay pending when the body is never read; it is rejected.

The body {#serverrequest-body}
------------------------------

The request body is consumed by iterating the {{ServerRequest}}. Each iteration step yields a batch of one or more {{Uint8Array}} chunks, and iteration completes when the body ends &mdash; immediately, for a request without a body. A {{ServerRequest}} therefore conforms to the `AsyncIterable<Uint8Array[]>` protocol of [[!ITER-STREAMS]]: it is a `ByteReadableStream` in that specification's terms, and can be passed directly to its consumers (for example, `await Stream.text(ctx.request)`).

Note: Each step yields a new `Uint8Array[]`, a plain JavaScript array: the batched chunks of [[ITER-STREAMS]].

```js
async fetch(ctx) {
  let size = 0;
  for await (const chunks of ctx.request) {
    for (const chunk of chunks) {
      size += chunk.byteLength;
    }
  }
  return new ServerResponse(`Received ${size} bytes`);
}
```

The body can be read only once: only the first iterator obtained from a {{ServerRequest}} reads it.

<div algorithm="ServerRequest iteration">
A {{ServerRequest}} has a <dfn for="ServerRequest">body reader</dfn>, the first async iterator obtained from it, initially null.

The `Symbol.asyncIterator` method of a {{ServerRequest}} |request|, when called, returns a new async iterator object |iterator|, whose prototype inherits from %AsyncIteratorPrototype% [[!ECMASCRIPT]]. If |request|'s [=ServerRequest/body reader=] is null, it is set to |iterator|.

The `next()` method of |iterator| performs the following steps. Each call settles only after the promise returned by the previous call has settled, as for a Web IDL asynchronous iterator [[!WEBIDL]].

<ol>
<li>If |iterator| is not |request|'s [=ServerRequest/body reader=], return [=a promise rejected with=] a {{TypeError}}.
<li>If |request| is the request of a CONNECT or extended CONNECT exchange, return [=a promise rejected with=] an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}. Such a request has no content: the bytes that follow its header section belong to the [=tunnel=], and are read through the {{Tunnel}} that {{ConnectContext/accept()}} returns ([[#tunnel-raw]]). (An [=upgrade-originated=] request is an ordinary HTTP request, and its content is read as for any other.)
<li>If this is the first call for |iterator|, begin reading the body. For the handling of a [=pending 100 obligation=] at this point, see [[#servercontext-100-continue]].
<li>Return a promise that settles with the first of the following:
    <ul>
    <li>fulfillment with an iterator result object whose `value` is a new Array of one or more {{Uint8Array}} objects holding the next batch of body data, and whose `done` is `false`, once that data is available;
    <li>fulfillment with an iterator result object whose `value` is `undefined` and whose `done` is `true`, once the body has ended;
    <li>a rejection with the [=abort reason=], if the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/aborted=] ([[#errors-inbound]]);
    <li>a rejection with a [=protocol error=] whose code is `ERR_HTTP_CONTENT_TOO_LARGE`, if the body exceeds a configured content limit ([[#serve-limits]]).
    </ul>
</ol>

The `return(value)` method of |iterator| performs the following steps:

<ol>
<li>If |iterator| is |request|'s [=ServerRequest/body reader=] and the body has not ended, refuse the remainder of the body ([[#errors-outbound]]).
<li>Return [=a promise resolved with=] an iterator result object whose `value` is the argument passed to `return()`, and whose `done` is `true`.
</ol>
</div>

The return steps run when iteration ends early &mdash; for example, when a `for await` loop over the body exits through `break`, `return`, or `throw`.


ServerResponse {#sec-serverresponse}
====================================

A {{ServerResponse}} represents the HTTP response message a handler sends for an [=exchange=]. A handler constructs one and returns it from `fetch()`, `connect()`, or `error()` ([[#handler-model]]); the implementation then sends it ([[#serverresponse-sending]]).

A {{ServerResponse}} object has:

*   a <dfn for="ServerResponse">response type</dfn>, "`default`" or "`error`", initially "`default`";
*   a <dfn for="ServerResponse">status code</dfn>, a [=status=], initially 200;
*   a <dfn for="ServerResponse">status message</dfn>, a [=byte sequence=], initially empty;
*   a <dfn for="ServerResponse">headers object</dfn>, a {{ServerHeaders}} object whose [=ServerHeaders/header list=] holds the response header fields;
*   a <dfn for="ServerResponse" id="concept-serverresponse-body">body</dfn>, null or a [=response body source=] ([[#serverresponse-body]]), initially null;
*   a [=ServerResponse/body reader=] ([[#serverresponse-body]]); and
*   a [=ServerResponse/trailer state=] ([[#serverresponse-trailers]]).

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>create a server response</dfn> in a [=ECMAScript/realm=] |realm|:

<ol>
<li>Let |response| be a new {{ServerResponse}} object in |realm|.
<li>Set |response|'s [=ServerResponse/headers object=] to the result of [=create a server headers object|creating a server headers object=] in |realm|.
<li>Return |response|.
</ol>
</div>

Creating a response {#serverresponse-constructor}
-------------------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn constructor for="ServerResponse" lt="ServerResponse(body, init)|ServerResponse(body)|ServerResponse()">new ServerResponse(|body|, |init|)</dfn> constructor steps are:

<ol>
<li>Set [=this=]'s [=ServerResponse/headers object=] to the result of [=create a server headers object|creating a server headers object=] in [=this=]'s [=relevant realm=].
<li>Let |bodyWithType| be null.
<li>If |body| is non-null, set |bodyWithType| to the result of [=extract a response body|extracting a response body=] from |body|.
<li>Perform [=initialize a server response=] given [=this=], |init|, and |bodyWithType|.
</ol>
</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>initialize a server response</dfn>, given a {{ServerResponse}} |response|, a {{ServerResponseInit}} |init|, and null or a [=response body with type=] |bodyWithType|:

<ol>
<li>If |init|["{{ServerResponseInit/status}}"] is not in the range 200 to 599, inclusive, throw a {{RangeError}}.
<li>If |init|["{{ServerResponseInit/statusText}}"] is not the empty string and does not match the `reason-phrase` production ([RFC 9112 Section 4](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9112#section-4)), throw a {{TypeError}}.
<li>Set |response|'s [=ServerResponse/status code=] to |init|["{{ServerResponseInit/status}}"].
<li>Set |response|'s [=ServerResponse/status message=] to |init|["{{ServerResponseInit/statusText}}"].
<li>If |init|["{{ServerResponseInit/headers}}"] [=map/exists=], then [=ServerHeaders/fill=] |response|'s [=ServerResponse/headers object=] with |init|["{{ServerResponseInit/headers}}"].
<li>If |bodyWithType| is non-null:
    <ol>
    <li>If |response|'s [=ServerResponse/status code=] is a [=null body status=], throw a {{TypeError}}.
    <li>Set |response|'s [=ServerResponse/body=] to |bodyWithType|'s [=response body with type/source=].
    <li>If |bodyWithType|'s [=response body with type/type=] is non-null and |response|'s [=ServerResponse/headers object=]'s [=ServerHeaders/header list=] does not [=header list/contain=] `` `Content-Type` ``, then [=header list/append=] (`` `Content-Type` ``, |bodyWithType|'s [=response body with type/type=]) to that [=/header list=].
    </ol>
<li>If |init|["{{ServerResponseInit/trailers}}"] [=map/exists=], then [=process a trailers init=] given |response| and |init|["{{ServerResponseInit/trailers}}"]. Otherwise, set |response|'s [=ServerResponse/trailer state=]'s [=trailer state/state=] to "`complete`".
</ol>
</div>

These steps follow those that the Fetch Standard defines for initializing a `Response`, with the trailer fields added as [[FETCH-SEND-TRAILERS]] proposes.

<div algorithm>
The static <dfn method for="ServerResponse">error()</dfn> method steps are:

<ol>
<li>Let |response| be the result of [=create a server response|creating a server response=] in the <a href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/#current-realm">current realm</a>.
<li>Set |response|'s [=ServerResponse/response type=] to "`error`" and its [=ServerResponse/status code=] to 0.
<li>Set |response|'s [=ServerResponse/trailer state=]'s [=trailer state/state=] to "`complete`".
<li>Return |response|.
</ol>
</div>

A response created by {{ServerResponse/error()}} represents a network error. Returning one resets the stream instead of sending a response ([[#serverresponse-sending]]). Its {{ServerResponse/headers}} can be modified, but are never sent.

<div algorithm>
The static <dfn method for="ServerResponse">redirect(|url|, |status|)</dfn> method steps are:

<ol>
<li>If |url| does not match the `URI-reference` production ([RFC 3986 Section 4.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3986#section-4.1)), throw a {{TypeError}}.
<li>If |status| is not a [=redirect status=], throw a {{RangeError}}.
<li>Let |response| be the result of [=create a server response|creating a server response=] in the <a href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/#current-realm">current realm</a>.
<li>Set |response|'s [=ServerResponse/status code=] to |status|.
<li>[=header list/Append=] (`` `Location` ``, |url|, [=isomorphic encode|isomorphic encoded=]) to |response|'s [=ServerResponse/headers object=]'s [=ServerHeaders/header list=].
<li>Set |response|'s [=ServerResponse/trailer state=]'s [=trailer state/state=] to "`complete`".
<li>Return |response|.
</ol>
</div>

Unlike the Fetch Standard's `Response.redirect()`, {{ServerResponse/redirect()}} does not parse the URL or resolve it against a base URL: the value is sent as given, so a relative reference reaches the client as a relative reference, as [RFC 9110 Section 10.2.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-10.2.2) permits. A `URI-reference` consists of ASCII characters only, so the caller percent-encodes anything else, such as non-ASCII characters and spaces. The headers of the returned response can be modified, for example to set a cookie.

<div algorithm>
The static <dfn method for="ServerResponse">json(|data|, |init|)</dfn> method steps are:

<ol>
<li>Let |bytes| be the result of running [=serialize a JavaScript value to JSON bytes=] on |data|.
<li>Let |response| be the result of [=create a server response|creating a server response=] in the <a href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/#current-realm">current realm</a>.
<li>Perform [=initialize a server response=] given |response|, |init|, and (|bytes|, `` `application/json` ``).
<li>Return |response|.
</ol>
</div>

<div algorithm>
The static <dfn method for="ServerResponse">from(|response|)</dfn> method converts a Fetch Standard {{Response}}, such as one obtained from `fetch()`, into a {{ServerResponse}}. Its steps are:

<ol>
<li>Let |inner| be |response|'s [=Response/response=].
<li>If |inner|'s [=response/type=] is "`opaque`" or "`opaqueredirect`", throw a {{TypeError}}: the status, header fields, and body of an opaque response are not available to script.
<li>If |response| is [=Body/unusable=], throw a {{TypeError}}.
<li>Let |result| be the result of [=create a server response|creating a server response=] in the <a href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/#current-realm">current realm</a>.
<li>If |inner|'s [=response/type=] is "`error`", set |result|'s [=ServerResponse/response type=] to "`error`", its [=ServerResponse/status code=] to 0, and its [=ServerResponse/trailer state=]'s [=trailer state/state=] to "`complete`", and return |result|.
<li>Set |result|'s [=ServerResponse/status code=] to |inner|'s [=response/status=], and its [=ServerResponse/status message=] to |inner|'s [=response/status message=].
<li>Set |result|'s [=ServerResponse/headers object=]'s [=ServerHeaders/header list=] to a copy of |inner|'s [=response/header list=].
<li>If |inner|'s body was decoded according to its `Content-Encoding` when it was fetched ([=handle content codings=]), [=header list/delete=] `` `Content-Encoding` `` and `` `Content-Length` `` from |result|'s [=ServerResponse/headers object=]'s [=ServerHeaders/header list=]: they describe the encoded content, not the decoded body that |result| will send.
<li>If |inner|'s [=response/body=] is non-null, set |result|'s [=ServerResponse/body=] to |inner|'s [=response/body=]'s [=body/stream=]. Reading |result|'s body reads that stream, so |response| becomes [=ReadableStream/disturbed|disturbed=] as it is sent.
<li>If |response| has trailer fields as [[FETCH-TRAILERS]] proposes, [=process a trailers init=] given |result| and the promise for them. Otherwise, set |result|'s [=ServerResponse/trailer state=]'s [=trailer state/state=] to "`complete`".
<li>Return |result|.
</ol>
</div>

A handler that relays an upstream response converts it explicitly:

```js
async fetch(ctx) {
  const upstream = await fetch(new URL(new URL(ctx.request.url).pathname, origin));
  return ServerResponse.from(upstream);
}
```

Returning a {{Response}} itself from a handler is an invalid return ([[#handler-fetch]]), unless the implementation accepts it. An implementation that accepts a returned {{Response}} SHOULD treat it as if it had been converted with {{ServerResponse/from()}}, so that relaying code behaves the same everywhere that it is accepted.

Attributes {#serverresponse-attributes}
---------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerResponse">type</dfn> getter steps are to return [=this=]'s [=ServerResponse/response type=].
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerResponse">url</dfn> getter steps are to return the empty string.
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerResponse">redirected</dfn> getter steps are to return false.
</div>

Note: A {{ServerResponse}} is not obtained by fetching, so it has no URL and is never the result of a redirect.

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerResponse">status</dfn> getter steps are to return [=this=]'s [=ServerResponse/status code=].
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerResponse">ok</dfn> getter steps are to return true if [=this=]'s [=ServerResponse/status code=] is an [=ok status=]; otherwise false.
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerResponse">statusText</dfn> getter steps are to return [=this=]'s [=ServerResponse/status message=].
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerResponse">headers</dfn> getter steps are to return [=this=]'s [=ServerResponse/headers object=].
</div>

The body {#serverresponse-body}
-------------------------------

The body of a {{ServerResponse}} is read by iterating it, as the body of a {{ServerRequest}} is ([[#serverrequest-body]]): each iteration step yields a batch of one or more {{Uint8Array}} chunks, and iteration completes when the body ends &mdash; immediately, for a response without a body. A {{ServerResponse}} is therefore also a `ByteReadableStream` in the terms of [[!ITER-STREAMS]], and can be the body of another {{ServerResponse}}. This lets a handler observe or transform the response of another:

```js
async function* tally(body, done) {
  let size = 0;
  for await (const chunks of body) {
    for (const chunk of chunks) size += chunk.byteLength;
    yield chunks;
  }
  done(size);
}

async fetch(ctx) {
  const inner = await app.fetch(ctx);
  // Pass through denials (undefined), error() responses, and null body statuses.
  if (inner?.type !== 'default' || [204, 205, 304].includes(inner.status)) {
    return inner;
  }
  return new ServerResponse(tally(inner, (size) => log(size)), {
    status: inner.status,
    statusText: inner.statusText,
    headers: inner.headers,
    trailers: inner.trailers,
  });
}
```

The body can be read only once: only the first iterator obtained from a {{ServerResponse}} reads it. Sending the response counts as reading it ([[#serverresponse-sending]]).

Note: A {{ServerResponse}} with a body can therefore be returned only once. A handler that caches a response and returns the same object to every request gets an invalid return on the second request ([[#handler-fetch]]). It should cache the bytes and construct a new response for each request instead. (Returning a {{ServerResponse/clone()}} of the cached response also works, but each clone splits the cached response's body, so the body data is buffered for the cached response for as long as it lives.) A response without a body can be returned any number of times.

<div algorithm="response body source">
A <dfn>response body source</dfn> is one of:

*   a [=byte sequence=];
*   a {{ReadableStream}} provided as the body;
*   an async sequence provided as the body; or
*   one side of a [=split a response body source|split=] body ([[#serverresponse-clone]]).

A <dfn>response body with type</dfn> is a [=tuple=] of a <dfn for="response body with type">source</dfn>, which is a [=response body source=], and a <dfn for="response body with type">type</dfn>, which is null or a [=byte sequence=].

To <dfn>extract a response body</dfn> from a {{ServerBodyInit}} |object|, switch on |object|:

<dl class="switch">
<dt>{{ReadableStream}}
<dd>If |object| is [=ReadableStream/disturbed=] or [=ReadableStream/locked=], throw a {{TypeError}}. Otherwise, return (|object|, null).
<dt>async sequence
<dd>Return (|object|, null).
<dt>{{Blob}}
<dd>Return (the [=byte sequence=] that |object| represents, |object|'s {{Blob/type}} attribute value [=isomorphic encode|isomorphic encoded=] if it is not the empty string, and null otherwise).
<dt>{{BufferSource}}
<dd>Return ([=get a copy of the buffer source|a copy of the bytes held by=] |object|, null).
<dt>{{FormData}}
<dd>Return (the result of running the <a spec=html>multipart/form-data encoding algorithm</a> with |object|'s [=FormData/entry list=] and [=UTF-8=], `` `multipart/form-data; boundary=` `` followed by the <a spec=html>multipart/form-data boundary string</a> generated by that algorithm).
<dt>{{URLSearchParams}}
<dd>Return (the result of running the [=urlencoded serializer|application/x-www-form-urlencoded serializer=] with |object|'s [=URLSearchParams/list=], `` `application/x-www-form-urlencoded;charset=UTF-8` ``).
<dt>scalar value string
<dd>Return ([=UTF-8 encode|the UTF-8 encoding=] of |object|, `` `text/plain;charset=UTF-8` ``).
</dl>

For the non-streaming types, these are the content and type that the Fetch Standard's [=extract|body extraction=] defines for the same values.
</div>

<div algorithm="ServerResponse iteration">
A {{ServerResponse}} has a <dfn for="ServerResponse">body reader</dfn>, the first async iterator obtained from it, initially null. A {{ServerResponse}} is <dfn for="ServerResponse">unusable</dfn> if its [=ServerResponse/body=] and its [=ServerResponse/body reader=] are both non-null.

The [=asynchronous iterator initialization steps=] for a {{ServerResponse}} |response| and its async iterator |iterator| are: if |response|'s [=ServerResponse/body reader=] is null, set it to |iterator|.

To [=get the next iteration result=] for a {{ServerResponse}} |response| and its async iterator |iterator|:

<ol>
<li>If |iterator| is not |response|'s [=ServerResponse/body reader=], return [=a promise rejected with=] a {{TypeError}}.
<li>If |response|'s [=ServerResponse/body=] is null, return [=a promise resolved with=] [=end of iteration=].
<li>Return the result of [=read a batch|reading a batch=] from |response|'s [=ServerResponse/body=].
</ol>

The [=asynchronous iterator return=] steps for a {{ServerResponse}} |response|, its async iterator |iterator|, and a value |reason| are:

<ol>
<li>If |iterator| is |response|'s [=ServerResponse/body reader=], |response|'s [=ServerResponse/body=] is non-null, and the body has not ended, [=close a response body source|close=] |response|'s [=ServerResponse/body=] with |reason|.
<li>Return [=a promise resolved with=] `undefined`.
</ol>
</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>read a batch</dfn> from a [=response body source=] |source|, return a promise that is fulfilled with the next batch from |source| &mdash; a <a spec=infra>list</a> of one or more {{Uint8Array}} objects &mdash; or with [=end of iteration=] once |source| has ended, or that is rejected if reading fails. Depending on |source|:

<dl class="switch">
<dt>A [=byte sequence=]
<dd>The first read yields a batch of one {{Uint8Array}} containing the bytes, unless the byte sequence is empty. After that, |source| has ended.
<dt>A {{ReadableStream}}
<dd>The first read [=ReadableStream/get a reader|gets a reader=] for the stream. Each read then [=ReadableStreamDefaultReader/read a chunk|reads a chunk=] from it. A {{Uint8Array}} chunk is yielded as a batch of one. The end of the stream ends |source|. An error rejects with that error, and a chunk that is not a {{Uint8Array}} rejects with a {{TypeError}}.
<dt>An async sequence
<dd>The first read [=open an async sequence|opens=] it. Each read then [=get an async iterator next value|gets the next value=] of the resulting async iterator. A non-empty list is yielded as a batch; an empty list is skipped, and the next value is read instead. [=End of iteration=] ends |source|. A rejection, or a value that cannot be converted to `sequence<Uint8Array>`, rejects with that error.
<dt>One side of a split body
<dd>As described in [[#serverresponse-clone]].
</dl>

<div class="note">
Each value produced by an async sequence must be a batch: an array (or other iterable) of {{Uint8Array}} chunks, as in [[ITER-STREAMS]]. A generator that yields a bare {{Uint8Array}} is a common mistake. Such a value is converted as a sequence of its byte values, each of which fails to convert to a {{Uint8Array}}. Because the body is read only after the response has been committed, the mistake surfaces as a reset stream ([[#errors-outbound]]), not as an exception from the {{ServerResponse}} constructor. Yield `[chunk]` rather than `chunk`:

```js
async function* body() {
  yield [encoder.encode('hello')];  // correct: a batch of one chunk
  // yield encoder.encode('hello'); // wrong: fails after commit
}
```
</div>
</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>close a response body source</dfn> |source| with a |reason|:

<dl class="switch">
<dt>A {{ReadableStream}}
<dd>[=ReadableStream/Cancel=] |source| with |reason|.
<dt>An async sequence that has been opened
<dd>[=close an async iterator|Close=] its async iterator with |reason|.
<dt>One side of a split body
<dd>As described in [[#serverresponse-clone]].
<dt>Otherwise
<dd>Do nothing.
</dl>
</div>

Trailers {#serverresponse-trailers}
-----------------------------------

A <dfn>trailer state</dfn> is a [=struct=] with the following [=struct/items=]:

*   a <dfn for="trailer state">header list</dfn>, a [=/header list=], initially « »;
*   a <dfn for="trailer state">state</dfn>, "`pending`", "`complete`", or "`errored`", initially "`pending`"; and
*   an <dfn for="trailer state">error</dfn>, a JavaScript value, initially `undefined`.

A {{ServerResponse}} has a <dfn for="ServerResponse">trailer state</dfn>, a new [=/trailer state=] unless stated otherwise. A clone shares the trailer state of the response it was cloned from ([[#serverresponse-clone]]).

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>process a trailers init</dfn> given a {{ServerResponse}} |response| and a promise |trailersPromise|, [=react=] to |trailersPromise|:

<ul>
<li>If |trailersPromise| was fulfilled with |init|:
    <ol>
    <li>Let |trailers| be the result of [=convert a headers init|converting=] |init|.
    <li>Let |declared| be the result of [=header list/get, decode, and split|getting, decoding, and splitting=] `` `Trailer` `` from |response|'s [=ServerResponse/headers object=]'s [=ServerHeaders/header list=], or « » if that is null.
    <li>For each [=header/name=] in |trailers| that, [=isomorphic decode|isomorphic decoded=], is not an [=ASCII case-insensitive=] match for an item of |declared|, [=header list/delete=] it from |trailers|.
    <li>Set |response|'s [=ServerResponse/trailer state=]'s [=trailer state/header list=] to |trailers|, and its [=trailer state/state=] to "`complete`".
    </ol>
<li>If |trailersPromise| was rejected with |reason|, set |response|'s [=ServerResponse/trailer state=]'s [=trailer state/state=] to "`errored`" and its [=trailer state/error=] to |reason|.
</ul>

If the fulfillment value cannot be converted to a {{ServerHeadersInit}}, or [=convert a headers init|converting=] it throws, the [=ServerResponse/trailer state=] is errored with the resulting exception, as if |trailersPromise| had been rejected with it.
</div>

Trailer fields are sent only if the response declares them in its `Trailer` header field ([RFC 9110 Section 6.6.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-6.6.2)): fields that are not declared are dropped. A gRPC handler, for example, declares `Trailer: grpc-status, grpc-message`.

<div class="advisement">
Undeclared trailer fields are dropped silently. This is stricter than HTTP, where `Trailer` is a recommendation ([RFC 9110 Section 6.6.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-6.6.2)), and stricter than some HTTP/2 stacks, which send trailer fields without declaring them. Code ported from such a stack, such as a gRPC server that sends `grpc-status` in trailers without declaring it, must add the `Trailer` header field, or the client will not receive the fields. The rule follows the trailer handling proposed for the Fetch Standard [[FETCH-SEND-TRAILERS]], so that the same response behaves the same on both sides.
</div>

```js
async function* rows(db, done) {
  let count = 0;
  for await (const row of db.query('SELECT * FROM users')) {
    count++;
    yield [encoder.encode(JSON.stringify(row) + '\n')];
  }
  done.resolve({ 'Row-Count': String(count) });
}

async fetch(ctx) {
  const done = Promise.withResolvers();
  return new ServerResponse(rows(this.db, done), {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-ndjson', 'Trailer': 'Row-Count' },
    trailers: done.promise,
  });
}
```

<div algorithm="ServerResponse trailers">
A {{ServerResponse}} has a <dfn for="ServerResponse">trailers promise</dfn>, null or a promise, initially null.

The <dfn attribute for="ServerResponse">trailers</dfn> getter steps are:

<ol>
<li>If [=this=]'s [=ServerResponse/trailers promise=] is null, set it to [=a new promise=] in [=this=]'s [=relevant realm=], to be settled as follows:
    <ul>
    <li>Once [=this=]'s [=ServerResponse/trailer state=]'s [=trailer state/state=] is "`complete`", the promise is fulfilled with the result of [=create a server headers object|creating a server headers object=] in [=this=]'s [=relevant realm=], given a copy of the trailer state's [=trailer state/header list=] and "`immutable`".
    <li>Once it is "`errored`", the promise is rejected with the trailer state's [=trailer state/error=].
    </ul>
<li>Return [=this=]'s [=ServerResponse/trailers promise=].
</ol>

If the trailer state has already left "`pending`" when the promise is created, the promise is settled at once. The promise is always [=mark as handled|marked as handled=], so a rejection the application never observes is not reported as unhandled. A clone gets a promise of its own, settled from the trailer state it shares ([[#serverresponse-clone]]).
</div>

Note: These trailer fields follow those that [[FETCH-SEND-TRAILERS]] proposes for the Fetch Standard's {{Response}}. The {{ServerResponseInit/trailers}} member is a promise for the fields, and fields not declared in `Trailer` are dropped. A response created without that member has no trailer fields, a clone shares the trailer state, and the {{ServerResponse/trailers}} getter returns the same promise every time. Where the Fetch Standard applies its header guard, this specification instead keeps fields with a [=prohibited trailer name=] from being sent ([[#serverheaders-trailers]]). A {{Headers}} object is accepted as a {{ServerHeadersInit}}, so the `trailers` promise of a {{Response}} obtained from `fetch()` [[FETCH-TRAILERS]] can be given as {{ServerResponseInit/trailers}} when relaying that response.

Cloning {#serverresponse-clone}
-------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ServerResponse">clone()</dfn> method steps are:

<ol>
<li>If [=this=] is [=ServerResponse/unusable=], throw a {{TypeError}}.
<li>Let |clone| be the result of [=create a server response|creating a server response=] in [=this=]'s [=relevant realm=].
<li>Set |clone|'s [=ServerResponse/response type=], [=ServerResponse/status code=], and [=ServerResponse/status message=] to those of [=this=].
<li>Set |clone|'s [=ServerResponse/headers object=]'s [=ServerHeaders/header list=] to a copy of [=this=]'s [=ServerResponse/headers object=]'s [=ServerHeaders/header list=].
<li>Set |clone|'s [=ServerResponse/trailer state=] to [=this=]'s [=ServerResponse/trailer state=].
<li>If [=this=]'s [=ServerResponse/body=] is non-null, [=split a response body source|split=] it, and set [=this=]'s [=ServerResponse/body=] to the first side and |clone|'s [=ServerResponse/body=] to the second.
<li>Return |clone|.
</ol>
</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>split a response body source</dfn> |source| is to replace it with two sides, each of which yields the complete sequence of batches that |source| produces, independently of the other:

*   |source| is read only as far as the side that is further ahead requires. Batches that the other side has not read yet are buffered for it, without limit.
*   The first side yields the chunks that |source| produces. The second side yields copies of them, so that detaching or modifying a chunk read from one side does not affect the other.
*   If reading |source| fails, each side rejects with that error once it reaches the point of failure.
*   [=close a response body source|Closing=] one side stops only that side. Once both sides have been closed, |source| is closed with the reason given for the later of the two.

</div>

Sending a response {#serverresponse-sending}
--------------------------------------------

When a handler returns a {{ServerResponse}} |response| ([[#handler-model]]), and |response| is not [=ServerResponse/unusable=], the implementation sends it:

<ol>
<li>The [=exchange=] becomes [=exchange/committed=], and |response|'s [=ServerResponse/headers object=]'s [=ServerHeaders/mutability=] is set to "`immutable`".
<li>If |response|'s [=ServerResponse/response type=] is "`error`", the implementation resets the stream with `ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR`. On HTTP/1.1, which has no stream reset, it closes the connection without sending a response. Nothing else of |response| is sent, and the `error()` handler is not invoked.
<li>Otherwise, the implementation sends the response head: |response|'s [=ServerResponse/status code=], its [=ServerResponse/status message=] (HTTP/1.1 only; HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 have no reason phrase, so the status message is not sent), and its header fields, subject to [[#serverheaders-outgoing]].
<li>If |response|'s [=ServerResponse/body=] is non-null, the implementation reads it: it sets |response|'s [=ServerResponse/body reader=] to an iterator of its own, and [=read a batch|reads batches=] from the body, sending their bytes, until the body ends. If reading fails, the implementation [=close a response body source|closes=] the body with the error and resets the stream ([[#errors-outbound]]).
<li>Once the body has ended (at once, for a response without a body), the implementation waits for |response|'s [=ServerResponse/trailer state=] to leave "`pending`". The wait is bounded by the {{TimeoutOptions/send}} timeout ([[#serve-timeouts]]):
    <ul>
    <li>If it is "`complete`", the implementation sends the fields of its [=trailer state/header list=], if any, as the trailer section, subject to [[#serverheaders-outgoing]], and ends the stream.
    <li>If it is "`errored`", the implementation resets the stream ([[#errors-outbound]]). If the [=error code carried by=] the trailer state's [=trailer state/error=], given the codes sendable by aborting a committed response, is not null, that code is mapped to the wire; otherwise `ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR` is used.
    </ul>
</ol>

An implementation that does not support sending response trailers ([[#conformance]]) ends the stream once the body has ended, without waiting for the trailer state. So does any implementation on a connection that cannot carry trailer fields, such as HTTP/1.1 without the chunked transfer coding ([[#http-versions-features]]). For a response to a HEAD request, neither the body nor trailer fields are sent: the implementation sends the response head, [=close a response body source|closes=] the body, if any, without reading it, and ends the stream without waiting for the trailer state. The header fields are sent as the response gives them; the implementation does not derive a `Content-Length` from the unread body ([[#handler-fetch]]).

If the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/aborted=] while the response is being sent, reading stops and the body is closed with the [=abort reason=] ([[#errors-inbound]]).

Once a response is sent, its {{ServerResponse/headers}} can no longer be modified: {{ServerHeaders/append()}}, {{ServerHeaders/delete()}}, and {{ServerHeaders/set()}} throw a {{TypeError}}. This includes a response created by {{ServerResponse/error()}}, whose headers are never put on the wire. Until a response is returned from a handler, its headers remain modifiable, including those of a response created by {{ServerResponse/redirect()}} or {{ServerResponse/error()}}.

Sending does not change the trailer state: {{ServerResponse/trailers}} reports the fields that were provided, whether or not they were sent.


ServerHeaders {#sec-serverheaders}
==================================

A {{ServerHeaders}} object holds the header fields or trailer fields of a {{ServerRequest}} or {{ServerResponse}}. Its methods are those of the Fetch Standard's {{Headers}} interface, and they operate on that standard's [=/header list=], but none of the guards that {{Headers}} applies for browsers apply: no header name is forbidden, and `Set-Cookie` can be read and set like any other field. Header names and values are still validated, which prevents header injection and response splitting.

The model {#serverheaders-model}
--------------------------------

A {{ServerHeaders}} object has:

*   a <dfn for="ServerHeaders">header list</dfn>, a [=/header list=], initially « »; and
*   a <dfn for="ServerHeaders">mutability</dfn>, "`mutable`" or "`immutable`", initially "`mutable`".

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>create a server headers object</dfn> in a [=ECMAScript/realm=] |realm|, given an optional [=/header list=] |list| (default « ») and an optional mutability |mutability| (default "`mutable`"), return a new {{ServerHeaders}} object in |realm| whose [=ServerHeaders/header list=] is |list| and whose [=ServerHeaders/mutability=] is |mutability|.
</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn for="ServerHeaders">validate</dfn> a name |name| and value |value| for a {{ServerHeaders}} object |headers|:

<ol>
<li>If |name| is not a [=header name=] or |value| is not a [=header value=], throw a {{TypeError}}.
<li>If |headers|'s [=ServerHeaders/mutability=] is "`immutable`", throw a {{TypeError}}.
</ol>
</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>convert a headers init</dfn> |object|, a {{ServerHeadersInit}}, to a [=/header list=]:

<ol>
<li>Let |list| be a new [=/header list=].
<li>Let |pairs| be |object| if it is a sequence. Otherwise, |object| is a record; let |pairs| be the list of « |key|, |value| » for each |key| → |value| of |object|.
<li>For each |pair| of |pairs|:
    <ol>
    <li>If |pair|'s [=list/size=] is not 2, throw a {{TypeError}}.
    <li>Let |name| be |pair|[0], and let |value| be |pair|[1], [=header value/normalize|normalized=].
    <li>If |name| is not a [=header name=] or |value| is not a [=header value=], throw a {{TypeError}}.
    <li>[=header list/Append=] (|name|, |value|) to |list|.
    </ol>
<li>Return |list|.
</ol>
</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn for="ServerHeaders">fill</dfn> a {{ServerHeaders}} object |headers| with a {{ServerHeadersInit}} |object|, [=header list/append=] each [=/header=] of the result of [=convert a headers init|converting=] |object|, in order, to |headers|'s [=ServerHeaders/header list=].
</div>

Methods {#serverheaders-methods}
--------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ServerHeaders">append(|name|, |value|)</dfn> method steps are:

<ol>
<li>[=header value/Normalize=] |value|.
<li>[=ServerHeaders/Validate=] |name| and |value| for [=this=].
<li>[=header list/Append=] (|name|, |value|) to [=this=]'s [=ServerHeaders/header list=].
</ol>
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ServerHeaders">delete(|name|)</dfn> method steps are:

<ol>
<li>[=ServerHeaders/Validate=] |name| and the empty [=byte sequence=] for [=this=].
<li>[=header list/Delete=] |name| from [=this=]'s [=ServerHeaders/header list=].
</ol>
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ServerHeaders">get(|name|)</dfn> method steps are:

<ol>
<li>If |name| is not a [=header name=], throw a {{TypeError}}.
<li>Return the result of [=header list/get|getting=] |name| from [=this=]'s [=ServerHeaders/header list=].
</ol>
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ServerHeaders">getSetCookie()</dfn> method steps are to return the [=header/value|values=] of all headers in [=this=]'s [=ServerHeaders/header list=] whose [=header/name=] is a [=byte-case-insensitive=] match for `` `Set-Cookie` ``, in order.
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ServerHeaders">has(|name|)</dfn> method steps are:

<ol>
<li>If |name| is not a [=header name=], throw a {{TypeError}}.
<li>Return true if [=this=]'s [=ServerHeaders/header list=] [=header list/contains=] |name|; otherwise false.
</ol>
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ServerHeaders">set(|name|, |value|)</dfn> method steps are:

<ol>
<li>[=header value/Normalize=] |value|.
<li>[=ServerHeaders/Validate=] |name| and |value| for [=this=].
<li>[=header list/Set=] (|name|, |value|) in [=this=]'s [=ServerHeaders/header list=].
</ol>
</div>

The [=value pairs to iterate over=] are the result of running [=header list/sort and combine=] with [=this=]'s [=ServerHeaders/header list=].

These are the steps of the Fetch Standard's {{Headers}} methods without the guard checks: a name that the Fetch Standard forbids in requests or responses is set, appended, and deleted like any other.

Trailer fields {#serverheaders-trailers}
----------------------------------------

Fields that a recipient needs before the content cannot be sent after it: those that describe message framing, routing, authentication, request modifiers, response controls, or content format ([RFC 9110 Section 6.5.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-6.5.1)). A <dfn>prohibited trailer name</dfn> is a [=header name=] that is a [=byte-case-insensitive=] match for one of the following:

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Category</th><th>Names</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Message framing</td><td>`Content-Length`, `Transfer-Encoding`, `Trailer`</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Connection-specific</td><td>`Connection`, `Keep-Alive`, `Proxy-Connection`, `TE`, `Upgrade`</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Routing</td><td>`Host`</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Authentication</td><td>`Authorization`, `Proxy-Authorization`, `WWW-Authenticate`, `Proxy-Authenticate`, `Cookie`, `Set-Cookie`</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Request modifiers</td><td>`Cache-Control`, `Expect`, `Max-Forwards`, `Pragma`, `Range`, `If-Match`, `If-None-Match`, `If-Modified-Since`, `If-Unmodified-Since`, `If-Range`</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Response controls</td><td>`Age`, `Expires`, `Date`, `Location`, `Retry-After`, `Vary`</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Content format</td><td>`Content-Encoding`, `Content-Type`, `Content-Range`</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Fields with a [=prohibited trailer name=] are removed from request trailers before the handler sees them ([[#serverrequest-trailers]]), and are not sent in response trailers ([[#serverheaders-outgoing]]). For response trailers, this applies in addition to the requirement that a trailer field be declared in the `Trailer` header field ([[#serverresponse-trailers]]).

Sending header fields {#serverheaders-outgoing}
-----------------------------------------------

The implementation sends header fields in the header section of a final or informational response, including the success response of {{ConnectContext/accept()}}, and in the trailer section of a response. In each case it applies the following rules. They change only what is sent; the {{ServerHeaders}} object is not modified.

*   **Connection-specific fields** &mdash; `Connection`, `Keep-Alive`, `Proxy-Connection`, `Transfer-Encoding`, and `Upgrade` ([RFC 9110 Section 7.6.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-7.6.1)) &mdash; belong to the implementation. On HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, which do not permit them ([RFC 9113 Section 8.2.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9113#section-8.2.2), [RFC 9114 Section 4.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114#section-4.2)), they are not sent. On HTTP/1.1, the implementation honors a `close` option in `Connection` by closing the connection after the response, and sends `Upgrade` along with the `upgrade` connection option ([RFC 9110 Section 7.8](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-7.8)), for example on a 426 (Upgrade Required) response. It determines the other connection-specific fields, and the message framing, itself.
*   **`Content-Length`** can be set by the handler. If the content that is sent does not match it, the implementation resets the stream ([[#errors-outbound]]); on HTTP/1.1, it closes the connection. This does not apply to a response to a HEAD request or to a 304 (Not Modified) response, which carry no content ([RFC 9110 Section 8.6](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-8.6)). `Content-Length` is not sent in an informational (1xx) or 204 (No Content) response, in which [RFC 9110 Section 8.6](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-8.6) forbids it, or in the success response of {{ConnectContext/accept()}} for CONNECT.
*   **Trailer fields and `TE`**: the implementation sends a trailer section whenever the connection can carry one ([[#http-versions-features]]), whether or not the request's `TE` header field includes `trailers`. A recipient that does not understand trailer fields can discard them ([RFC 9110 Section 6.5.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-6.5.1)); a handler that wants to depend on the client's support checks `TE` itself.
*   **`Date`**: if the header section of a final response has no `Date` field, the implementation adds one ([RFC 9110 Section 6.6.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-6.6.1)). A `Date` field that the handler sets is sent as given.
*   **Trailer fields** with a [=prohibited trailer name=] are not sent ([[#serverheaders-trailers]]).


ConnectContext {#sec-connectcontext}
====================================

Protocol identification {#connectcontext-protocol}
---------------------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ConnectContext">connectProtocol</dfn> attribute returns the value of the `:protocol` pseudo-header for extended CONNECT requests:

<ul>
<li>`"websocket"` &mdash; WebSocket over HTTP/2 or HTTP/3
<li>`"webtransport"` &mdash; WebTransport session
<li>`"connect-udp"` &mdash; UDP proxying
<li>`"connect-ip"` &mdash; IP proxying
<li>Any other registered or private-use protocol identifier
<li>`null` &mdash; Plain CONNECT (no `:protocol` pseudo-header; TCP tunneling)
</ul>

On an [=upgrade-originated=] exchange, it returns the protocol name of the first protocol offered in the `Upgrade` header field ([[#connectcontext-upgrade]]).
</div>

HTTP/1.1 upgrade normalization {#connectcontext-upgrade}
---------------------------------------------------------

HTTP/1.1 uses the `Upgrade` header field ([RFC 9110 Section 7.8](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-7.8)) rather than extended CONNECT. To provide a unified handler model, an implementation normalizes HTTP/1.1 upgrade requests into {{ConnectContext}} objects: when an HTTP/1.1 request is received with `Upgrade` and `Connection: Upgrade` header fields, the implementation constructs a {{ConnectContext}} with {{ConnectContext/connectProtocol}} set to the protocol-name of the first protocol in the `Upgrade` field value, without any protocol-version, [=ASCII lowercase|ASCII-lowercased=] (so `Upgrade: WebSocket` yields `"websocket"`, and `Upgrade: Foo/2, websocket` yields `"foo"`), and the {{ServerContext/request}} preserving the original method, URL, and header fields (normalized as for any request, [[#serverrequest-attributes]]). Such an exchange is <dfn export>upgrade-originated</dfn>. The full, preference-ordered list of offered protocols, with versions, remains available via the request's `Upgrade` header field. A handler that prefers a protocol other than the first can select it with {{ConnectAcceptInit/protocol}} ([[#connectcontext-accept]]); [RFC 9110 Section 7.8](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-7.8) lets the server ignore the client's order of preference.

An `Upgrade` header field in an HTTP/1.0 request is ignored, as [RFC 9110 Section 7.8](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-7.8) requires: such a request is never [=upgrade-originated=], and is routed to `fetch()` as an ordinary request.

For WebSocket (`GET` with `Upgrade: websocket`), this means WebSocket handling is in one place &mdash; the `connect()` handler &mdash; regardless of HTTP version.

An upgrade request can carry content, for example a `POST` with `Upgrade`. The client sends the complete request message before it switches protocols ([RFC 9110 Section 7.8](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-7.8)), so that content precedes any bytes of the new protocol. On an [=upgrade-originated=] exchange, the content is the body of {{ServerContext/request}}, and can be read before the upgrade is accepted ([[#serverrequest-body]]). When the handler accepts the upgrade, the implementation first receives the remainder of the content, discarding any of it that the handler has not read, and only then sends the `101 (Switching Protocols)` response; bytes that follow the content belong to the new protocol. Iteration of the request body that is still in progress at that point completes once the content has ended, and iteration that begins after the upgrade has been accepted completes at once. The [=pending 100 obligation=] is handled as described below.

On an [=upgrade-originated=] exchange, {{ConnectContext/accept()}} performs the HTTP/1.1 upgrade handshake: it sends a `101 (Switching Protocols)` response with an `Upgrade` header field naming the selected protocol (the protocol given by {{ConnectAcceptInit/protocol}}, or, if it is absent, the first protocol offered, with its protocol-version if one was offered), and the resulting {{Tunnel}}'s {{Tunnel/readable}} and {{Tunnel/writer}} carry the raw connection bytes. If a [=pending 100 obligation=] exists, the implementation first sends a `100 (Continue)` response and clears the obligation, whatever the value of {{ServerContext/autoContinue}}: [RFC 9110 Section 7.8](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-7.8) requires a `100 (Continue)` before a `101 (Switching Protocols)` when the request carried both `Upgrade` and `Expect: 100-continue`. The same applies to the handshakes performed by {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}} and {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocketStream()}}. As with extended CONNECT, the application never sends the `101` itself; the handshake is the implementation's concern ([[#servercontext-informational]]).

### Routing ### {#connectcontext-upgrade-routing}

*   If a `connect()` handler is provided, [=upgrade-originated=] exchanges are routed to it. The handler completes the upgrade ({{ConnectContext/accept()}}, {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}}, {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocketStream()}}), refuses it (a {{ServerResponse}}, or {{ServerContext/deny()}}), or declines only the upgrade offer via the [=fall-through sentinel=] ([[#connectcontext-fallthrough]]).
*   If no `connect()` handler is provided, the upgrade offer is ignored, as [RFC 9110 Section 7.8](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-7.8) permits: the request is routed to `fetch()` as an ordinary request. The `Upgrade` header remains visible there, but the handler cannot act on it.
*   An implementation MAY instead handle particular upgrade protocols itself, without routing them to the application.

Note: This differs from CONNECT and extended CONNECT, which have no ordinary-request interpretation; those are answered with 501 Not Implemented when no `connect()` handler is provided.

Accepting a tunnel {#connectcontext-accept}
-------------------------------------------

<div algorithm="accept(init)">
The <dfn method for="ConnectContext">accept(init)</dfn> method accepts the CONNECT request and establishes a [=tunnel=]. It returns a `Promise<Tunnel>` that resolves when the tunnel is established.

The optional |init| parameter allows setting header fields on the success response, through its {{ConnectAcceptInit/headers}} member, and, on an [=upgrade-originated=] exchange, selecting the protocol to switch to, through its <dfn dict-member for="ConnectAcceptInit">protocol</dfn> member. The steps are:

<ol>
<li>If this context is [=ServerContext/inert=] ([[#connectcontext-fallthrough]]), or the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/committed=], [=exchange/complete=], or [=exchange/denied=], return a promise rejected with an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>Let |fields| be the result of [=convert a headers init|converting=] |init|["{{ConnectAcceptInit/headers}}"] if it [=map/exists=], and « » otherwise. If this throws, return a promise rejected with the exception.
<li>If |init|["{{ConnectAcceptInit/protocol}}"] [=map/exists=]:
    <ol>
    <li>If the exchange is not [=upgrade-originated=], return a promise rejected with a {{TypeError}}. (Extended CONNECT names exactly one protocol, which the server cannot change.)
    <li>If |init|["{{ConnectAcceptInit/protocol}}"] is not an [=ASCII case-insensitive=] match for one of the protocols, with or without its protocol-version, offered in the request's `Upgrade` header field, return a promise rejected with a {{TypeError}}. A server must not switch to a protocol that the client did not offer ([RFC 9110 Section 7.8](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-7.8)).
    </ol>
<li>If the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/aborted=], return a promise rejected with the [=abort reason=].
<li>If {{ConnectContext/connectProtocol}} identifies a protocol that the implementation knows to use the Capsule Protocol, such as `"connect-udp"` ([[RFC9298]]) or `"connect-ip"` ([[RFC9484]]), and |fields| does not [=header list/contain=] `Capsule-Protocol`, [=header list/append=] (`Capsule-Protocol`, `?1`) to |fields|. A success response for these protocols must start the Capsule Protocol ([RFC 9297 Section 3.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9297#section-3.2)). For other protocols that use the Capsule Protocol, the application supplies the field in |init|["{{ConnectAcceptInit/headers}}"].
<li>Set the exchange state to [=exchange/committed=].
<li>Send the success response with the header fields in |fields|, subject to [[#serverheaders-outgoing]]: a `200 (OK)` response for CONNECT and extended CONNECT, or, on an [=upgrade-originated=] exchange, the HTTP/1.1 upgrade handshake ([[#connectcontext-upgrade]]).
<li>Return a promise that is resolved with a new {{Tunnel}} once the tunnel is established, or rejected with the [=abort reason=] if the exchange is aborted first.
</ol>

Accepting commits the [=exchange=]: subsequent calls to {{ConnectContext/accept()}}, the upgrade methods, or {{ServerContext/deny()}} throw (or reject with) an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}, per [[#exchange-states]]. {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}}, {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocketStream()}}, and {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebTransport()}} are committing operations subject to the same rules.

A {{ServerResponse}} cannot establish a tunnel. A client treats any 2xx response to CONNECT or extended CONNECT as establishing one ([RFC 9110 Section 9.3.6](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-9.3.6)), so a 2xx {{ServerResponse}} returned for such an exchange is an invalid return ([[#handler-errors]]). On an [=upgrade-originated=] exchange, which is an ordinary HTTP request, any {{ServerResponse}} is valid and declines the upgrade.
</div>

WebSocket upgrade {#connectcontext-websocket}
---------------------------------------------

<div algorithm="WebSocket server handshake">
{{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}} and {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocketStream()}} share the following steps. To <dfn>check a WebSocket handshake request</dfn> for a {{ConnectContext}} |ctx|, return true if |ctx|'s request is a valid opening handshake, and false otherwise:

*   On an [=upgrade-originated=] exchange, a valid opening handshake is as [RFC 6455 Section 4.2.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-4.2.1) defines it: a `GET` request whose `Upgrade` header field includes `websocket`, with a `Sec-WebSocket-Key` header field whose value, base64-decoded, is 16 bytes long, and a `Sec-WebSocket-Version` header field whose value is `13`.
*   On extended CONNECT, it is a request with a `Sec-WebSocket-Version` header field whose value is `13` ([RFC 8441 Section 5](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8441#section-5), [RFC 9220 Section 3](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9220#section-3)).

To <dfn>perform the WebSocket server handshake</dfn> for a {{ConnectContext}} |ctx| and a {{WebSocketUpgradeInit}} |options|:

<ol>
<li>Let |offered| be the list of subprotocols in the request's `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol` header fields, and let |protocol| be the empty string.
<li>If |options|["{{WebSocketUpgradeInit/protocol}}"] [=map/exists=], set |protocol| to its first item that is an item of |offered| (compared case-sensitively, as subprotocol names are), if there is one. The server's order of preference decides. If no item matches, no subprotocol is selected, and the response has no `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol` header field ([RFC 6455 Section 4.2.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-4.2.2)). An application that requires a subprotocol checks the request's header fields before upgrading, and refuses the request otherwise.
<li>Let |extensions| be the empty string. If the client offered the `permessage-deflate` extension ([[RFC7692]]), and |options|["{{WebSocketUpgradeInit/compression}}"] is true, or is absent and the implementation enables compression by default, the implementation accepts it with parameters of its choosing and sets |extensions| to the negotiated `Sec-WebSocket-Extensions` value. No other extension is negotiated unless the implementation defines it.
<li>If |ctx|'s exchange is [=upgrade-originated=] and a [=pending 100 obligation=] exists, send a `100 (Continue)` response and clear the obligation ([[#connectcontext-upgrade]]).
<li>Set the exchange state to [=exchange/committed=], and send the handshake response: on an [=upgrade-originated=] exchange, a `101 (Switching Protocols)` response with `Upgrade: websocket`, `Connection: Upgrade`, and the `Sec-WebSocket-Accept` value computed from `Sec-WebSocket-Key`; on extended CONNECT, a `200 (OK)` response. Either response carries `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol` if |protocol| is not the empty string, and `Sec-WebSocket-Extensions` if |extensions| is not the empty string. The WebSocket connection is now established ([RFC 6455 Section 4.2.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-4.2.2)).
<li>Return (|protocol|, |extensions|).
</ol>

On an established connection, the implementation answers each Ping frame with a Pong frame ([RFC 6455 Section 5.5.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-5.5.2)); applications do not see Ping or Pong frames. If |options|["{{WebSocketUpgradeInit/pingInterval}}"] [=map/exists=] and is not 0, the implementation also sends a Ping frame whenever that many milliseconds pass without any frame being received, to keep the connection alive through intermediaries and to detect a peer that is gone. Pings and pongs count as activity for {{TimeoutOptions/tunnelIdle}} ([[#serve-timeouts]]).
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ConnectContext">upgradeWebSocket(options)</dfn> method is a convenience for WebSocket tunnel establishment. It performs the WebSocket-specific handshake, configured by |options|, and returns a {{WebSocket}} object [[!WEBSOCKETS]] already in the `OPEN` state. The steps are:

<ol>
<li>If {{ConnectContext/connectProtocol}} is not `"websocket"`, throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>If this context is [=ServerContext/inert=] ([[#connectcontext-fallthrough]]), or the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/committed=], [=exchange/complete=], or [=exchange/denied=], throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>If the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/aborted=], return a {{WebSocket}} object whose connection closed abnormally: an `error` event and then a `close` event, with code 1006 and `wasClean` false, are fired at it, as for any WebSocket connection that closes without a closing handshake. This method does not throw in this case ([[#exchange-states]]).
<li>If [=check a WebSocket handshake request=] for this context returns false, throw a {{TypeError}}. The exchange is not committed, so the handler can still respond, for example with 400 (Bad Request), or with 426 (Upgrade Required) and `Sec-WebSocket-Version: 13` for an unsupported version.
<li>Let (|protocol|, |extensions|) be the result of [=perform the WebSocket server handshake|performing the WebSocket server handshake=] for this context and |options|.
<li>Return a new {{WebSocket}} object for the connection, in the `OPEN` state, whose `url` is the request's {{ServerRequest/url}} with its scheme replaced by `wss` if the connection is encrypted and `ws` otherwise, whose `protocol` is |protocol|, and whose `extensions` is |extensions|.
</ol>
</div>

The returned {{WebSocket}} behaves as [[!WEBSOCKETS]] defines for an established connection, with the server as the endpoint. Its events are fired by tasks queued on the WebSocket task source, so a handler that adds listeners in the same turn as it calls {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}} receives every message. Its `binaryType` is initially `"blob"`, as for any {{WebSocket}}. One difference applies: its `close(code, reason)` method validates `code` and `reason` with [=validate a server close code and reason=] ([[#websocketstream-close-codes]]) instead of the client rules, so that a server can send codes such as 1001 (Going Away) and 1011 (Internal Error).

Note: A {{WebSocket}} provides no backpressure. Received messages are dispatched as `message` events as they arrive, with no way to pause them, and {{WebSocket/send()}} queues data without waiting for the transport. {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocketStream()}} establishes the same connection with backpressure in both directions.

WebSocket stream upgrade {#connectcontext-websocketstream}
----------------------------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ConnectContext">upgradeWebSocketStream(options)</dfn> method establishes a WebSocket connection with the same handshake as {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}}, configured by |options| ([[#connectcontext-websocket]]), and returns a {{ServerWebSocketStream}} through which its messages are read and written as streams ([[#sec-serverwebsocketstream]]).

<ol>
<li>If {{ConnectContext/connectProtocol}} is not `"websocket"`, throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>If this context is [=ServerContext/inert=] ([[#connectcontext-fallthrough]]), or the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/committed=], [=exchange/complete=], or [=exchange/denied=], throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>Let |stream| be a new {{ServerWebSocketStream}}.
<li>If the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/aborted=], reject |stream|'s [=ServerWebSocketStream/opened promise=] and [=ServerWebSocketStream/closed promise=] with the [=abort reason=], and return |stream| ([[#exchange-states]]).
<li>If [=check a WebSocket handshake request=] for this context returns false, throw a {{TypeError}}, as {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}} does.
<li>Let (|protocol|, |extensions|) be the result of [=perform the WebSocket server handshake|performing the WebSocket server handshake=] for this context and |options|. Messages sent on the connection follow the handshake response.
<li>Set |stream|'s [=ServerWebSocketStream/readable stream=] and [=ServerWebSocketStream/writable stream=] to new streams for the connection ([[#websocketstream-messages]]).
<li>Resolve |stream|'s [=ServerWebSocketStream/opened promise=] with a {{WebSocketOpenInfo}} whose {{WebSocketOpenInfo/readable}} and {{WebSocketOpenInfo/writable}} members are |stream|'s [=ServerWebSocketStream/readable stream=] and [=ServerWebSocketStream/writable stream=], whose {{WebSocketOpenInfo/protocol}} member is |protocol|, and whose {{WebSocketOpenInfo/extensions}} member is |extensions|.
<li>Return |stream|.
</ol>
</div>

As with {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}}, the connection is established before the method returns: on the server side, the opening handshake needs no reply from the client. The {{ServerWebSocketStream/opened}} promise is therefore already settled when the {{ServerWebSocketStream}} is returned. It is kept so that code written against `WebSocketStream` [[WEBSOCKETSTREAM]], such as `const { readable, writable } = await wss.opened`, works unchanged.

WebTransport upgrade {#connectcontext-webtransport}
---------------------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ConnectContext">upgradeWebTransport()</dfn> method establishes a WebTransport session. It returns a `Promise<WebTransportSession>` that resolves when the session is established.

<ol>
<li>If {{ConnectContext/connectProtocol}} is not `"webtransport"`, return a promise rejected with an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>If this context is [=ServerContext/inert=] ([[#connectcontext-fallthrough]]), or the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/committed=], [=exchange/complete=], or [=exchange/denied=], return a promise rejected with an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>If the implementation does not support WebTransport, or the exchange is [=upgrade-originated=] (WebTransport is not defined over HTTP/1.1; see [[#http-versions-features]]), return a promise rejected with a `"NotSupportedError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>If the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/aborted=], return a promise rejected with the [=abort reason=].
<li>Set the exchange state to [=exchange/committed=], and send the success response that establishes the session.
<li>Return a promise that is resolved with a new {{WebTransportSession}} once the session is established, or rejected with the [=abort reason=] if the exchange is aborted first.
</ol>
</div>

Denying a tunnel {#connectcontext-deny}
---------------------------------------

A `connect()` handler can refuse a tunnel outright in two ways:

**Protocol-level denial** via {{ServerContext/deny()}} (inherited from {{ServerContext}}). This resets the stream without sending an HTTP response:

```js
async connect(ctx) {
  ctx.deny();  // REFUSED_STREAM — client may retry
  return;
}
```

**Application-level denial** by returning a {{ServerResponse}}. This sends a standard HTTP error response:

```js
async connect(ctx) {
  return new ServerResponse(null, { status: 403 });
}
```

The choice depends on the intended signal: {{ServerContext/deny()}} says "I never processed this" (protocol-level); a {{ServerResponse}} says "I processed this and the answer is no" (application-level).

On [=upgrade-originated=] exchanges there is a third option: declining only the upgrade offer while letting the request proceed as ordinary HTTP. See [[#connectcontext-fallthrough]].

Upgrade fall-through {#connectcontext-fallthrough}
--------------------------------------------------

Calling {{ServerContext/deny()}} with the <dfn export>fall-through sentinel</dfn> &mdash; `Symbol.for('wintertc.server.fallthrough')` &mdash; denies the [=tunnel=] without denying the carrying request. The upgrade offer is treated as ignored, and the request proceeds to `fetch()` as ordinary HTTP:

```js
async connect(ctx) {
  if (ctx.connectProtocol === 'websocket' && !wsEnabled) {
    ctx.deny(Symbol.for('wintertc.server.fallthrough'));
    return;  // fetch() takes it from here
  }
  const ws = ctx.upgradeWebSocket();
  // ...
}
```

The sentinel is a registered symbol following the [[SYMBOL-NAMING]] convention, the same pattern as the [=protocol marker=]: it costs no allocation, cannot collide with an application's error objects, and is not an [=error code=] &mdash; nothing is sent on the wire.

The sentinel is valid only on [=upgrade-originated=] exchanges. An HTTP/1.1 request bearing `Upgrade` is an ordinary request carrying an opportunistic offer that a server may ignore ([RFC 9110 Section 7.8](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-7.8)); CONNECT and extended CONNECT requests have no such interpretation &mdash; the request *is* the tunnel &mdash; so the sentinel on those exchanges throws an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.

A {{ServerContext}} has an <dfn for="ServerContext">inert</dfn> flag, initially unset, which is set on a {{ConnectContext}} by {{ServerContext/deny()}} with the sentinel. The flag belongs to the context, not to the [=exchange=], whose state is unchanged. While it is set:

*   {{ConnectContext/accept()}}, the upgrade methods, {{ServerContext/sendInformational()}}, and {{ServerContext/deny()}} throw (or, for promise-returning methods, reject with) an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
*   {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} works as on any context, and {{ServerContext/completed}} returns the same value as on the {{ServerContext}} that `fetch()` receives: both contexts belong to the same exchange.
*   Assignments to {{ServerContext/serverPriority}} and {{ServerContext/onpriority}} have no effect.

After the `connect()` invocation settles with `undefined`, the implementation invokes `fetch()` with a fresh {{ServerContext}} for the same [=exchange=], unless the exchange has been [=exchange/aborted=] in the meantime, in which case `fetch()` is not invoked:

*   Nothing has been sent and the exchange is still [=exchange/open=], so the `fetch()` handler retains its full capabilities &mdash; including {{ServerContext/deny()}}, honestly, since no processing is visible to the client.
*   The {{ServerContext/request}} is the same {{ServerRequest}}, `Upgrade` header intact.
*   {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} registrations and any [=pending 100 obligation=] carry over.
*   `fetch()` has no fall-through of its own; the flow terminates there.

`fetch()` is not invoked until the `connect()` invocation settles, so it is in the application's interest to return promptly after calling {{ServerContext/deny()}} with the sentinel &mdash; {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} is the tool for anything slow. If the invocation rejects, or resolves with anything other than `undefined`, the fault takes precedence: the exchange is still [=response-capable=], so the standard fault path applies ([[#handler-errors]]) and `fetch()` is not invoked. The `error()` handler receives the [=ServerContext/inert=] context: it can return a {{ServerResponse}}, but cannot call {{ServerContext/deny()}} or accept the upgrade.


Tunnel {#sec-tunnel}
====================

A {{Tunnel}} represents an established [=tunnel=] through an HTTP connection. It provides three layers of communication corresponding to the layers defined in the [Capsule Protocol](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9297):

1.  Raw data stream: Bidirectional byte stream on the CONNECT data channel.
2.  Capsule Protocol: Typed TLV-framed messages for control signaling.
3.  HTTP Datagrams: Discrete messages that may be unreliable on HTTP/3.

Raw data stream {#tunnel-raw}
-----------------------------

The {{Tunnel/readable}} and {{Tunnel/writer}} attributes provide direct access to the CONNECT data stream as iterable streams [[!ITER-STREAMS]]: {{Tunnel/readable}} is a {{ByteReadableStream}} of the bytes received from the peer, and {{Tunnel/writer}} is a {{Writer}} for the bytes sent to the peer.

Iterating {{Tunnel/readable}} yields batches of one or more {{Uint8Array}} chunks as data arrives, and iteration completes when the peer ends its direction of the tunnel ([[#tunnel-closure]]). The data can be read once: the first async iterator obtained from {{Tunnel/readable}} is the tunnel's reader, and `next()` on any later iterator returns a promise rejected with a {{TypeError}}. Ending iteration early (the iterator's `return()` method, called for example on `break`) terminates the tunnel abnormally ([[#tunnel-closure]]).

Note: Ending iteration of a request body early refuses only the rest of the body ([[#serverrequest-body]]), but ending iteration of {{Tunnel/readable}} early terminates the tunnel. The difference is deliberate. A tunnel over HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2 is carried by a TCP connection or by a stream within one, and neither can tell the peer to stop sending in one direction while the other stays open. (HTTP/3 has `STOP_SENDING`, but behavior that only some versions could provide would not be version-transparent.) Silently discarding the data that keeps arriving would leave the peer sending into a void with no indication. Terminating the tunnel is the only behavior that is both portable and visible to the peer. An application that wants to keep sending after it stops reading can keep iterating and discard what it receives.

Bytes written to {{Tunnel/writer}} are sent in order. Its `end()` method ends the tunnel's send direction (a local half-close), and its returned promise resolves with the total number of bytes written once they have all been sent. Its `fail()` method terminates the tunnel abnormally ([[#tunnel-closure]]). The writer follows the `"strict"` backpressure policy of [[ITER-STREAMS]]; its byte budget is implementation-defined and may follow the transport's flow-control window, as that specification permits. The writer implements the drainable protocol, so `Stream.ondrain()` can be used to wait for capacity.

For a plain CONNECT tunnel (no `:protocol`), the raw data stream carries the tunneled TCP payload. For WebSocket, the raw data stream carries WebSocket frames.

A {{Tunnel}} has a <dfn for="Tunnel">data stream mode</dfn>, which is "`unclaimed`", "`raw`", or "`capsules`", initially "`unclaimed`". It records whether the data stream is being consumed as bytes or as capsules, which are mutually exclusive:

*   The mode becomes "`raw`" when an async iterator is first obtained from {{Tunnel/readable}}, or when a method of {{Tunnel/writer}} is first called, while the mode is "`unclaimed`".
*   The mode becomes "`capsules`" when {{Tunnel/capsules()}} is called, or when {{Tunnel/datagrams()}} is called and datagrams are carried as DATAGRAM capsules ([[#tunnel-datagrams]]), while the mode is "`unclaimed`".
*   While the mode is "`capsules`", getting {{Tunnel/readable}} or {{Tunnel/writer}} throws an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.

{{Tunnel/capsules()}} and {{Tunnel/datagrams()}} are available only for protocols that may use the Capsule Protocol. On a plain CONNECT tunnel (one whose {{ConnectContext/connectProtocol}} is `null`), whose data stream is opaque TCP payload, and on a WebSocket connection established with {{ConnectContext/accept()}}, whose data stream is WebSocket frames, both methods throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.

Capsule Protocol {#tunnel-capsules}
-----------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="Tunnel">capsules()</dfn> method returns a {{CapsuleStream}} providing typed access to the Capsule Protocol on the CONNECT data stream. The steps are:

<ol>
<li>If the tunnel is a plain CONNECT tunnel or a WebSocket connection ([[#tunnel-raw]]), throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>If this's [=Tunnel/data stream mode=] is "`raw`", throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}: the data stream has already been consumed as bytes, and parsing capsules from the middle of it would be meaningless.
<li>If {{Tunnel/capsules()}} has been called before on this tunnel, throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>Set this's [=Tunnel/data stream mode=] to "`capsules`", and return a new {{CapsuleStream}} for the tunnel.
</ol>

While the [=Tunnel/data stream mode=] is "`capsules`", the implementation parses the data stream into capsules. DATAGRAM capsules (type `0x00`) are not delivered through the {{CapsuleStream}}: they are delivered to the readable of {{Tunnel/datagrams()}} ([[#tunnel-datagrams]]), and DATAGRAM capsules received before {{Tunnel/datagrams()}} is first called are discarded. Every other capsule is delivered to {{CapsuleStream/readable}}, and capsules received before {{Tunnel/capsules()}} is called (because {{Tunnel/datagrams()}} put the data stream in "`capsules`" mode first) are buffered for it.
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="CapsuleStream">readable</dfn> attribute of a {{CapsuleStream}} is an object conforming to the `AsyncIterable<Capsule>` protocol. Iterating it yields each capsule received from the peer as a {{Capsule}}, one per iteration step, and iteration completes when the peer ends its direction of the tunnel. It follows the rules of {{Tunnel/readable}} ([[#tunnel-raw]]): the capsules can be read once, and ending iteration early terminates the tunnel abnormally.
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="CapsuleStream">send(capsule)</dfn> method sends |capsule| on the CONNECT data stream, after any capsule previously passed to {{CapsuleStream/send()}}. It returns a promise that resolves once |capsule| has been accepted by the transport, so awaiting each call applies backpressure. The promise rejects with a {{TypeError}} if |capsule|'s {{Capsule/type}} is `0x00` (DATAGRAM), which is sent through {{Tunnel/datagrams()}} instead; if the send direction has ended ({{Tunnel/close()}}), and with the error that terminated the tunnel if it has terminated abnormally.
</div>

```js
const capsules = tunnel.capsules();

// Reading capsules
for await (const capsule of capsules.readable) {
  console.log(capsule.type, capsule.data);
}

// Sending capsules
await capsules.send({ type: 0xff37a2, data: new Uint8Array([...]) });
```

HTTP Datagrams {#tunnel-datagrams}
----------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="Tunnel">datagrams()</dfn> method returns a {{DatagramStream}} providing access to HTTP Datagrams associated with this [=tunnel=]. The steps are:

<ol>
<li>If the tunnel is a plain CONNECT tunnel or a WebSocket connection ([[#tunnel-raw]]), throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>If the implementation does not support HTTP Datagrams, or the peer has not negotiated their use on this connection, throw a `"NotSupportedError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>If datagrams are carried as DATAGRAM capsules on this tunnel (see below):
    <ol>
    <li>If this's [=Tunnel/data stream mode=] is "`raw`", throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
    <li>Set this's [=Tunnel/data stream mode=] to "`capsules`".
    </ol>
<li>Return a new {{DatagramStream}} whose {{DatagramStream/readable}} and {{DatagramStream/writer}} are the tunnel's datagram readable and datagram writer, created on the first call and the same on every call; whose {{DatagramStream/unreliable}} member is as described below; and whose {{DatagramStream/maxDatagramSize}} member is the largest datagram payload that can currently be sent.
</ol>

The method is idempotent in effect: each call returns a new dictionary object containing the same underlying {{DatagramStream/readable}} and {{DatagramStream/writer}} objects. Because the largest sendable payload can change during the life of a tunnel, for example as the QUIC path MTU is discovered, calling {{Tunnel/datagrams()}} again gives its current value.

Each chunk of {{DatagramStream/readable}} is the HTTP Datagram Payload of one received datagram ([RFC 9297 Section 2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9297#section-2)), and each chunk written to {{DatagramStream/writer}} is sent as the HTTP Datagram Payload of one datagram. The payload is passed through without interpretation: for `connect-udp` and `connect-ip`, it begins with the Context ID ([RFC 9298 Section 5](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9298#section-5), [RFC 9484 Section 6](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9484#section-6)), which the application reads and writes.

Each chunk of {{DatagramStream/readable}} is one received datagram payload; a batch can hold several. The datagrams can be read once, as for {{Tunnel/readable}} ([[#tunnel-raw]]). Ending iteration early discards the datagrams that arrive afterward and does not affect the tunnel.

{{DatagramStream/writer}} sends each chunk written to it as one datagram: `write()` sends one datagram, and `writev()` sends one per chunk. Like {{Tunnel/writer}}, it follows the `"strict"` backpressure policy with an implementation-defined byte budget. A chunk larger than the largest payload that can be sent at the time is dropped: the write resolves, and nothing is sent, as for any datagram that the network loses. Its `end()` method stops local sends without any wire signal, and its `fail()` method errors the writer without affecting the tunnel. The byte count with which `end()` resolves counts the bytes written, including those of dropped datagrams; it does not report delivery.

Note: {{DatagramStream/readable}} is a {{ByteReadableStream}} so that it can be read with the same tools as other byte data, but datagram boundaries are significant. Iterating it directly yields each datagram as a separate chunk. A consumer or transform that treats it as a byte stream, concatenating or re-chunking its contents, loses those boundaries.

On HTTP/3 connections where QUIC DATAGRAM frames are available, datagrams are sent and received as unreliable QUIC DATAGRAM frames. The {{DatagramStream/unreliable}} member is `true`.

On HTTP/2 or HTTP/1.1 connections, datagrams are sent as DATAGRAM capsules (type `0x00`) on the data stream. The {{DatagramStream/unreliable}} member is `false`. Delivery is reliable (TCP guarantees it), but the API is the same.
</div>

Both [CONNECT-UDP](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9298) and [CONNECT-IP](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9484) use HTTP Datagrams for their data plane. The {{Tunnel/datagrams()}} API provides the foundation for both.

Closure {#tunnel-closure}
-------------------------

Tunnel closure is a property of the tunnel's two directions. Each direction completes independently, and the tunnel closes gracefully when both have completed &mdash; no call to {{Tunnel/close()}} is required.

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Event</th><th>Tunnel effect</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>`end()` is called on {{Tunnel/writer}}, or {{Tunnel/close()}} is called</td>
      <td>Local half-close: pending data is flushed, then the end of the send direction is signaled (END_STREAM on HTTP/2, stream FIN on HTTP/3, TCP half-close for HTTP/1.1 CONNECT)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>The peer half-closes its send direction</td>
      <td>Remote half-close: iteration of {{Tunnel/readable}} completes after delivering any remaining data</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Both directions have completed</td>
      <td>The tunnel is closed; {{Tunnel/closed}} resolves</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>`fail()` is called on {{Tunnel/writer}}, or iteration of {{Tunnel/readable}} ends early</td>
      <td>Abnormal termination: the stream is reset carrying the [=error code=] mapped from the fail reason, defaulting to `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR` ([[#errors-outbound]]); ending iteration early carries no reason, so it always uses the default. The other of {{Tunnel/readable}} and {{Tunnel/writer}} errors; {{Tunnel/closed}} rejects</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>The peer resets the stream, or the connection is lost</td>
      <td>Abnormal termination: iteration of {{Tunnel/readable}} rejects, and {{Tunnel/writer}} errors, with the [=protocol error=] ([[#errors-inbound]]); {{Tunnel/closed}} rejects</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Closure is wire-level, not consumption-level: the tunnel is closed once its send direction has been flushed and ended and the peer's end-of-stream has been received. {{Tunnel/closed}} can therefore resolve before all the data received on {{Tunnel/readable}} has been read; the application may continue iterating after closure.

Half-closes are graceful; aborts are terminal. A lone half-close leaves the opposite direction fully operational &mdash; a tunnel can finish sending while continuing to receive, and vice versa. Failing {{Tunnel/writer}}, ending iteration of {{Tunnel/readable}} early, or calling {{Tunnel/destroy()}} terminates the entire tunnel.

When the [=Tunnel/data stream mode=] is "`capsules`" ([[#tunnel-raw]]), the {{CapsuleStream}} carries these semantics in place of {{Tunnel/readable}} and {{Tunnel/writer}}: its {{CapsuleStream/readable}} takes the place of {{Tunnel/readable}}, and {{Tunnel/close()}} ends the send direction after the last capsule passed to {{CapsuleStream/send()}}. {{DatagramStream}} streams do not participate in closure: datagrams have no end-of-stream concept, ending a datagram writer stops local sends without any wire signal, and tunnel closure ends (or errors) the datagram readable and writer rather than the reverse.

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="Tunnel">close()</dfn> method closes the [=tunnel=] gracefully: it ends the send direction (a local half-close), as `end()` on {{Tunnel/writer}} does, or, when the [=Tunnel/data stream mode=] is "`capsules`", once every capsule passed to {{CapsuleStream/send()}} has been sent. It does not end iteration of {{Tunnel/readable}} &mdash; graceful closure does not discard the peer's in-flight data. It returns a promise that settles as {{Tunnel/closed}} does: fulfilled when the peer has also completed its direction and the tunnel is closed, or rejected if the tunnel terminates abnormally. An application that does not want the remaining inbound data can end iteration of {{Tunnel/readable}} instead, at the cost of abnormal termination. A peer that never completes its direction is subject to the {{TimeoutOptions/tunnelIdle}} timeout ([[#serve-timeouts]]).
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="Tunnel">destroy(error)</dfn> method immediately terminates the [=tunnel=] without draining:

<ol>
<li>If the tunnel is already closed or has already terminated abnormally, return.
<li>Reset the stream, carrying the [=error code=] mapped from |error| per the tunnel abort mechanism ([[#errors-outbound]]); when |error| is omitted or carries no sendable code, `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR` is used.
<li>Error {{Tunnel/readable}} and {{Tunnel/writer}} (or, after {{Tunnel/capsules()}}, the {{CapsuleStream}}) and the {{DatagramStream}} readable and writer; pending iteration steps, writes, and sends reject.
<li>Reject {{Tunnel/closed}} with |error| if provided, or with a [=protocol error=] whose code is `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR` otherwise.
</ol>

{{Tunnel/destroy()}} operates on the tunnel rather than through its streams: it remains effective while {{Tunnel/readable}} is being iterated or {{Tunnel/writer}} is being written to (e.g., mid-pipe), and after {{Tunnel/capsules()}} has superseded them. Called while a graceful {{Tunnel/close()}} is pending, it short-circuits the drain &mdash; destroy wins.
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="Tunnel">closed</dfn> attribute returns a promise that resolves when the tunnel is closed cleanly, and rejects with a [=protocol error=] when the tunnel terminates abnormally &mdash; for example, the peer reset the stream or the connection was lost (see [[#errors-inbound]]). It returns the same promise every time, and the promise is always [=mark as handled|marked as handled=].
</div>

<div algorithm="Tunnel async disposal">
{{Tunnel}} supports `Symbol.asyncDispose`, enabling `await using` declarations. The disposal steps are:

<ol>
<li>If the tunnel is already closed or has already terminated abnormally, return a promise resolved with `undefined`. Disposal does not re-surface an error the application has already had the opportunity to observe via {{Tunnel/closed}}.
<li>Otherwise, return the result of calling {{Tunnel/close()}}: a promise that settles as closure completes, fulfilled on clean closure or rejected if the tunnel terminates abnormally while closing.
</ol>
</div>

```js
async connect(ctx) {
  if (ctx.connectProtocol !== 'connect-udp') {
    return new ServerResponse(null, { status: 501 });
  }
  await using tunnel = await ctx.accept();
  await proxyUdpPayloads(tunnel.datagrams());
  // Scope exit closes the tunnel and awaits closure —
  // including on early return or throw.
}
```

Note: There is no `Symbol.dispose`: tunnel closure is inherently asynchronous, so {{Tunnel}} supports `await using` but not synchronous `using`.


ServerWebSocketStream {#sec-serverwebsocketstream}
==================================================

A {{ServerWebSocketStream}} represents an established WebSocket connection ([RFC 6455](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455)) whose messages are read and written as streams. It is obtained via {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocketStream()}}, whether the connection was requested by an HTTP/1.1 upgrade or by extended CONNECT ([[#connectcontext-upgrade]]):

```js
async connect(ctx) {
  if (ctx.connectProtocol !== 'websocket') {
    return new ServerResponse(null, { status: 501 });
  }
  await using wss = ctx.upgradeWebSocketStream({ protocol: ['chat.v1'] });
  const { readable, writable } = await wss.opened;
  const writer = writable.getWriter();
  for await (const message of readable) {
    // The next message is not read until this one has been handled,
    // and each write waits while the client is not keeping up.
    await writer.write(await handle(message));
  }
  // Scope exit closes the connection and awaits closure.
}
```

A {{ServerWebSocketStream}} has:

*   an <dfn for="ServerWebSocketStream">opened promise</dfn>, a promise;
*   a <dfn for="ServerWebSocketStream">closed promise</dfn>, a promise;
*   a <dfn for="ServerWebSocketStream">readable stream</dfn>, a {{ReadableStream}} or null, initially null; and
*   a <dfn for="ServerWebSocketStream">writable stream</dfn>, a {{WritableStream}} or null, initially null.

Both promises are always [=mark as handled|marked as handled=], so a rejection the application never observes is not reported as unhandled. The streams are null only when the [=exchange=] was [=exchange/aborted=] before {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocketStream()}} was called.

Opening {#websocketstream-opened}
---------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerWebSocketStream">opened</dfn> attribute returns [=this=]'s [=ServerWebSocketStream/opened promise=]. The promise is settled before the {{ServerWebSocketStream}} is returned: fulfilled with the {{WebSocketOpenInfo}} for the connection, or rejected with the [=abort reason=] if the [=exchange=] was [=exchange/aborted=] ([[#connectcontext-websocketstream]]).
</div>

Messages {#websocketstream-messages}
------------------------------------

The [=ServerWebSocketStream/readable stream=] delivers the messages received from the peer. Each chunk is one message: a string for a text message, and a {{Uint8Array}} for a binary message. The stream is pull-based: the implementation enqueues a message only when the stream requests one. While messages are not being read, the implementation stops reading from the transport once it has buffered an implementation-defined amount, so that the flow control of the underlying connection (TCP, or the HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 stream) slows the peer. Backpressure applies between messages: each message is delivered whole, so an implementation MAY limit the size of a received message, failing the WebSocket connection with close code 1009 (Message Too Big) when a message exceeds it ([RFC 6455 Section 10.4](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-10.4)). Cancelling the stream starts the closing handshake ([[#websocketstream-closure]]).

The [=ServerWebSocketStream/writable stream=] sends messages to the peer. Each chunk written is sent as one message: a {{BufferSource}} as a binary message containing a copy of its bytes, and any other value, converted to a string, as a text message; if the conversion throws, the write rejects with the exception. A write waits until the implementation can accept the message, then sends it and resolves. The amount of data the implementation accepts before writes wait is implementation-defined and may follow the transport's flow-control window, as for a [=tunnel=]'s {{Tunnel/writer}} ([[#tunnel-raw]]), so the stream's backpressure &mdash; observable, for example, through the `ready` promise of a writer &mdash; reflects the peer's progress. A message written after the closing handshake has started is not sent, and its write rejects with an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}. Closing or aborting the stream starts the closing handshake ([[#websocketstream-closure]]).

Note: These are the message semantics of `WebSocketStream` [[WEBSOCKETSTREAM]]. A {{WebSocket}} cannot express them: it dispatches each message as it arrives, whether or not the application is ready for it.

Closure {#websocketstream-closure}
----------------------------------

A WebSocket connection closes through the closing handshake of [RFC 6455 Section 7](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-7): each endpoint sends a Close frame, which may carry a close code and a reason. Once the server has both sent and received a Close frame, the closing handshake is complete and the implementation closes the transport: on HTTP/1.1 it closes the connection, and on HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 it ends the stream, which is how those versions represent an orderly TCP-level close ([RFC 8441 Section 5](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8441#section-5), [RFC 9220 Section 3](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9220#section-3)). Closure is clean if the closing handshake completed, and abnormal otherwise.

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Event</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>{{ServerWebSocketStream/close()}} is called, or the [=ServerWebSocketStream/writable stream=] is closed</td>
      <td>The closing handshake starts: a Close frame is sent, carrying the close code and reason given to {{ServerWebSocketStream/close()}}, or no close code when the writable stream is closed</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>The [=ServerWebSocketStream/readable stream=] is cancelled, or the [=ServerWebSocketStream/writable stream=] is aborted</td>
      <td>The closing handshake starts, with the close code and reason carried by the cancel or abort reason, if any (see below)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>The peer sends a Close frame</td>
      <td>The closing handshake starts, if it has not already: the implementation sends a Close frame in response ([RFC 6455 Section 5.5.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-5.5.1)). The messages received before the peer's Close frame can still be read</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>The closing handshake completes and the transport is closed</td>
      <td>Clean closure: the readable stream closes, after any messages already received have been read; the writable stream errors with an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}; {{ServerWebSocketStream/closed}} fulfills with the peer's close code and reason</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>The peer resets the stream, or the connection is lost</td>
      <td>Abnormal closure: the readable and writable streams error, and {{ServerWebSocketStream/closed}} rejects, with the [=protocol error=] ([[#errors-inbound]])</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>The transport is closed before the closing handshake completes, or the implementation fails the WebSocket connection ([RFC 6455 Section 7.1.7](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-7.1.7)) &mdash; for example, because the peer violated the protocol</td>
      <td>Abnormal closure, as above, with a [=protocol error=] whose code is `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR`</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{ServerWebSocketStream/destroy()}} is called</td>
      <td>Abnormal closure without a closing handshake: the stream is reset ([[#errors-outbound]])</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ServerWebSocketStream">close(closeInfo)</dfn> method starts the closing handshake:

<ol>
<li>Let |code| be |closeInfo|["{{WebSocketCloseInfo/closeCode}}"] if it [=map/exists=], and null otherwise.
<li>Let |reason| be |closeInfo|["{{WebSocketCloseInfo/reason}}"].
<li>[=Validate a server close code and reason=] given |code| and |reason|.
<li>If the closing handshake has started, or the connection is closed, return.
<li>If |reason| is not the empty string and |code| is null, set |code| to 1000 (Normal Closure).
<li>Start the closing handshake ([RFC 6455 Section 7.1.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-7.1.2)) by sending a Close frame after the messages already sent. If |code| is null, the Close frame has no body. Otherwise its status code is |code|, followed by the [=UTF-8 encode|UTF-8 encoding=] of |reason|.
</ol>
</div>

Messages written to the [=ServerWebSocketStream/writable stream=] but not yet sent when {{ServerWebSocketStream/close()}} is called are discarded, and their writes reject with an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.

Note: Rejecting these writes differs from `WebSocketStream` [[WEBSOCKETSTREAM]], where they resolve. On a server, a message that silently fails to reach a client (a notification, a result) is a correctness problem, so the writer is told. To close after every written message has been sent, close the writable stream instead: its close takes effect after the writes queued before it.

<div algorithm="ServerWebSocketStream cancel and abort">
When the [=ServerWebSocketStream/readable stream=] is cancelled, or the [=ServerWebSocketStream/writable stream=] is aborted, with |reason|, the closing handshake starts as if {{ServerWebSocketStream/close()}} had been called with the result of converting |reason| to a {{WebSocketCloseInfo}}. If the conversion throws, or the {{ServerWebSocketStream/close()}} steps would throw, the Close frame has no body instead. A `WebSocketError` [[WEBSOCKETSTREAM]], or any object with `closeCode` and `reason` properties, therefore supplies the close code and reason; a reason without them, such as an `Error`, supplies none.
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerWebSocketStream">closed</dfn> attribute returns [=this=]'s [=ServerWebSocketStream/closed promise=]. On clean closure, the promise is fulfilled with a {{WebSocketCloseInfo}} whose {{WebSocketCloseInfo/closeCode}} is the status code of the first Close frame received from the peer, or 1005 (No Status Rcvd) if that frame had none, and whose {{WebSocketCloseInfo/reason}} is that frame's reason, decoded as UTF-8 ([RFC 6455 Sections 7.1.5 and 7.1.6](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-7.1.5)). On abnormal closure, it is rejected with the error given in the table above.
</div>

A peer that never completes the closing handshake is subject to the {{TimeoutOptions/closeHandshake}} timeout ([[#serve-timeouts]]): when it expires, the implementation closes the transport, and closure is abnormal, with a [=protocol error=] whose code is `ERR_HTTP_TIMEOUT`. The same applies to a {{WebSocket}} returned by {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}}, which then closes with code 1006.

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ServerWebSocketStream">destroy(error)</dfn> method immediately terminates the WebSocket connection, without a closing handshake:

<ol>
<li>If the connection is already closed, cleanly or abnormally, return.
<li>Reset the stream, carrying the [=error code=] mapped from |error| per the tunnel abort mechanism ([[#errors-outbound]]); when |error| is omitted or carries no sendable code, `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED` is used. On HTTP/1.1, the connection is closed.
<li>Let |reason| be |error| if it was given, and a [=protocol error=] whose code is `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED` otherwise.
<li>Error the [=ServerWebSocketStream/readable stream=] and [=ServerWebSocketStream/writable stream=] with |reason|; pending reads and writes reject.
<li>Reject the [=ServerWebSocketStream/closed promise=] with |reason|.
</ol>

The default is `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED` rather than the `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR` of {{Tunnel/destroy()|Tunnel.destroy()}} because it is the signal that [RFC 8441 Section 5](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8441#section-5) and [RFC 9220 Section 3](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9220#section-3) assign to the abrupt closure of a WebSocket connection: RST_STREAM with CANCEL on HTTP/2, and H3_REQUEST_CANCELLED on HTTP/3. Like {{Tunnel/destroy()|Tunnel.destroy()}}, {{ServerWebSocketStream/destroy()|ServerWebSocketStream.destroy()}} operates on the connection rather than through its streams: it remains effective while the streams are locked, and it short-circuits a closing handshake in progress.
</div>

{{ServerWebSocketStream}} supports `Symbol.asyncDispose`, enabling `await using` declarations, with the same disposal steps as {{Tunnel}}: if the connection is already closed, cleanly or abnormally, the disposal promise resolves with `undefined`; otherwise {{ServerWebSocketStream/close()}} is called with no arguments, and the promise settles as closure completes &mdash; fulfilled on clean closure, rejected if closure turns abnormal. As with {{Tunnel}}, there is no `Symbol.dispose`.

Close codes {#websocketstream-close-codes}
------------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>validate a server close code and reason</dfn> given |code| and |reason|:

<ol>
<li>If |code| is not null and is not in one of the ranges 1000&ndash;1003, 1007&ndash;1009, 1011&ndash;1014, or 3000&ndash;4999, inclusive, throw an `"InvalidAccessError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>If the [=UTF-8 encode|UTF-8 encoding=] of |reason| is longer than 123 bytes, throw a `"SyntaxError"` {{DOMException}}.
</ol>
</div>

These are the close codes that a server can send: those defined by [RFC 6455 Section 7.4](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-7.4) or registered in the [IANA WebSocket Close Code Number Registry](https://www.iana.org/assignments/websocket/websocket.xhtml#close-code-number), together with the ranges reserved for libraries, frameworks, applications, and private use. The codes excluded are reserved (1004); never sent in a Close frame (1005, 1006, and 1015); sent only by clients (1010); or reserved for future definition (1016&ndash;2999). The 123-byte limit keeps the Close frame's payload within the 125 bytes that a control frame allows.

Note: This deliberately differs from `WebSocketStream` [[WEBSOCKETSTREAM]] and {{WebSocket}}, which accept only 1000 and 3000&ndash;4999 from script. On a server, the application is the endpoint, and codes such as 1001 (Going Away, for example on shutdown), 1008 (Policy Violation), 1009 (Message Too Big), 1011 (Internal Error), 1012 (Service Restart), and 1013 (Try Again Later) are its to send.


WebTransport {#webtransport}
============================

WebTransport provides multiplexed streams and unreliable datagrams over an HTTP connection. On HTTP/3, it uses native QUIC streams and QUIC DATAGRAM frames. On HTTP/2, it falls back to the Capsule Protocol.

Session establishment {#webtransport-session}
---------------------------------------------

A {{WebTransportSession}} is obtained via {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebTransport()}}:

```js
async connect(ctx) {
  if (ctx.connectProtocol === 'webtransport') {
    const session = await ctx.upgradeWebTransport();

    // Accept incoming bidirectional streams
    for await (const stream of session.incomingBidirectionalStreams) {
      handleStream(stream);  // { readable, writable }
    }
  }
}
```

Streams {#webtransport-streams}
-------------------------------

A {{WebTransportSession}} can carry multiple independent streams, using the stream types defined by [[WEBTRANSPORT]]:

*   Bidirectional streams: Opened by either side. Each is a {{WebTransportBidirectionalStream}} whose `readable` is a {{WebTransportReceiveStream}} and whose `writable` is a {{WebTransportSendStream}}.
*   Unidirectional streams: Opened by one side, read by the other, as a {{WebTransportSendStream}} or {{WebTransportReceiveStream}} respectively.

On HTTP/3, each WebTransport stream maps to a native QUIC stream. Streams are independent: a slow stream does not block others.

On HTTP/2, streams are multiplexed over the single CONNECT data stream using the Capsule Protocol. Head-of-line blocking applies (TCP guarantees ordering).

Datagrams {#webtransport-datagrams}
-----------------------------------

The {{WebTransportSession/datagrams}} attribute is a {{WebTransportDatagramDuplexStream}} as defined by [[WEBTRANSPORT]]: incoming datagrams arrive on its `readable`, and outgoing datagram writables are created with `createWritable()`.

Note: This deliberately differs from a [=tunnel=]'s {{Tunnel/datagrams()}}, which returns the simpler {{DatagramStream}}. The WebTransport datagram surface carries session-level controls (maximum datagram size, expiration ages, buffer depths) and send-ordering machinery that [[WEBTRANSPORT]] defines and this specification does not duplicate.

Reliability {#webtransport-reliability}
---------------------------------------

The {{WebTransportSession/reliability}} attribute (a {{WebTransportReliabilityMode}}, per [[WEBTRANSPORT]]) reflects the underlying transport mechanism:

*   `"supports-unreliable"`: Native QUIC streams and QUIC DATAGRAM frames (HTTP/3). No head-of-line blocking between streams. Datagrams may be dropped.
*   `"reliable-only"`: Capsule Protocol over a single HTTP/2 data stream. Head-of-line blocking applies. Datagrams are delivered reliably as DATAGRAM capsules.

Because {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebTransport()}} resolves only once the session is established, the `"pending"` value is never observed on a {{WebTransportSession}}.

Lifecycle {#webtransport-lifecycle}
-----------------------------------

Two [=server-side extensions=] supplement the [[WEBTRANSPORT]] lifecycle members, mirroring {{Tunnel}}.

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="WebTransportSession">destroy(error)</dfn> method immediately terminates the session without a clean close signal:

<ol>
<li>If the session is already closed or has already terminated abnormally, return.
<li>Reset the session's underlying CONNECT stream, carrying the [=error code=] mapped from |error| per the tunnel abort mechanism ([[#errors-outbound]]); when |error| is omitted or carries no sendable code, `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR` is used. No clean session-close signal is sent.
<li>Error all of the session's streams &mdash; bidirectional, unidirectional, and datagram &mdash; and reject any pending stream-creation promises.
<li>Reject {{WebTransportSession/closed}} with |error| if provided, or with a [=protocol error=] whose code is `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR` otherwise.
</ol>

Where {{WebTransportSession/close()}} performs the clean termination defined by [[WEBTRANSPORT]] &mdash; the peer's `closed` promise resolves with the {{WebTransportCloseInfo}} &mdash; {{WebTransportSession/destroy()|WebTransportSession.destroy()}} is the abrupt one: the peer observes abnormal session termination, which the WebTransport API already defines as a `closed` rejection. Like {{Tunnel/destroy()|Tunnel.destroy()}}, it operates on the session rather than through its streams, remains effective when streams are locked, and short-circuits a pending clean close.
</div>

{{WebTransportSession}} supports `Symbol.asyncDispose`, enabling `await using` declarations, with the same disposal steps as {{Tunnel}}: if the session is already closed or has terminated abnormally, the disposal promise resolves with `undefined`; otherwise {{WebTransportSession/close()}} is called with a default {{WebTransportCloseInfo}} and the promise settles as termination completes &mdash; fulfilled on clean closure, rejected if termination goes abnormal while closing. As with {{Tunnel}}, there is no `Symbol.dispose`.

```js
async connect(ctx) {
  if (ctx.connectProtocol !== 'webtransport') {
    return new ServerResponse(null, { status: 501 });
  }
  await using session = await ctx.upgradeWebTransport();
  for await (const stream of session.incomingBidirectionalStreams) {
    handleStream(stream);
  }
  // Scope exit performs a clean close and awaits termination.
}
```


Errors {#errors}
================

HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 carry typed error signals in both directions: stream resets (`RST_STREAM`, `RESET_STREAM`, `STOP_SENDING`) and connection-level `GOAWAY` frames, each with an error code. This section defines a protocol-version-independent registry of [=error codes=], the shape of the JavaScript errors that carry them, and how they propagate outbound (server to client) and inbound (client to server).

Error codes {#errors-codes}
---------------------------

An <dfn export>error code</dfn> is a string identifying a protocol-level error condition independently of HTTP version.

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Code</th><th>HTTP/2</th><th>HTTP/3</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_REJECTED`</dfn></td>
      <td>REFUSED_STREAM</td>
      <td>H3_REQUEST_REJECTED</td>
      <td>Not processed. The client may safely retry, including non-idempotent methods.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED`</dfn></td>
      <td>CANCEL</td>
      <td>H3_REQUEST_CANCELLED</td>
      <td>Intentionally cancelled. May have been partially processed.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR`</dfn></td>
      <td>INTERNAL_ERROR</td>
      <td>H3_INTERNAL_ERROR</td>
      <td>Internal error in the HTTP stack or application.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_STREAM_RESET`</dfn></td>
      <td>any other code</td>
      <td>any other code</td>
      <td>Generic stream reset; the wire code has no more specific mapping.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY`</dfn></td>
      <td>GOAWAY frame</td>
      <td>GOAWAY frame</td>
      <td>The connection is winding down; no new streams will be accepted.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_PROTOCOL_ERROR`</dfn></td>
      <td>PROTOCOL_ERROR</td>
      <td>H3_GENERAL_PROTOCOL_ERROR</td>
      <td>Framing or protocol violation.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR`</dfn></td>
      <td>CONNECT_ERROR</td>
      <td>H3_CONNECT_ERROR</td>
      <td>A tunnel was reset or abnormally closed.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_BODY_REJECTED`</dfn></td>
      <td>RST_STREAM (NO_ERROR) after a complete response</td>
      <td>STOP_SENDING (H3_NO_ERROR)</td>
      <td>The server does not want the remainder of the request body; the response is unaffected.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_CONNECTION_RESET`</dfn></td>
      <td colspan=2>transport-level</td>
      <td>The underlying connection was lost.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_TIMEOUT`</dfn></td>
      <td colspan=2>transport-level</td>
      <td>An implementation-defined or configured ([[#serve-timeouts]]) timeout elapsed.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_CONTENT_TOO_LARGE`</dfn></td>
      <td colspan=2>None of its own: the remainder of the request body is refused as for `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_BODY_REJECTED`, and the handler chooses the response (413 (Content Too Large) is the fallback when it faults; see [[#serve-limits]])</td>
      <td>A configured request content limit was exceeded ([[#serve-limits]]). A declared length over the limit is rejected with 413 before dispatch, without an [=exchange=].</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Where each code may appear:

*   Sendable via {{ServerContext/deny()}}: `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_REJECTED` (the default), `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED`, `ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR`, `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY`, and, on a {{ConnectContext}} for a CONNECT or extended CONNECT exchange only, `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR`.
*   Sendable by returning an {{ServerResponse/error()}} response ([[#errors-outbound]]): `ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR`.
*   Sendable by aborting a committed response ([[#errors-outbound]]): `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED`, `ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR` (the default).
*   Sendable by ending iteration of the request body early ([[#errors-outbound]]): `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_BODY_REJECTED`.
*   Sendable by failing a [=tunnel=]'s {{Tunnel/writer|Tunnel.writer}} or calling {{Tunnel/destroy()|Tunnel.destroy()}} ([[#errors-outbound]]): `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED`, `ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR`, `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR` (the default).
*   Sendable by calling {{ServerWebSocketStream/destroy()|ServerWebSocketStream.destroy()}} ([[#errors-outbound]]): `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED` (the default), `ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR`, `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR`.
*   Surfaced on request body iteration when a configured content limit is exceeded ([[#serve-limits]]): `ERR_HTTP_CONTENT_TOO_LARGE`.
*   Surfaced on {{ServerRequest/trailers}} when the request body will not be read to its end ([[#serverrequest-trailers]]): `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_BODY_REJECTED`.
*   Observable inbound ([[#errors-inbound]]): `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED`, `ERR_HTTP_STREAM_RESET`, `ERR_HTTP_PROTOCOL_ERROR`, `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY`, `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR`, `ERR_HTTP_CONNECTION_RESET`, `ERR_HTTP_TIMEOUT`.

Note: The registry is deliberately small. Most HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 error codes (`FLOW_CONTROL_ERROR`, `FRAME_SIZE_ERROR`, ...) are protocol plumbing that applications cannot act on; unrecognized wire codes surface as `ERR_HTTP_STREAM_RESET`. The taxonomy is designed to be shared with client-side `fetch()` error reporting, where the same codes describe the same wire events observed from the other end.

Note: The names in the registry share the `ERR_HTTP_` prefix with error codes that some runtimes already use for their own HTTP APIs, such as Node.js's `ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT` and `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_TIMEOUT`. At the time of writing, Node.js uses none of the names in the registry. The registry is closed: an implementation MUST NOT use a name defined here for any other meaning, and SHOULD NOT add codes with the `ERR_HTTP_` prefix to the objects this specification defines unless they are added to this registry.

Protocol error objects {#errors-objects}
----------------------------------------

A <dfn export>protocol error</dfn> is a {{TypeError}} with:

*   a `code` own property whose value is an [=error code=]; and
*   optionally, a `detail` own property whose value is an object of the form `{ protocol, errorCode, errorName }` carrying the raw wire-level information (e.g., `{ protocol: "h2", errorCode: 0x07, errorName: "REFUSED_STREAM" }`). The `protocol` member is the ALPN protocol identifier of the connection, as {{ServerContext/alpnProtocol}} returns it. For errors that have no wire-level code, such as a lost HTTP/1.1 connection, `detail` is absent.

The `cause` property is not used for protocol metadata; it retains its conventional role of chaining an underlying `Error`.

Wherever an application supplies a value that selects the [=error code=] to send (the argument of {{ServerContext/deny()}}, the reason with which a response body or trailers promise fails, or the reason given to a `fail()` or `destroy()` method), the value need not be a [=protocol error=]. To get the <dfn export>error code carried by</dfn> a value |value|, given a set |sendable| of the [=error codes=] that may be sent at that point ([[#errors-codes]]):

<ol>
<li>If |value| is not an Object, return null.
<li>Let |code| be [$Get$](|value|, `"code"`). If this throws, return null.
<li>If |code| is a String that is one of the codes in |sendable|, return |code|.
<li>Return null.
</ol>

So any object with a suitable `code` property works, for example `Object.assign(new Error('stop'), { code: 'ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED' })`, a [=protocol error=] received from another exchange, or an error constructed with the `code` option of the TC39 proposal below. When the result is null, the default for that point is sent.

Note: Constructing errors with a `code` option depends on the [TC39 Error Code proposal](https://github.com/tc39/proposal-error-code-property), which adds a standardized `code` property to the `Error` constructor options. If that proposal does not advance, `code` remains an own property assigned by the implementation; the observable shape is the same.

Outbound errors {#errors-outbound}
----------------------------------

A handler signals protocol-level errors to the client through five mechanisms:

1.  **Before commitment**: {{ServerContext/deny()}} resets the stream. Valid only while the exchange is [=exchange/open=]. See [[#servercontext-denial]].

2.  **A network-error response**: returning a {{ServerResponse}} created by {{ServerResponse/error()}} while the exchange is [=response-capable=] resets the stream with `ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR`. Unlike {{ServerContext/deny()}}, this remains available after informational responses have been sent, and does not tell the client that the request went unprocessed. See [[#serverresponse-sending]].

3.  **After commitment**: if the body of the {{ServerResponse}} being sent cannot be read to its end ([[#serverresponse-body]]), or its trailer fields fail ([[#serverresponse-trailers]]), the implementation resets the stream. If the error [=error code carried by|carries=] a code sendable by aborting a committed response, that code is mapped to the wire; otherwise `ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR` is used. `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_REJECTED` is not sendable here: its retryability guarantee cannot be honored once bytes have been sent, and the state model keeps it structurally unreachable after commitment.

4.  **Request body refusal**: ending iteration of the request body before the body has ended ([[#serverrequest-body]]) signals that the server does not want the remainder of the upload &mdash; `STOP_SENDING` on HTTP/3, or `RST_STREAM` with NO_ERROR after a complete response on HTTP/2, per [RFC 9113 Section 8.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9113#section-8.1). The response side of the exchange is unaffected. This is the outbound use of `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_BODY_REJECTED`. The same refusal applies implicitly when the exchange becomes [=exchange/complete=] or [=exchange/denied=] before the request body has been read to its end. HTTP/1.1 has no signal for it: to keep the connection usable for later requests, the implementation MAY read and discard the rest of the body, up to an implementation-defined amount, after the response; otherwise, or if that amount is exceeded, it closes the connection after the response.

5.  **Tunnel abort**: failing the {{Tunnel/writer|Tunnel.writer}} of an established [=tunnel=], ending iteration of its {{Tunnel/readable|Tunnel.readable}} (or of its {{CapsuleStream/readable|CapsuleStream.readable}}) early, or calling {{Tunnel/destroy()|Tunnel.destroy()}} terminates the tunnel abnormally and resets the stream. If the fail or destroy reason [=error code carried by|carries=] a sendable code, that code is mapped to the wire; otherwise `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR` is used, as it always is when iteration ends early, which carries no reason. See [[#tunnel-closure]]. {{ServerWebSocketStream/destroy()|ServerWebSocketStream.destroy()}} terminates a WebSocket connection the same way, except that its default is `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED`; cancelling or aborting the streams of a {{ServerWebSocketStream}} starts the WebSocket closing handshake instead of resetting the stream ([[#websocketstream-closure]]). {{WebTransportSession/destroy()|WebTransportSession.destroy()}} terminates a WebTransport session the same way, by resetting its CONNECT stream ([[#webtransport-lifecycle]]).

Inbound errors {#errors-inbound}
--------------------------------

When the client resets the request stream or the connection is lost before the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/complete=], the exchange becomes [=exchange/aborted=] and the event propagates as a [=protocol error=] &mdash; the exchange's [=abort reason=] &mdash; on every surface the handler may be observing:

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Surface</th><th>Propagation</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>{{ServerRequest/signal}}</td><td>Aborted, with the protocol error as the abort reason</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Iterating the request body</td><td>A pending or subsequent `next()` rejects with the protocol error</td></tr>
    <tr><td>{{ServerRequest/trailers}}</td><td>Rejects with the protocol error, if not already settled</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The body of the {{ServerResponse}} being sent</td><td>Reading stops, and the body is closed with the protocol error as the reason ([[#serverresponse-body]])</td></tr>
    <tr><td>{{Tunnel/closed|Tunnel.closed}} / {{ServerWebSocketStream/closed|ServerWebSocketStream.closed}} / {{WebTransportSession/closed|WebTransportSession.closed}}</td><td>Rejects with the protocol error on abnormal termination</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A [=tunnel=]'s {{Tunnel/readable}}, {{Tunnel/writer}}, or {{CapsuleStream}}</td><td>A pending or subsequent iteration step rejects, and the writer errors (pending and subsequent writes and sends reject), with the protocol error</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The streams of a {{ServerWebSocketStream}}</td><td>Both error with the protocol error; pending and subsequent reads and writes reject</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pending {{ConnectContext/accept()}} or upgrade promises</td><td>Reject with the protocol error</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

The code distinguishes situations a handler may reasonably treat differently: `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED` (the client deliberately cancelled), `ERR_HTTP_CONNECTION_RESET` (the connection died), `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY` (the connection was closed after a GOAWAY frame, sent by either endpoint, before the exchange could complete; for example, a peer that sends GOAWAY and then closes the connection, or a server-initiated drain cut short by {{Closeable/destroy()}}). Inbound wire codes with no specific mapping surface as `ERR_HTTP_STREAM_RESET`.

Handler model {#handler-model}
==============================

An application provides one or two handler functions: `fetch()` for standard request/response exchanges, and optionally `connect()` for tunnel protocols.

The FetchHandler {#handler-fetch}
---------------------------------

The `fetch()` [=handler=] receives a {{ServerContext}} and returns a {{ServerResponse}}, `undefined`, or a {{Promise}} resolving to one. A returned {{ServerResponse}} is sent as described in [[#serverresponse-sending]].

Returning `undefined` is valid only after calling {{ServerContext/deny()}}, or when the [=exchange=] was [=exchange/aborted=]. Any other `undefined` &mdash; or any other return value that is not a {{ServerResponse}}, unless the implementation accepts the value as a response in an implementation-defined manner (for a {{Response}}, as if converted by {{ServerResponse/from()}}; see [[#serverresponse-constructor]]) &mdash; is a programming error: the implementation invokes the `error()` handler ([[#handler-error]]) with a {{TypeError}} describing the invalid return, falling back to 500 Internal Server Error. A {{ServerResponse}} that is [=ServerResponse/unusable=], because its body has already been read, is likewise an invalid return.

Responses to HEAD requests are transmitted without content, per HTTP semantics: the implementation sends the head of the returned {{ServerResponse}} but not its body or trailer fields. The body is closed without being read, so a generator or stream that produces it does not run to completion, and no header field is derived from it ([[#serverresponse-sending]]). A handler that wants a `Content-Length` (or other content-derived field) in a response to HEAD sets it.

```js
async fetch(ctx) {
  const { request } = ctx;
  const url = new URL(request.url);

  // Deny under load
  if (atCapacity) {
    ctx.deny();
    return;
  }

  if (url.pathname === '/api/data') {
    return ServerResponse.json({ ok: true });
  }

  return new ServerResponse("Not Found", { status: 404 });
}
```

The ConnectHandler {#handler-connect}
-------------------------------------

The `connect()` [=handler=] receives a {{ConnectContext}} and:

*   Returns `undefined` if the tunnel has been accepted, denied via {{ServerContext/deny()}}, or fallen through via the [=fall-through sentinel=].
*   Returns a {{ServerResponse}} to deny the request at the application level. For CONNECT and extended CONNECT, the response's status must not be 2xx, because a 2xx response would tell the client that a tunnel was established ([[#connectcontext-accept]]); a 2xx {{ServerResponse}} is an invalid return, handled as for `fetch()` ([[#handler-fetch]]). On an [=upgrade-originated=] exchange, any {{ServerResponse}} may be returned.
*   Returning `undefined` in any other case (without accepting, denying, or falling through) is an invalid return, handled as for `fetch()`.
*   Throws or returns a rejected promise while the exchange is [=response-capable=], causing the `error()` handler to be invoked ([[#handler-error]]), with a 502 Bad Gateway response as the fallback for CONNECT and extended CONNECT, and a 500 Internal Server Error response for [=upgrade-originated=] exchanges. (A CONNECT server acts as a proxy for the tunnel, so its failure is a gateway failure. An HTTP/1.1 upgrade request is an ordinary request to the server itself.)

```js
async connect(ctx) {
  switch (ctx.connectProtocol) {
    case 'websocket': {
      const ws = ctx.upgradeWebSocket();
      ws.addEventListener('message', e => ws.send(`Echo: ${e.data}`));
      return;
    }
    case 'webtransport': {
      const session = await ctx.upgradeWebTransport();
      handleWebTransport(session);
      return;
    }
    case 'connect-udp': {
      const tunnel = await ctx.accept();
      const dg = tunnel.datagrams();
      pipeUdpPayloads(dg);
      return;
    }
    default:
      return new ServerResponse(null, { status: 501 });
  }
}
```

If no `connect()` handler is provided, the implementation responds to CONNECT requests with 501 Not Implemented.

The ErrorHandler {#handler-error}
---------------------------------

The optional `error()` [=handler=] customizes the response when another handler faults. It receives the same context object the faulting handler received and the thrown value, and may:

*   return a {{ServerResponse}} (or a {{Promise}} resolving to one), which is sent (for a CONNECT or extended CONNECT exchange, a 2xx response is an invalid return, and the default response is sent instead; see [[#handler-connect]]);
*   call {{ServerContext/deny()}} (valid only while the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/open=]) and return `undefined`; or
*   return `undefined`, falling through to the default: 500 Internal Server Error for `fetch()` and for `connect()` on an [=upgrade-originated=] exchange, and 502 Bad Gateway for `connect()` on CONNECT and extended CONNECT ([[#handler-connect]]).

```js
export default {
  [Symbol.for('wintertc.server.protocol')]: 1,

  async fetch(ctx) {
    return handle(ctx.request);
  },

  error(ctx, err) {
    if (err.code === 'ERR_AT_CAPACITY') {
      ctx.deny();  // REFUSED_STREAM — client may retry elsewhere
      return;
    }
    return new ServerResponse('Internal Error', { status: 500 });
  },
};
```

`error()` is invoked only while the exchange is [=response-capable=]: a `fetch()` or `connect()` handler threw, returned a rejected promise, or returned an invalid value before committing. It is not invoked for:

*   faults after commitment &mdash; the response or tunnel is aborted instead ([[#errors-outbound]]);
*   exchanges already [=exchange/denied=] or [=exchange/aborted=] &mdash; there is nothing to respond to;
*   rejected {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} promises &mdash; those are reported by the implementation;
*   faults in `error()` itself &mdash; there is no re-entry; the default response is sent.

Error handling {#handler-errors}
--------------------------------

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Condition</th><th>Behavior</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Handler calls {{ServerContext/deny()}}, returns `undefined`</td><td>Stream reset (per [=error code=])</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Handler calls {{ServerContext/deny()}} with `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY`</td><td>Stream reset + GOAWAY on the connection</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Handler resolves with a non-`undefined` value after {{ServerContext/deny()}}</td><td>Invalid return: reported; nothing is sent; `error()` not invoked</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Handler throws or rejects after {{ServerContext/deny()}}</td><td>Reported; nothing further is sent; `error()` not invoked</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`connect()` calls {{ServerContext/deny()}} with the [=fall-through sentinel=], settles with `undefined`</td><td>`fetch()` is invoked for the same exchange</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`connect()` rejects or resolves with a non-`undefined` value after the [=fall-through sentinel=]</td><td>`error()` is invoked; fallback 500 (the exchange is [=upgrade-originated=]); `fetch()` not invoked</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`fetch()` returns a {{ServerResponse}}</td><td>That response is sent</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A handler returns an {{ServerResponse/error()}} response while [=response-capable=]</td><td>Stream reset; `ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR`; `error()` not invoked</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`fetch()` throws or rejects while [=response-capable=]</td><td>`error()` is invoked; fallback 500</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`fetch()` returns an invalid value</td><td>`error()` is invoked with a {{TypeError}}; fallback 500</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The body or trailer fields of the {{ServerResponse}} being sent fail</td><td>Stream reset; the error's sendable code, or `ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR` ([[#errors-outbound]])</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`connect()` returns a non-2xx {{ServerResponse}} before accepting, or any {{ServerResponse}} on an [=upgrade-originated=] exchange</td><td>That response is sent</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`connect()` or `error()` returns a 2xx {{ServerResponse}} on a CONNECT or extended CONNECT exchange</td><td>Invalid return: `error()` is invoked with a {{TypeError}} (unless the faulting handler is `error()` itself); fallback 502</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`connect()` returns `undefined` after accept or deny</td><td>Normal completion</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`connect()` throws or rejects while [=response-capable=]</td><td>`error()` is invoked; fallback 502, or 500 on an [=upgrade-originated=] exchange</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`connect()` throws or rejects after accepting</td><td>Tunnel aborted; `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR`, or, for a WebSocket connection established by {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}} or {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocketStream()}}, `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED`, as for {{ServerWebSocketStream/destroy()}}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`error()` throws, rejects, or returns an invalid value</td><td>Default 500/502; no re-entry</td></tr>
    <tr><td>[=Exchange=] is [=exchange/aborted=] (client gone)</td><td>Handler outcome discarded; `error()` not invoked</td></tr>
    <tr><td>{{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} promise rejects</td><td>Reported by the implementation; response unaffected</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

The handler object {#handler-object}
-------------------------------------

Handlers are provided as an object with a `fetch` method, a `connect` method, or both, and optionally an `error` method:

```js
const handler = {
  [Symbol.for('wintertc.server.protocol')]: 1,

  fetch(ctx) {
    return new ServerResponse("Hello");
  },
  connect(ctx) {
    // ...
  },
};

serve(handler, { port: 8080 });
```

The handler object is converted to a {{HandlerObject}} once, when it is passed to {{WindowOrWorkerGlobalScope/serve()}} or when a declarative export is loaded ([[#handler-declarative]]). Its methods are therefore read at that time, and later changes to its `fetch`, `connect`, or `error` properties have no effect. To <dfn>convert a handler object</dfn> |handler|:

<ol>
<li>Let |marker| be [$Get$](|handler|, `Symbol.for('wintertc.server.protocol')`), rethrowing any exception.
<li>If |marker| is not `undefined` and [$SameValue$](|marker|, 1) is false, throw a {{TypeError}}: the handler object was written for a version of this API that the implementation does not support. (The marker is optional for {{WindowOrWorkerGlobalScope/serve()}}, whose use already identifies the calling convention, but when it is present it must be valid.)
<li>Let |dict| be the result of converting |handler| to a {{HandlerObject}}, rethrowing any exception.
<li>If neither |dict|["{{HandlerObject/fetch}}"] nor |dict|["{{HandlerObject/connect}}"] [=map/exists=], throw a {{TypeError}}.
<li>Return |dict|.
</ol>

A handler object without `fetch` serves only tunnels: non-CONNECT requests are answered with 501 (Not Implemented), and [=upgrade-originated=] exchanges are routed to `connect()` as usual, but cannot fall through ([[#connectcontext-fallthrough]]); a fall-through on such an exchange is answered with 501. Each handler is invoked with the handler object as its callback this value, so `this` inside a handler refers to the handler object. This allows the handler object to carry application state:

```js
const app = {
  [Symbol.for('wintertc.server.protocol')]: 1,
  db: createPool(databaseUrl),

  async fetch(ctx) {
    const rows = await this.db.query('SELECT * FROM users');
    return ServerResponse.json(rows);
  },
};

serve(app, { port: 443, tls: { cert, key } });
```

Declarative export and protocol identification {#handler-declarative}
---------------------------------------------------------------------

As an alternative to the imperative `serve()` call, an application may export a default handler object. Because existing runtimes already use `export default { fetch() {} }` with their own, mutually incompatible handler signatures (typically a {{Request}} followed by runtime-specific arguments), a handler object includes a [=protocol marker=] so the runtime can distinguish this API from legacy invocation conventions.

The marker is a `Symbol.for('wintertc.server.protocol')` property with an integer version value:

```js
export default {
  [Symbol.for('wintertc.server.protocol')]: 1,

  async fetch(ctx) {
    return new ServerResponse("Hello");
  },

  async connect(ctx) {
    if (ctx.connectProtocol === 'websocket') {
      const ws = ctx.upgradeWebSocket();
      ws.addEventListener('message', e => ws.send(e.data));
      return;
    }
    return new ServerResponse(null, { status: 501 });
  },
};
```

A conforming runtime that supports declarative export checks for the presence and value of `Symbol.for('wintertc.server.protocol')` on the default export before invoking handler methods:

*   If `Symbol.for('wintertc.server.protocol')` is present and its value is `1` (compared with [$SameValue$], so `"1"` is not a match), the runtime [=convert a handler object|converts=] the default export as {{WindowOrWorkerGlobalScope/serve()}} does ([[#handler-object]]), and invokes `fetch()` with a {{ServerContext}} and `connect()` with a {{ConnectContext}}.
*   If `Symbol.for('wintertc.server.protocol')` is absent, the runtime invokes handler methods using its existing (legacy) calling convention. Existing application code continues to work without modification.
*   If `Symbol.for('wintertc.server.protocol')` is present but its value is not a recognized version, the runtime rejects the handler with a descriptive error when the module is loaded, before any request is dispatched to it, in the way it reports any other error that prevents the module from serving.

The version number allows the protocol to evolve. This specification defines version `1`. Future revisions that change handler signatures would increment the version.

The **handler object** is the portable unit. The same handler object works with both patterns:

```js
const handler = {
  [Symbol.for('wintertc.server.protocol')]: 1,
  fetch(ctx) { return new ServerResponse("Hello"); },
};

// Declarative, where the platform manages the server:
export default handler;

// Imperative, where the application manages the server:
serve(handler, { port: 443, tls: { cert, key } });
```


Server configuration {#server-configuration}
=============================================

The serve() function {#serve-function}
--------------------------------------

<div algorithm="serve(handler, options)">
The <dfn method for="WindowOrWorkerGlobalScope">serve(handler, options)</dfn> function creates a {{Server}}. |handler| is [=convert a handler object|converted=], rethrowing any exception, and each handler is invoked with |handler| as its callback this value ([[#handler-object]]). If |options|["{{ServerOptions/port}}"] [=map/exists=], the server begins listening immediately, as if {{Server/listen()}} had been called with no arguments: on |options|["{{ServerOptions/port}}"], and on |options|["{{ServerOptions/hostname}}"] if it exists and the [=default bind address=] otherwise. Otherwise, the server is created in an unbound state and must be explicitly started with {{Server/listen()}}.

Binding is asynchronous, but {{WindowOrWorkerGlobalScope/serve()}} returns the {{Server}} at once. Its outcome is reported through the <dfn attribute for="Server">ready</dfn> attribute, a promise that is the same on every get and is always [=mark as handled|marked as handled=]:

*   If the server listens immediately, the promise is fulfilled with `undefined` once that listener is bound, and the listener is then the first item when the {{Server}} is iterated. If binding fails, for example because the address is in use, or a requested binding capability is not supported ([[#serve-options]]), the promise is rejected with the error, and the server is destroyed as if by {{Closeable/destroy()}} with that error, so {{Closeable/closed}} rejects with it too.
*   Otherwise, the promise is fulfilled with `undefined` at once. Each call to {{Server/listen()}} reports its own outcome through the promise it returns.

```js
const server = serve(handler, { port: 0 });
await server.ready;
const [listener] = server;
console.log(`listening on port ${listener.address.port}`);
```
</div>

```js
// One-step: create and listen
const server = serve({
  [Symbol.for('wintertc.server.protocol')]: 1,
  fetch(ctx) {
    return new ServerResponse("Hello");
  },
}, {
  port: 443,
  hostname: '0.0.0.0',
  tls: { cert, key },
  quic: true,
});

// Two-step: create then listen
const server = serve({
  [Symbol.for('wintertc.server.protocol')]: 1,
  fetch(ctx) {
    return new ServerResponse("Hello");
  },
}, {
  tls: { cert, key },
});

await server.listen({ port: 443, hostname: '0.0.0.0' });
```

ServerOptions {#serve-options}
------------------------------

When {{ServerOptions/port}} is `0`, the operating system assigns an available port. When {{ServerOptions/hostname}} is omitted, the server binds to the <dfn>default bind address</dfn>, which is all local interfaces: the IPv6 wildcard address `"::"` as a dual-stack binding, where the platform supports one, and the IPv4 wildcard address `"0.0.0.0"` otherwise. An application that wants an IPv4-only binding passes `"0.0.0.0"` explicitly.

Most members of {{ServerOptions}} have a like-named member of {{ListenOptions}}. When {{Server/listen()}} is called, the configuration of the new binding is determined member by member:

*   A member that [=map/exists=] in the {{ListenOptions}} is used.
*   Otherwise, the like-named member of the {{ServerOptions}} given to {{WindowOrWorkerGlobalScope/serve()}} is used, if it [=map/exists=] there.
*   Otherwise, the default applies: port `0` for {{ListenOptions/port}}, the [=default bind address=] for {{ListenOptions/hostname}}, `false` for {{ListenOptions/reusePort}}, {{ListenOptions/ipv6Only}}, and {{ListenOptions/quic}}, and the defaults described for the other members.

For {{ServerOptions/timeouts}} and {{ServerOptions/limits}}, this rule applies to the members of those dictionaries one by one, so a {{ListenOptions}} can override a single timeout and inherit the rest ([[#serve-timeouts]], [[#serve-limits]]). Other dictionary-valued members, such as {{ServerOptions/tls}}, are taken whole from one source or the other. {{ServerOptions/autoContinue}} and {{ServerOptions/signal}} apply to the server as a whole, and have no {{ListenOptions}} counterpart. The listener that {{WindowOrWorkerGlobalScope/serve()}} creates when {{ServerOptions/port}} is given uses the {{ServerOptions}} alone.

The {{ServerOptions/autoContinue}} member controls automatic handling of `Expect: 100-continue` requests; see [[#servercontext-100-continue]]. The configured value is exposed to handlers via {{ServerContext/autoContinue}}.

Three members, available on both {{ServerOptions}} and {{ListenOptions}}, configure the binding itself: {{ListenOptions/reusePort}} requests SO_REUSEPORT-style load distribution, allowing multiple processes or listeners to bind the same address and port; {{ListenOptions/ipv6Only}} restricts a wildcard IPv6 binding (e.g., `"::"`) to IPv6 rather than dual-stack; {{ListenOptions/backlog}} sets the accept queue depth, defaulting to an implementation-defined value. Where the platform cannot provide a requested binding capability, the binding fails with a `"NotSupportedError"` {{DOMException}}, per [[#conformance]]: the promise returned by {{Server/listen()}} rejects with it, or, for the listener that {{WindowOrWorkerGlobalScope/serve()}} creates, {{Server/ready}} does.

TLS configuration {#serve-tls}
------------------------------

When {{ServerOptions/tls}} is provided, the server listens for TLS connections and negotiates ALPN. The {{TLSOptions/alpn}} member lists the protocols offered over TLS on TCP, in order of preference; the default is `["h2", "http/1.1"]`. A value that is not an HTTP version supported over TCP by the implementation (such as `"h3"`, which runs only over QUIC) causes the binding to fail with a {{TypeError}}. QUIC connections always negotiate `"h3"`.

### SNI-based certificate selection ### {#serve-tls-sni}

The {{TLSOptions/sni}} option enables serving multiple hostnames with different certificates on the same listener. It can be either an object mapping hostnames to certificates or a callback function.

**Object form** &mdash; when the set of hostnames is known up front:

```js
serve(handler, {
  port: 443,
  tls: {
    cert: defaultCert,
    key: defaultKey,
    sni: {
      'example.com': { cert: exampleCert, key: exampleKey },
      '*.example.com': { cert: wildcardCert, key: wildcardKey },
      'other.net': { cert: otherCert, key: otherKey },
    },
  },
});
```

Keys support a wildcard (`*`) as the complete left-most label only, matched as [RFC 9525 Section 6.3](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9525#section-6.3) specifies: the wildcard matches exactly one label, so `*.example.com` matches `www.example.com` but neither `example.com` nor `a.b.example.com`. Keys and the client's server name are compared ASCII case-insensitively. A key without a wildcard takes precedence over a wildcard key that also matches.

**Callback form** &mdash; for dynamic selection (ACME, vault lookup, etc.):

```js
serve(handler, {
  port: 443,
  tls: {
    cert: defaultCert,
    key: defaultKey,
    async sni(hostname) {
      const record = await certStore.lookup(hostname);
      if (record) return { cert: record.cert, key: record.key };
      return null;  // fall through to default
    },
  },
});
```

The callback is invoked during the TLS handshake with the server name from the ClientHello, [=ASCII lowercase|ASCII-lowercased=], in the A-label form in which it is sent for internationalized names. It is not invoked when the client sends no server name; the default certificate is used. It may return a {{TLSCertificate}}, `null`, or `undefined`, or a promise for one of these, and the handshake waits for the promise. A {{TLSCertificate}} is used for the connection. `null` or `undefined` selects the default certificate ({{TLSCertificate/cert}} and {{TLSCertificate/key}} of the {{TLSOptions}}), and if there is none, the handshake fails. If the callback throws, the promise rejects, or the returned value cannot be converted to a {{TLSCertificate}}, the handshake fails, and the error is reported in an implementation-defined manner. The callback is subject to the {{TimeoutOptions/handshake}} timeout ([[#serve-timeouts]]).

A {{TLSCertificate/cert}} given as a string is in PEM format, and may contain the certificate followed by its intermediate certificates. Given as a {{BufferSource}}, it is a single DER-encoded certificate. A {{TLSCertificate/key}} is an unencrypted private key, in PEM format (as a string) or DER format (as a {{BufferSource}}).

QUIC configuration {#serve-quic}
--------------------------------

When {{ServerOptions/quic}} is `true` or a {{QUICOptions}} object, the server additionally listens for QUIC connections on the same port and supports HTTP/3.

HTTP/3 requires TLS. If {{ServerOptions/quic}} is enabled and {{ServerOptions/tls}} is not provided, the implementation throws a {{TypeError}}. If {{ServerOptions/quic}} is enabled and the implementation does not support QUIC, it throws a `"NotSupportedError"` {{DOMException}} (see [[#conformance]]).

The {{QUICOptions}} dictionary has no members in this specification. It is the place for implementation-specific QUIC configuration ([[#conformance]]), such as congestion control or transport parameters.

Tunnel protocol support {#serve-tunnel-protocols}
-------------------------------------------------

Extended CONNECT, HTTP Datagrams, and WebTransport are enabled per connection by settings that the server advertises to the client. Three members, available on both {{ServerOptions}} and {{ListenOptions}}, control what the implementation advertises:

: <dfn dict-member for="ServerOptions">extendedConnect</dfn>
:: Whether extended CONNECT is enabled: `SETTINGS_ENABLE_CONNECT_PROTOCOL` on HTTP/2 ([[RFC8441]]) and HTTP/3 ([[RFC9220]]). When it is disabled, the client does not send extended CONNECT requests, and a request with a `:protocol` pseudo-header is malformed, and is rejected before dispatch. It does not affect plain CONNECT, or HTTP/1.1 upgrades ([[#connectcontext-upgrade]]).
: <dfn dict-member for="ServerOptions">datagrams</dfn>
:: Whether HTTP Datagrams are enabled on HTTP/3: `SETTINGS_H3_DATAGRAM` ([[RFC9297]]), together with the QUIC `max_datagram_frame_size` transport parameter ([[RFC9221]]). When it is disabled, datagrams on HTTP/3 are carried as DATAGRAM capsules, as on HTTP/2, and the {{DatagramStream/unreliable}} member of {{Tunnel/datagrams()}} is `false`.
: <dfn dict-member for="ServerOptions">webTransport</dfn>
:: Whether WebTransport is enabled: the settings by which the server indicates support for WebTransport sessions on HTTP/2 and HTTP/3. When it is disabled, {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebTransport()}} rejects with a `"NotSupportedError"` {{DOMException}}. Enabling WebTransport also enables extended CONNECT and, on HTTP/3, HTTP Datagrams, which WebTransport requires.

When a member is absent, its value is `true` if the handler object has a `connect()` method and the implementation supports the feature, and `false` otherwise: a server whose application cannot handle tunnels does not invite them. An explicit `true` for a feature that the implementation does not support throws a `"NotSupportedError"` {{DOMException}} ([[#conformance]]). In runtimes without the infrastructure layer, what is advertised is determined by the runtime.

Alternative services {#serve-altsvc}
------------------------------------

The {{ServerOptions/altSvc}} member &mdash; available on both {{ServerOptions}} and {{ListenOptions}}, overriding per binding &mdash; controls advertisement of alternative services per [RFC 7838](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7838):

*   When `true`, or absent while {{ServerOptions/quic}} is enabled, advertisement is automatic: on responses sent over TCP-based connections, the implementation advertises its own QUIC binding(s) for the same effective origin (e.g., `h3=":443"`), composing the field value and its `ma` parameter itself. The implementation MAY use the `Alt-Svc` response header field or, on HTTP/2, the ALTSVC frame.
*   When a string, the value is used verbatim as the `Alt-Svc` field value. This covers advertising a different host or port, custom `ma` or `persist` parameters, and `clear`.
*   When `false`, no advertisement is performed.

If a {{ServerResponse}} returned by the handler already carries an `Alt-Svc` header field, the implementation does not override or supplement it. No advertisement is sent on connections that are not TLS-protected; clients ignore alternative services from insecure origins.

Note: Advertisement is how clients discover an HTTP/3 binding ([[#serve-quic]]). Without it, a QUIC-enabled listener is reachable only by clients that already know of it through other means, such as DNS HTTPS records &mdash; which are outside the scope of this specification. Only the implementation can perform the frame-based form of advertisement, and only the implementation reliably knows its own bindings; this is why advertisement is implementation behavior rather than an application concern.

Timeouts {#serve-timeouts}
--------------------------

Activity timeouts are configured through the {{ServerOptions/timeouts}} member of {{ServerOptions}}. A {{ListenOptions/timeouts}} member on {{ListenOptions}} overrides the server-level configuration member-wise for that binding. All values are in milliseconds. An explicit `0` disables the timeout; an absent member leaves the value implementation-defined.

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Member</th><th>Scope</th><th>The clock runs while&hellip;</th><th>On expiry</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>{{TimeoutOptions/handshake}}</td>
      <td>connection</td>
      <td>TLS or connection setup is incomplete</td>
      <td>Close the connection</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{TimeoutOptions/idle}}</td>
      <td>connection</td>
      <td>No active [=exchanges=] exist and no frames are being exchanged</td>
      <td>GOAWAY and close (HTTP/1.1: close the keep-alive connection; QUIC: maps to the negotiated idle timeout)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{TimeoutOptions/headers}}</td>
      <td>connection (or stream), before an [=exchange=] exists</td>
      <td>The request head is incomplete</td>
      <td>HTTP/1.1: 408 (Request Timeout) and close. HTTP/2 and HTTP/3: an implementation-defined stream or connection error &mdash; a complete head usually arrives in a single frame, so this is chiefly an HTTP/1.1 (slowloris) defense</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{TimeoutOptions/receive}}</td>
      <td>exchange</td>
      <td>The exchange is awaiting client data it needs &mdash; a step of request body iteration is pending ([[#serverrequest-body]])</td>
      <td>Abort the exchange: [=exchange/aborted=], with a [=protocol error=] whose code is `ERR_HTTP_TIMEOUT` propagating through the surfaces in [[#errors-inbound]]</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{TimeoutOptions/send}}</td>
      <td>exchange</td>
      <td>Response data is queued and is not being accepted by the transport; or the response body has ended and the implementation is waiting for its trailer fields ([[#serverresponse-sending]])</td>
      <td>Same as {{TimeoutOptions/receive}}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{TimeoutOptions/tunnelIdle}}</td>
      <td>tunnel / session</td>
      <td>An established [=tunnel=] (including a WebSocket connection) or {{WebTransportSession}} has no data flowing in either direction</td>
      <td>{{Tunnel/destroy()|Tunnel.destroy()}} (or {{ServerWebSocketStream/destroy()|ServerWebSocketStream.destroy()}}, or {{WebTransportSession/destroy()|WebTransportSession.destroy()}}) with a [=protocol error=] whose code is `ERR_HTTP_TIMEOUT`. The connection of a {{WebSocket}} returned by {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}} is reset as by {{ServerWebSocketStream/destroy()|ServerWebSocketStream.destroy()}}, and the {{WebSocket}} closes abnormally, as in [[#connectcontext-websocket]]</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{TimeoutOptions/closeHandshake}}</td>
      <td>WebSocket connection</td>
      <td>The server has sent a Close frame and has not yet received one from the peer ([[#websocketstream-closure]])</td>
      <td>Close the transport. Closure is abnormal, with a [=protocol error=] whose code is `ERR_HTTP_TIMEOUT`</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{TimeoutOptions/waitUntil}}</td>
      <td>exchange</td>
      <td>{{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} promises remain unsettled after the response has completed</td>
      <td>The exchange stops waiting and completes; the promises are not cancelled (see below)</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Two principles govern every member:

*   **Activity, not duration.** Each clock measures the absence of progress and resets when progress occurs. Apart from {{TimeoutOptions/waitUntil}}, which bounds work that runs after the response has completed and which no peer is waiting on, no timeout in this specification caps the total duration of an exchange or tunnel &mdash; total-duration caps break streaming responses, large uploads, and long-lived tunnels by design. A consequence worth stating plainly: a slow <em>handler</em> never trips {{TimeoutOptions/receive}} or {{TimeoutOptions/send}}; those clocks run only while the peer owes progress. The one exception is the wait for trailer fields: once the body has ended, nothing else can make progress on the stream, so a trailers promise that never settles would hold it open indefinitely, and {{TimeoutOptions/send}} bounds that wait. Defenses against deliberately slow trickle traffic (minimum-rate enforcement) are implementation latitude.

*   **Expiry is existing machinery.** Exchange-level expiry produces the [=exchange/aborted=] state with a typed [=protocol error=], flowing through the same surfaces as any inbound termination; tunnel expiry is {{Tunnel/destroy()}}; connection expiry is the GOAWAY path. The `error()` handler is never invoked for a timeout &mdash; timeouts are not handler faults.

On the wire, a stream reset caused by {{TimeoutOptions/receive}} or {{TimeoutOptions/send}} expiry carries CANCEL (HTTP/2) or H3_REQUEST_CANCELLED (HTTP/3), while the JavaScript-observable [=protocol error=] carries `ERR_HTTP_TIMEOUT`: the wire says the request is no longer wanted; the API says why.

Expiry of {{TimeoutOptions/waitUntil}} does not &mdash; and cannot &mdash; cancel the outstanding promises: JavaScript has no promise cancellation, and the work behind them is not aborted. Expiry means only that the [=exchange=] stops waiting for them: the exchange is considered complete, and resources the implementation associates with it may be torn down without further delay. The promises may still settle afterward; a late settlement has no effect on the exchange, and a late rejection is reported the same way as any {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} rejection ([[#servercontext-lifecycle]]). Whether the work itself continues to run after teardown depends on the runtime's execution model.

Implementations SHOULD enable {{TimeoutOptions/handshake}}, {{TimeoutOptions/idle}}, and {{TimeoutOptions/headers}} by default with implementation-defined values &mdash; they are connection hygiene. {{TimeoutOptions/tunnelIdle}} SHOULD be disabled by default: a quiet WebSocket or WebTransport session is legitimate, and keepalive traffic (ping/pong frames, session keepalives) counts as activity, so deployments that enable it compose with standard liveness practice. Implementations SHOULD also enable {{TimeoutOptions/closeHandshake}} by default: unlike an idle connection, a connection whose closing handshake has started has no further legitimate use, and RFC 6455 expects the server to close the transport promptly ([RFC 6455 Section 7.1.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-7.1.1)).

Note: Runtimes that do not implement the infrastructure layer determine timeout behavior externally, as with the other members of {{ServerOptions}} (see [[#conformance]]).

Limits {#serve-limits}
----------------------

Resource limits are configured through the {{ServerOptions/limits}} member of {{ServerOptions}}. A {{ListenOptions/limits}} member on {{ListenOptions}} overrides the server-level configuration member-wise for that binding. An explicit `0` disables a limit; an absent member leaves the value implementation-defined. Like timeouts, limits are enforced below the handler: they are denial-of-service hygiene that applications cannot enforce efficiently themselves, because by the time application code can count bytes, the resources are already committed.

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Member</th><th>Enforced</th><th>On violation</th><th>Wire alignment</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>{{LimitOptions/maxHeaderSize}}</td>
      <td>During head parsing, before dispatch</td>
      <td>431 (Request Header Fields Too Large) on HTTP/1.1; stream or connection error on HTTP/2 and HTTP/3. The handler is never invoked</td>
      <td>Advertised as SETTINGS_MAX_HEADER_LIST_SIZE (HTTP/2) and SETTINGS_MAX_FIELD_SECTION_SIZE (HTTP/3)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{LimitOptions/maxRequestBodySize}}</td>
      <td>Against the declared length before dispatch, and against streamed bytes during the exchange</td>
      <td>Declared (`Content-Length` exceeds the limit): 413 (Content Too Large) before dispatch; the handler is never invoked. Streamed overrun: see below</td>
      <td>&mdash;</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{LimitOptions/maxRequestsPerConnection}}</td>
      <td>Per connection, over its lifetime</td>
      <td>Connection recycling, not an error: the final permitted response carries `Connection: close` (HTTP/1.1) or is followed by GOAWAY (HTTP/2, HTTP/3), and the connection closes gracefully</td>
      <td>&mdash;</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{LimitOptions/maxConcurrentStreams}}</td>
      <td>Per connection</td>
      <td>Protocol-level refusal of excess streams</td>
      <td>SETTINGS_MAX_CONCURRENT_STREAMS (HTTP/2); stream credit (HTTP/3)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{LimitOptions/maxConnections}}</td>
      <td>Per listener</td>
      <td>Implementation-defined refusal of new connections</td>
      <td>&mdash;</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

{{LimitOptions/maxHeaderSize}} also applies to the trailer section of a request: request trailers that exceed it abort the [=exchange=] with a [=protocol error=] whose code is `ERR_HTTP_CONTENT_TOO_LARGE`. {{LimitOptions/maxRequestBodySize}} applies to the content of every request, including an [=upgrade-originated=] request, but not to the data of an established [=tunnel=], which is not request content.

Pre-dispatch rejections follow the {{Closeable/busy}} precedent: no handler is invoked, and no context or {{ServerRequest}} need be materialized for the rejected exchange.

When a request body exceeds {{LimitOptions/maxRequestBodySize}} mid-stream &mdash; the length was undeclared, or the declaration was dishonest &mdash; iteration of the request body rejects with a [=protocol error=] whose code is `ERR_HTTP_CONTENT_TOO_LARGE` ([[#serverrequest-body]]), and the implementation stops accepting the remainder of the upload as with request body refusal ([[#errors-outbound]]). The handler remains in control of the response. If the handler subsequently faults with that protocol error while the exchange is still [=response-capable=] and the `error()` handler does not produce a response, the implementation SHOULD use 413 (Content Too Large) as the fallback status rather than 500.

Note: Runtimes that do not implement the infrastructure layer determine limit behavior externally, as with the other members of {{ServerOptions}} (see [[#conformance]]).


Server and Listener {#server-listener}
======================================

{{Server}} and {{Listener}} share the {{Closeable}} interface for lifecycle management. On a {{Listener}}, these apply to a single binding. On a {{Server}}, they apply to all listeners collectively.

Binding and listeners {#server-binding}
---------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="Server">listen(options)</dfn> method binds the server to a network address and returns a `Promise<Listener>` that resolves when the binding is established.
</div>

{{Server/listen()}} may be called multiple times to bind the server to multiple addresses or ports:

```js
const server = serve(handler);
const http  = await server.listen({ port: 80 });
// TCP (HTTPS) and UDP (HTTP/3) on port 443:
const https = await server.listen({ port: 443, tls: { cert, key }, quic: true });
```

{{Server}} is iterable over its active listeners:

```js
for (const listener of server) {
  console.log(listener.address);
}
```

### One-step vs. two-step vs. multi-listener ### {#server-binding-patterns}

```js
// One-step: serve() with port (single listener)
const server = serve(handler, { port: 8080 });

// Two-step: serve() then listen() (single listener)
const server = serve(handler);
await server.listen({ port: 8080 });

// Multi-listener: HTTP + HTTPS + HTTP/3
const server = serve(handler);
await server.listen({ port: 80 });
await server.listen({ port: 443, tls: { cert, key, sni }, quic: true });
```

The Closeable interface {#sec-closeable}
-----------------------------------------

The {{Closeable}} mixin provides a uniform lifecycle interface shared by {{Server}} and {{Listener}}.

### busy ### {#closeable-busy}

<div algorithm>
When <dfn attribute for="Closeable">busy</dfn> is `true`, new [=exchanges=] are refused as if by {{ServerContext/deny()}} with no |error| argument: no handler is invoked, and the exchange is refused with the default [=error code=] &mdash; `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_REJECTED`, i.e. REFUSED_STREAM on HTTP/2 and H3_REQUEST_REJECTED on HTTP/3. On HTTP/1.1, the implementation MAY respond with a 503 (Service Unavailable) &mdash; optionally carrying a `Retry-After` header field &mdash; without closing the connection, since the condition is temporary. In-flight exchanges continue to be processed, and outstanding {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} promises are unaffected.

The attribute is initially `false`. Refusal is decided per listener: an exchange is refused if the {{Listener}} on which it arrives is busy. Setting {{Closeable/busy}} on a {{Server}} sets {{Closeable/busy}} to the same value on every active {{Listener}} of the server, and a {{Listener}} created later by {{Server/listen()}} starts with the server's current value. Getting {{Closeable/busy}} on a {{Server}} returns the value most recently set on it. A {{Listener}}'s value can then be changed individually, for example to keep accepting on one binding while another is drained.
</div>

The refusal applies uniformly to all exchange kinds &mdash; request/response, CONNECT, and [=upgrade-originated=] alike &mdash; since no handler runs to distinguish them. Because no handler is invoked, no context (and, per [[#why-context]], no {{ServerRequest}}) need ever be materialized for a refused exchange.

Setting {{Closeable/busy}} does not wind down connections: no GOAWAY is sent, and connections remain open so that requests are accepted again the moment the attribute returns to `false`. Connection wind-down is the domain of {{Closeable/close()}}, {{Closeable/destroy()}}, and per-request denial with `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY`.

Note: {{Closeable/busy}} deliberately reuses {{ServerContext/deny()}}'s default semantics because they state exactly what a temporarily busy server wants to communicate: nothing was processed, and the client may safely retry &mdash; including non-idempotent requests. HTTP/2's ENHANCE_YOUR_CALM (0x0b) and HTTP/3's H3_EXCESSIVE_LOAD (0x0107) are deliberately not used: those codes signal that the <em>peer</em> is generating suspicious or excessive load &mdash; an accusation of client misbehavior, not a statement of the server's own unavailability.

Note: {{Closeable/busy}} is intended for brief back-pressure scenarios such as waiting for a downstream dependency to recover, performing a configuration reload, or coordinating with a load balancer during a rolling deploy. For permanent shutdown, use {{Closeable/close()}}.

### close() ### {#closeable-close}

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="Closeable">close()</dfn> method initiates graceful shutdown.

On a {{Listener}}, it stops accepting new connections, sends GOAWAY frames on HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 connections, allows in-flight requests to complete, and resolves when all connections are closed.

New [=exchanges=] arriving on existing connections during the drain &mdash; a request on an HTTP/1.1 keep-alive connection, or a stream racing the GOAWAY &mdash; are refused exactly as {{Closeable/busy}} refuses them, with the HTTP/1.1 fallback response additionally carrying `Connection: close`.

Established connections that are part of in-flight exchanges are asked to wind down, in the way their protocols provide:

*   A WebSocket connection, whether established by {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}} or {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocketStream()}}, has its closing handshake started with close code 1001 (Going Away), as if by {{ServerWebSocketStream/close()}}.
*   A WebTransport session is sent the drain signal of its protocol, so that the peer's `draining` promise resolves, and its {{WebTransportSession/draining}} promise resolves.
*   A [=tunnel=] established by {{ConnectContext/accept()}} is not signaled, since the protocols it carries have no common wind-down signal. The application decides when to close it.

The listener then waits for these exchanges, like any other, to complete. There is no deadline: an application that wants to bound the drain calls {{Closeable/destroy()}} after a timer of its own.

{{Closeable/close()}} also waits for the {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} promises of the exchanges on its connections to settle, subject to the {{TimeoutOptions/waitUntil}} limit ([[#serve-timeouts]]). Its returned promise settles as {{Closeable/closed}} does.

On a {{Server}}, it calls {{Closeable/close()}} on every active listener and waits for all of them to close.

Closing a listener does not close the server. Other listeners remain active, and {{Server/listen()}} can still add new ones. After a {{Server}} has been closed or destroyed, {{Server/listen()}} rejects with an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}. Calling {{Closeable/close()}} again, on a {{Server}} or {{Listener}} that is closing or closed, returns a promise that settles as {{Closeable/closed}} does, and has no other effect; calling it after {{Closeable/destroy()}} does the same.
</div>

### destroy() ### {#closeable-destroy}

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="Closeable">destroy(error)</dfn> method immediately terminates without draining. The {{Closeable/closed}} promise rejects with |error|, or with an `"AbortError"` {{DOMException}} when no |error| is provided: abrupt termination aborts in-flight work, and {{Closeable/closed}} must remain distinguishable from the graceful completion signaled by {{Closeable/close()}}.
</div>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Behavior</th><th>`close()`</th><th>`destroy()`</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>New connections</td><td>Refused</td><td>Refused</td></tr>
    <tr><td>GOAWAY sent</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>In-flight requests</td><td>Allowed to complete</td><td>Aborted immediately</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`waitUntil()` promises</td><td>Allowed to settle</td><td>Ignored</td></tr>
    <tr><td>{{Closeable/closed}} promise</td><td>Resolves</td><td>Rejects (destroy reason, or `"AbortError"`)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Returns</td><td>Promise (async)</td><td>undefined (sync)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

### closed ### {#closeable-closed}

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="Closeable">closed</dfn> attribute returns a promise that resolves when the server or listener is fully closed after a graceful {{Closeable/close()}}, and rejects &mdash; with the destroy reason, or an `"AbortError"` {{DOMException}} if none was given &mdash; when terminated via {{Closeable/destroy()}}. It returns the same promise every time, and the promise is always [=mark as handled|marked as handled=], so an application that never observes it does not receive an unhandled-rejection report.
</div>

### Symbol.asyncDispose ### {#closeable-dispose}

Both {{Server}} and {{Listener}} implement `Symbol.asyncDispose`, which calls {{Closeable/close()}} and waits for the {{Closeable/closed}} promise.

```js
{
  await using server = serve(handler, { port: 8080 });
  // Server is running
}
// Server has been gracefully closed
```

Signal-based termination {#server-signal}
-----------------------------------------

If an {{AbortSignal}} is provided in {{ServerOptions}}, aborting the signal triggers abrupt termination (equivalent to calling {{Closeable/destroy()}} with the signal's reason).

```js
const ac = new AbortController();
const server = serve(handler, { port: 8080, signal: ac.signal });

// Later:
ac.abort(new Error('shutting down'));  // Triggers destroy()
await server.closed.catch(() => {});
```

For graceful close, call {{Closeable/close()}} directly rather than using the signal.


HTTP version negotiation {#http-versions}
=========================================

Transparent protocol handling {#http-versions-transparent}
----------------------------------------------------------

A conforming implementation routes requests to the appropriate handler regardless of HTTP version. The `fetch()` handler receives all non-CONNECT requests (or, if there is none, they are answered with 501 Not Implemented; see [[#handler-object]]). The `connect()` handler receives all CONNECT and extended CONNECT requests. The handler does not select which HTTP version to handle.

Input that cannot be parsed into an [=exchange=] &mdash; invalid framing, an unparseable request line, a protocol violation &mdash; is handled entirely by the implementation (e.g., a 400 response, a connection close, or the protocol-appropriate error). No exchange is created, no handler is invoked, and no context or {{ServerRequest}} is materialized.

A request whose target is in asterisk-form (`OPTIONS *`, [RFC 9112 Section 3.2.4](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9112#section-3.2.4)) applies to the server as a whole rather than to a resource, and its target cannot be represented as a URL. It is likewise answered by the implementation, for example with a 204 (No Content) response carrying an `Allow` header field, and is not dispatched to a handler.

On HTTP/1.1, the request and response of an exchange can be in flight at the same time: a handler may begin sending a response, including a streaming body, while it is still reading the request body, and both directions proceed. Many HTTP/1.1 clients, however, do not read the response until they have sent the whole request. With such a client, a handler that waits for the response to be read before reading more of the request can stall, as both directions wait on flow control. This is not an error; the {{TimeoutOptions/receive}} and {{TimeoutOptions/send}} timeouts apply ([[#serve-timeouts]]). The "Full-duplex streaming" entry in the table below refers to what clients can be relied on to support, not to what the API permits.

Feature availability by protocol version {#http-versions-features}
-------------------------------------------------------------------

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>HTTP/1.1</th><th>HTTP/2</th><th>HTTP/3</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Trailers (request)</td><td>Chunked TE only</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Trailers (response)</td><td>Chunked TE only</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Informational responses</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Extended CONNECT</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>WebSocket</td><td>Via Upgrade</td><td>Via ext. CONNECT</td><td>Via ext. CONNECT</td></tr>
    <tr><td>WebTransport</td><td>No</td><td>Capsule fallback</td><td>Native QUIC</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HTTP Datagrams (unreliable)</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (QUIC DG)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HTTP Datagrams (reliable)</td><td>Yes (capsule)</td><td>Yes (capsule)</td><td>Yes (capsule)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CONNECT-UDP</td><td>Via Upgrade (capsule)</td><td>Capsule fallback</td><td>Native QUIC DG</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CONNECT-IP</td><td>Via Upgrade (capsule)</td><td>Capsule fallback</td><td>Native QUIC DG</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Full-duplex streaming</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>


Security considerations {#security}
====================================

*   **No CORS Enforcement**: This specification is for server-side runtimes. CORS is a browser security mechanism. Server-side implementations do not enforce CORS restrictions.

*   **Header Filtering**: {{ServerHeaders}} applies none of the Fetch Standard's browser guards, so a handler can read `Cookie` and set `Set-Cookie`. Header names and values are still validated, which prevents header injection and response splitting ([[#serverheaders-model]]). Connection-specific and pseudo-header fields are not exposed to handlers ([[#serverrequest-attributes]]). The implementation controls the connection-specific fields and framing of what it sends ([[#serverheaders-outgoing]]). Fields with a [=prohibited trailer name=] are removed from request trailers and are not sent in response trailers ([[#serverheaders-trailers]]).

*   **Tunnel Security**: The `connect()` handler establishes tunnels that can carry arbitrary traffic. Applications must perform their own authorization before calling {{ConnectContext/accept()}}, {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}}, {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocketStream()}}, or {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebTransport()}}.

*   **Tunnel Destinations**: A `connect()` handler that opens connections on the client's behalf (plain CONNECT, `connect-udp`, `connect-ip`) can be abused as an open proxy or for server-side request forgery. Applications should restrict permitted destinations, for example by excluding loopback, link-local, and private address ranges and cloud metadata endpoints, and should check destinations again after DNS resolution.

*   **Host Header Trust**: {{ServerRequest/url}} is built from the client-supplied request target and `Host` (or `:authority`) ([[#serverrequest-attributes]]). Applications that use it to build absolute URLs, such as redirect targets, links in emails, or cache keys, should check the host against the hosts they serve. {{ServerContext/serverName}} and the `Host` header field can legitimately differ, but a mismatch can also be an attempt at cross-tenant routing or domain fronting. Multi-tenant deployments should decide explicitly which of the two they trust.

*   **Request Smuggling**: Disagreement between parties about message framing allows request smuggling. The implementation, not the handler, owns framing: conflicting framing fields are rejected or removed before the handler sees the request ([[#serverrequest-attributes]]), and the framing of what is sent is determined by the implementation ([[#serverheaders-outgoing]]).

*   **Retry Safety**: The default code of {{ServerContext/deny()}}, also used when {{Closeable/busy}} refuses exchanges, tells clients that the request may be retried safely, even for non-idempotent methods. A handler that denies after performing side effects causes those side effects to be repeated ([[#exchange-states]]). Refusals sent to many clients at once can also cause retry storms. Deployments that use {{Closeable/busy}} should expect them, for example by using `Retry-After` on HTTP/1.1.

*   **Denial of Service**: WebTransport sessions can open many streams; implementations should impose limits. {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} extends request lifetime; implementations should impose timeouts. The Capsule Protocol and datagrams can generate high throughput; implementations should apply flow control. Several mechanisms buffer data without a bound defined by this specification: splitting a response body with {{ServerResponse/clone()}} ([[#serverresponse-clone]]), assembling a received WebSocket message ([[#websocketstream-messages]]), and queuing capsules and datagrams. Implementations should bound these buffers. Implementations should also defend against known HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 resource exhaustion attacks, such as rapid stream resets, unbounded `CONTINUATION` sequences, and field compression bombs. Deferring construction of the {{ServerRequest}} ([[#why-context]]) and refusing exchanges before dispatch ([[#serve-limits]], [[#closeable-busy]]) reduce what such an attack costs the server.

*   **Information Leakage**: The fallback responses produced when a handler faults (500 and 502) should not include the thrown value, stack traces, or other internal detail. The `detail` property of [=protocol errors=] carries wire-level information for logging, and is not meant to be sent to peers.

*   **Compression Side Channels**: WebSocket compression (`permessage-deflate`) combined with secrets and attacker-influenced data in the same message is subject to compression side-channel attacks in the style of CRIME and BREACH.

*   **TLS Material and SNI**: Private keys passed in {{TLSOptions}} reside in the memory of the JavaScript environment. An {{TLSOptions/sni}} callback is invoked with a client-chosen host name before any authentication. Callbacks that perform expensive or remote lookups should cache their results, including negative ones, and bound their latency.

*   **Privacy**: {{ServerContext/remoteAddress}} and {{ServerContext/serverName}} identify clients and the sites they visit. Applications that log them are subject to the privacy obligations that apply to such data.


Open issues {#open-issues}
==========================

Structured fields on ServerHeaders {#open-structured-fields}
------------------------------------------------------------

[RFC 9651](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9651) defines typed values (integers, booleans, tokens, byte sequences, etc.) for HTTP fields. {{ServerHeaders}}, like the Fetch Standard's {{Headers}}, exposes only raw strings. A structured field accessor (e.g., a `getStructured()` method) would benefit many use cases beyond priority.

Because {{ServerHeaders}} is defined by this specification, such an accessor could be added here without a change to the Fetch Standard.

Typed stream resets and error codes {#open-typed-resets}
-------------------------------------------------------

This specification defines the server-side half of typed error signaling: the [=error code=] registry, outbound signaling via {{ServerContext/deny()}} and stream aborts, and inbound propagation as [=protocol errors=] ([[#errors]]).

The client-side half remains open. When a `fetch()` call observes a stream reset, the Fetch Standard currently collapses it into a bare {{TypeError}}. Surfacing the same [=error codes=] there &mdash; on the rejection, and on errors of the response body stream &mdash; requires changes to the Fetch Standard's network error concept, and benefits from the [TC39 Error Code proposal](https://github.com/tc39/proposal-error-code-property) for a standardized `code` constructor option. The registry in [[#errors-codes]] is designed to be shared by both sides.

Constructing request contexts {#open-context-construction}
----------------------------------------------------------

There is no way for application code to construct a {{ServerRequest}} or a {{ServerContext}}. Two use cases need one:

*   **Delegation with rewriting.** Middleware that delegates to an inner handler passes along its own context ([[#serverresponse-body]]), so it cannot change what the inner handler sees: the request's URL (for example, to strip a mount prefix) or its header fields (for example, to add an authenticated identity). The header fields of {{ServerContext/request}} are immutable by design.
*   **Testing.** A handler cannot be invoked without a server, so unit tests depend on implementation-specific helpers.

A possible design is a method that derives a context from an existing one, sharing its [=exchange=] state (so that {{ServerContext/deny()}}, {{ServerContext/sendInformational()}}, and the upgrade methods act on the real exchange) but presenting a different request, together with a way to construct a request from a URL, method, header fields, and body. Construction for testing would also need a context that is not backed by a network exchange, whose observable effects (responses, denials, informational responses) a test can inspect.

WebTransport server profile {#open-webtransport-profile}
-------------------------------------------------------

{{WebTransportSession}} omits some members of the [[WEBTRANSPORT]] `WebTransport` interface that are not specific to clients, among them `protocol` (application protocol negotiation) and `congestionControl`, and {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebTransport()}} takes no options, so the server cannot select an application protocol or add header fields to the success response. The mapping of WebTransport stream reset and `STOP_SENDING` error codes, and of session termination (CLOSE_WEBTRANSPORT_SESSION), onto the server-side API is also not yet described, and the WebTransport over HTTP/3 and HTTP/2 protocol drafts are not yet referenced.

Connection and TLS metadata {#open-connection-metadata}
-------------------------------------------------------

{{ServerContext}} exposes the peer and local addresses, the ALPN protocol, and the SNI server name, but no identifier for the connection or stream (useful to correlate log entries for requests on the same connection), no details of the TLS session (protocol version, cipher suite), and no client certificate. {{TLSOptions}} has no way to request or verify client certificates (mutual TLS).

Additional limits {#open-additional-limits}
-------------------------------------------

Several resources mentioned in this specification have no configuration member: the size of a received WebSocket message ([[#websocketstream-messages]]), the size of a capsule, the number of WebTransport sessions on a connection and of streams in a session, and the number of header fields in a request.
